window.BAHR_DATA=window.BAHR_DATA||{};window.BAHR_DATA["passages-30"]=[{"id":"q4-24","q":"4:24","s":4,"surah":"Al-Nisāʾ","title":"4:24 — Marriage impediments, the inward severance of attachments, and the bridal gift","source":"Cairo Arabic edition I:485–489","cairo":"I:485–489","source_page_first":485,"source_page_last":489,"ar":"وَالْمُحْصَناتُ مِنَ النِّساءِ إِلاَّ مَا مَلَكَتْ أَيْمانُكُمْ كِتابَ اللَّهِ عَلَيْكُمْ ...\nقلت: «كتاب الله عليكم» : مصدر مؤكد. أي: كتب الله ذلك كتاباً، أو على الإغراء.\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله: حُرِّمَتْ عَلَيْكُمْ من النساء أصنافٌ: منها بالنسب ومنها بالرضاع ومنها بالمصاهرة: فأما التي تحرم بالنسب فهي أُمَّهاتُكُمْ، وهي الأم، والجدة من الأم ومن الأب ما عَلَوْن، وَبَناتُكُمْ وهي البنت وبنت الابن، وبنت البنت ما سفلن، وَأَخَواتُكُمْ وهي الأخت الشقيقة والتي للأب والأخت للأم، (وعماتكم) وهي أخت الوالد وأخت الجد ما علت، شقيقة أو لأب أو لأم، وَخالاتُكُمْ وهي أخت الأم وأخت الجدة ما علت، شقيقة أو لأب أو لأم، وَبَناتُ الْأَخِ الشقيق، أو للأب، وما تناسل منهم. وَبَناتُ الْأُخْتِ، فيدخل كل ما تناسل من الأخت الشقيقة أو للأب أو للأم.\nوالضابط في ذلك: أنه يحرم على الرجل أصوله وإن علت، وفصوله وإن سفلت، وفصول أبويه ما سفلت، وأول فصل من كل أصل متقدم على أبويه.\nثم ذكر ما يحرم بالرضاع، فقال: وَأُمَّهاتُكُمُ اللَّاتِي أَرْضَعْنَكُمْ وَأَخَواتُكُمْ مِنَ الرَّضاعَةِ ذكر تعالى صنفين، وحرمت السُّنَّةُ كل ما يحرم من النسب. قال صلّى الله عليه وسلم: «يَحْرُمُ مِنَ الرَّضَاعِ مَا يَحْرُمُ مِنَ النَّسَبِ» فيدخل الأصناف السبعة، وهي الأم من الرضاع والبنت والأخت والعمة والخالة وبنت الأخ وبنت الأخت.\nثم ذكر ما يحرم بالمصاهرة، فقال: وَأُمَّهاتُ نِسائِكُمْ، وتقدمت زوجة الأب، وسيأتي حليلة الابن، وَرَبائِبُكُمُ اللَّاتِي فِي حُجُورِكُمْ مِنْ نِسائِكُمُ لا مفهوم لهذا القيد، لكنه جرى مجرى الغالب، فهي محرمة، كانت في حجره أم لا، على قول الجمهور، ورُوي عن علي رضي الله عنه أنه أجاز نكاحها إن لم تكن في حجره. وأما قوله:\nاللَّاتِي دَخَلْتُمْ بِهِنَّ فهو معتبر إجماعًا، فلو عقد على المرأة ولم يدخل بها، فله طلاقها ويأخذ ابنتها، ولذلك قال: فَإِنْ لَمْ تَكُونُوا دَخَلْتُمْ بِهِنَّ فَلا جُناحَ عَلَيْكُمْ أن تَنكحوهن.\nوَحَلائِلُ أَبْنائِكُمُ وهي التي عقد عليها الابن فحلت له، فتحرم على الأب بمجرد العقد. والحاصل: أن زوجة الأب وزوجة الابن وأم الزوجة يحرمن بالعقد، وأما بنت المرأة فلا تحرم إلا بالدخول بأمها، فالعقد على البنات يُحرم الأمهات، والدخول بالأمهات يُحرم البنات. وقوله تعالى: الَّذِينَ مِنْ أَصْلابِكُمْ احترز به من زوجة المتبنِّي فلا تحرم حليلته، كقضية زيد مع رسول الله صلى الله عليه وسلم.\nوَأَنْ تَجْمَعُوا بَيْنَ الْأُخْتَيْنِ، شقيقتين أو للأب أو للأم، وهذا في النكاح، وأما في الملك دون الوطء فلا بأس، أما في الوطء فمنعه مالك والشافعي وأبو حنيفة، وأجازه الظاهرية، إِلَّا مَا قَدْ سَلَفَ أي: في الجاهلية، فقد عفا عنكم، إِنَّ اللَّهَ كانَ غَفُوراً رَحِيماً، قال ابن عباس: (كانت العرب تُحرم كل ما حرمت الشريعة إلا امرأة الأب والجمع بين الأختين، فلذلك ذكر الحق تعالى: إِلَّا ما قَدْ سَلَفَ فيهما.\nوَحرم الله تعالى- الْمُحْصَناتُ مِنَ النِّساءِ وهُنَّ اللاتي في عصمة أزواجهن، فلا يحل نكاحهن ما دُمْنَ في عصمة الزوج، إِلَّا مَّا مَلَكَتْ أَيْمانُكُمْ مِّن الغنيمة، فإذا سُبيت الكافرة، ولها زوج، جاز لمن ملكها أن يطأها بالملك بعد الاستبراء، قال في المختصر: وهدم السبيْ النكاحَ، إلا أن تُسبى وتُسلْم في عدتها فهو أحق بها، وقد بعث رسول الله صلى الله عليه وسلم جيشًا إلى أَوْطَاس، فأصابوا سبيًا من العدو، ولهن أزواج من المشركين فتأثموا من غشيانهن، فنزلت الآية مُبيحة لذلك، كِتابَ اللَّهِ عَلَيْكُمْ أي: كتب الله ذلك عليكم كتاباً، وهو ما حرّم في الآية من النساء.\nالإشارة: اعلم أن الإنسان لا يصير كاملاً عارفًا حتى يولد ثلاث مرات بعد الأم الحسية، أولها: خروجه من بطن حب الدنيا الدنية، ثم من الغفلة والشهوات الجسمانية، ثم من ضيق الأكوان الظلمانية، إلى فضاء المشاهدة والمعاينة، وقال بعض الأولياء: (ليس منا من لم يولد مرتين) : فاعتبر الأولى والثالثة، فإذا خرج الإنسان من هذه البطون حرَّم الله عليه نكاحها والرجوع إليها.\nوكذا يحرم عليه الرجوع إلى ما تولد منه من الزلات، والأحوال الظلمانية، وما كان ألفه وتواخى معه من البطالات والمألوفات، وما وجد عليه أسلافه من التعصبات والحميات والرئاسات، ولا فرق بين ما واجهه من ذلك من قبل الآباء والأمهات، وكذلك ما ارتضع من ثدي الشهوات من لِبان الغفلة، وتراكم الأَكِنَّات (١) ، فليبادر إلى تحريمها، وفطام نفسه عنها، قبل تحكمها، كما قال البوصيري رضي الله عنه:\nوالنَّفْسُ كالطِّفلِ إنْ تُهْمِلْهُ شَبَّ ... عَلَى حُبِّ الرَّضاعِ وإنْ تَفْطِمْهُ يَنْفَطِم\nوكذا يحرم عليه، صحبة من ارتضع معه في هذا الثدي قبل الفطام من الأخوة والأخوات، وكذا أمهات الخطايا، وهي حب الدنيا والرياسة والجاه، وكذلك حرمت عليكم ربائب العلائق والعوائق، لتدخلوا بلاد الحقائق، فإن لم تكونوا من أهل الحقائق فلا جناح عليكم إذ كنتم من عوام الخلائق، وكذلك يحرم عليكم ما حل لأبناء جنسكم من تعاطي الأسباب والاشتغال بها عن خدمة رب الأرباب، وأن تجمعوا بين حب الدنيا ومحبة المولى. قال الشافعي رضي الله عنه: (من ادَّعى أنه جمَعَ بين حب الدنيا وحب خالقها، فقد كذب) .\nإِلاَّ مَا قَدْ سَلَفَ في أيام البطالة، وكذا يحرم على المريد المتجرد المستشرف على المعاني تعاطى العلوم الظاهرة، التي دخل بها أهل الظاهر وافتضوا بكارتها- إلا ما ملكه قبل التجريد، فلا يضره إن غاب عنها في أسرار التوحيد، والله تعالى أعلم بأسرار غيبه.\nثم ذكر الحق تعالى ما يحلّ من النساء، فقال:\n... وَأَحَلَّ لَكُمْ مَّا وَراءَ ذلِكُمْ أَنْ تَبْتَغُوا بِأَمْوالِكُمْ مُحْصِنِينَ غَيْرَ مُسافِحِينَ فَمَا اسْتَمْتَعْتُمْ بِهِ مِنْهُنَّ فَآتُوهُنَّ أُجُورَهُنَّ فَرِيضَةً وَلا جُناحَ عَلَيْكُمْ فِيما تَراضَيْتُمْ بِهِ مِنْ بَعْدِ الْفَرِيضَةِ إِنَّ اللَّهَ كانَ عَلِيماً حَكِيماً (24)\nقلت: «وأَحَلَّ» عطف على الفعل العامل في «كتاب الله عليكم» أي: كتب الله عليكم تحريم ما ذكر، وأحل ما سوى ذلك. ومن قرأ بالبناء للمفعول فعطف على «حُرمت» . و (أن تبتغوا) مفعول لأجله، أي: إرادة أن تبتغوا. أو بدل من (وراء ذلكم) . و (محصنين) حال من الواو. والسفاح: الزنا، من السفح وهو الصب، لأنه يصب المنيّ في غير مَحِلَّهُ.\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله: وَأَحَلَّ لَكُمْ أن تتزوجوا من النساء ما سوى ذلكم المحرمات، وما سوى ما حرمته السنة بالرضاع، كما تقدم، والجمع بين المرأة وعمتها، وبين المرأة وخالتها، فقد حرَمتْه السُنة، وإنما أحل لكم نكاح النساء إرادة أن تطلبوا بأموالكم الحلال، فتصرفوها في مهور النساء.. حال كونكم مُحْصِنِينَ. أي: أعفة متحصنين بها من الحرام، غَيْرَ مُسافِحِينَ أي: غير زناة، تصبون الماء في غير موضعه، فَمَا اسْتَمْتَعْتُمْ بِهِ مِنْهُنَّ أي: من تمتعتم به من المنكوحات فَآتُوهُنَّ أُجُورَهُنَّ أي: مهورهن، لأن المهر في مقابلة الاستمتاع فَرِيضَةً، أي: مفروضة مقدرة، لا جَهْلَ فيها ولا إبهام، وَلا جُناحَ عَلَيْكُمْ فِيما تَراضَيْتُمْ بِهِ من زيادة على المهر المشروط، أو نقص منه، مِنْ بَعْدِ الْفَرِيضَةِ، التي وقع العقد عليها، إِنَّ اللَّهَ كانَ عَلِيماً بمصالح خلقه، حَكِيماً فيما شرع من الأحكام.\nوقيل قوله: فَمَا اسْتَمْتَعْتُمْ بِهِ ... إلى آخره. نزل في نكاح المتعة، التي كانت ثلاثة أيام في فتح مكة، ثم نُسِخَ بما روى عنه- عليه الصلاة والسلام- أنه أباحه، ثم أصبح يقول: «أيُّهَا النَّاسِ، إِنِّي كُنْتُ أمرتكم بالاسِتْمْتَاعِ مِنَ هذه النَّساء، ألا إنَّ اللهَ حَرَمَ ذَلِكَ إلىَ يَوْمِ القِيَامةِ» . وهو النكاح المؤقت بوقت معلوم، سُمي به لأن الغرض منه مجرد الاستمتاع. وتمتعها بما يُعطى لها. وجوَّزه ابن عباس رضي الله عنه ثم رجع عنه. والله تعالى أعلم.\nالإشارة: يقول الحق جلّ جلاله من طريق الإشارة: إذا خرجتم من بطن الشهوات، ورفضتم ما كنتم عليه من العوائد والمألوفات، وزهدتم فيما يشغل فكرتكم من العلوم الرسميات، حل لكم ما وراء ذلكم من العلوم اللدنية\nوالأسرار الربانية، التي هي وراء طور العقول ولا تدرك بالطروس (١) ولا بالنقول، وإليها أشار ابن الفارض رضي الله عنه حيث قال:\nولا تكُ مِمَنْ طَيّشَتْهُ طروسه ... بحيثُ استخفت عقَلَهُ واستفزّتِ\nفَثمَ وراء النّقلِ عِلْمٌ يَدِقُّ عن ... مَدارِكِ غايات العقولِ السليمةِ\nتَلَقيْتُه منّي وعني أَخَذْتهُ ... ونفسي كانت من عَطاءٍ مُمدَّةِ\nأردنا منكم أن تبتغوا ببذل أموالكم ومُهجكم تلك العلوم المقدسة، والأسرار المطهرة، متحصنين من دنس الحس والهوى، غير مباشرين لنجاسة الدنيا، ولا مصطحبين مع أهلها، لتتمتعوا بشهود أسرارنا، وأنوار قدسنا، فما استمتعتم به من ذلك، فصونوه عن غير أهله، ولا جناح عليكم فيما تراضيتم به من إعطائه لأهله، من بعد حفظه عمن لا يستحقه، والله تعالى أعلم.\nثم ذكر حكم من عجز عن صداق الحرة، فقال:","quran_ar":"وَالْمُحْصَناتُ مِنَ النِّساءِ إِلاَّ مَا مَلَكَتْ أَيْمانُكُمْ كِتابَ اللَّهِ عَلَيْكُمْ وَأَحَلَّ لَكُمْ مَّا وَراءَ ذلِكُمْ أَنْ تَبْتَغُوا بِأَمْوالِكُمْ مُحْصِنِينَ غَيْرَ مُسافِحِينَ فَمَا اسْتَمْتَعْتُمْ بِهِ مِنْهُنَّ فَآتُوهُنَّ أُجُورَهُنَّ فَرِيضَةً وَلا جُناحَ عَلَيْكُمْ فِيما تَراضَيْتُمْ بِهِ مِنْ بَعْدِ الْفَرِيضَةِ إِنَّ اللَّهَ كانَ عَلِيماً حَكِيماً\n﴿24﴾","quran_en":"Also forbidden to you are women already married, except those whom your right hands possess. Such is God’s ordinance upon you. And He has made lawful to you those beyond these, provided that you seek them with your wealth in chastity, not in fornication. So for the women with whom you have enjoyed marital intimacy, give them their bridal gifts as an obligation. There is no blame upon you concerning whatever you mutually agree upon after the obligation has been fixed. God is ever Knowing, Wise.","segments":[{"type":"exposition","title":"The prohibition concerning married women","html":"<p>God also prohibited <em>al-muḥṣanāt<\/em> (المحصنات) among women: that is, women bound by marriage to their husbands. It is therefore not lawful to marry them while the marriage bond remains in force, <strong>“except those whom your right hands possess”<\/strong> from the spoils of war. Thus, when a non-Muslim woman is captured while she has a husband, the person who becomes her owner, according to the rule Ibn ʿAjība is describing, may have intercourse with her by reason of ownership after <em>istibrāʾ<\/em> (الاستبراء), the ascertainment that her womb is free [from a previous pregnancy]. In the <em>Mukhtaṣar<\/em> it is said: “Captivity annuls marriage—except that, if she is captured and embraces Islam during her waiting period (<em>ʿidda<\/em>, العدة), he [her previous husband] has the prior claim to her.” <a class=\"noteref\" href=\"#/note/N127\">[N127]<\/a><\/p><p>The Messenger of God, blessings and peace upon him, sent an army to Awṭās. They took captive women from the enemy who had husbands among the polytheists. The Muslims felt religious scruple (<em>taʾaththum<\/em>, التأثم) about intercourse with them, and this verse was revealed permitting it [under the legal conditions understood by the commentator]. <a class=\"noteref\" href=\"#/hadith/H021\">[H021]<\/a> <strong>“God’s ordinance upon you”<\/strong> means that God prescribed this for you as an ordinance: the prohibition of the women named in the verse.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"God also prohibited al-muḥṣanāt (المحصنات) among women: that is, women bound by marriage to their husbands. It is therefore not lawful to marry them while the marriage bond remains in force, “except those whom your right hands possess” from the spoils of war. Thus, when a non-Muslim woman is captured while she has a husband, the person who becomes her owner, according to the rule Ibn ʿAjība is describing, may have intercourse with her by reason of ownership after istibrāʾ (الاستبراء), the ascertainment that her womb is free [from a previous pregnancy]. In the Mukhtaṣar it is said: “Captivity annuls marriage—except that, if she is captured and embraces Islam during her waiting period ( ʿidda , العدة), he [her previous husband] has the prior claim to her.” [N127] The Messenger of God, blessings and peace upon him, sent an army to Awṭās. They took captive women from the enemy who had husbands among the polytheists. The Muslims felt religious scruple ( taʾaththum , التأثم) about intercourse with them, and this verse was revealed permitting it [under the legal conditions understood by the commentator]. [H021] “God’s ordinance upon you” means that God prescribed this for you as an ordinance: the prohibition of the women named in the verse."},{"type":"spiritual","title":"the three births and the renunciation of former attachments","html":"<p>Know that a human being does not become a perfected knower (<em>ʿārif kāmil<\/em>, عارف كامل) until he is born three times after his birth from his bodily mother. The first is his emergence from the womb of love for this lowly world; then [he emerges] from heedlessness and bodily appetites; then from the narrowness of the darkened created orders (<em>al-akwān al-ẓulmāniyya<\/em>, الأكوان الظلمانية), into the open expanse of witnessing and direct vision (<em>al-mushāhada wa-l-muʿāyana<\/em>, المشاهدة والمعاينة). One of the saints said: “One who has not been born twice is not of us.” Here he counted the first and the third [of the births just described]. When a person has emerged from these wombs, God has made it forbidden for him to “marry” them or return to them. <a class=\"noteref\" href=\"#/note/N128\">[N128]<\/a><\/p><p>In the same way, returning to the lapses and dark states born from him is forbidden for him; likewise the vain pursuits and customary attachments with which he had formerly made himself intimate and brotherly, and the partisanships, heated loyalties and claims to eminence in which he found his forebears established. There is no difference here between what confronts him through the fathers and what confronts him through the mothers. So too with what he has suckled from the breast of appetites—the milk of heedlessness—and with the veils (<em>akinna<\/em>, أكنة) that have accumulated over him: let him hasten to prohibit such things to himself and wean his soul from them before they gain mastery over it. As al-Būṣīrī, may God be pleased with him, said:<\/p><blockquote>\n<p>The soul is like a child: neglect it, and it will grow up loving to suckle; <strong>wean it, and it will be weaned.<\/strong><\/p>\n<\/blockquote><p>Likewise forbidden to him is the companionship of the brothers and sisters who suckled alongside him from that breast before weaning. The “mothers of sins,” too, are love of this world, command, and standing among people (<em>al-jāh<\/em>, الجاه). Likewise the “stepdaughters” of entanglements and obstacles (<em>al-ʿalāʾiq wa-l-ʿawāʾiq<\/em>, العلائق والعوائق) have been forbidden to you, so that you may enter the lands of realities (<em>al-ḥaqāʾiq<\/em>, الحقائق). If you are not among the people of realities, however, there is no blame upon you, for you belong to the ordinary company of created beings. Equally forbidden to [the advanced seeker] are occupations with worldly means (<em>al-asbāb<\/em>, الأسباب)—though permissible for people of his own kind—when they distract him from serving the Lord of lords, and also the combining of love of the world with love of the Master. Al-Shāfiʿī, may God be pleased with him, is reported to have said: “Whoever claims to have joined love of the world with love of its Creator has lied.” <a class=\"noteref\" href=\"#/note/N129\">[N129]<\/a><\/p><p><strong>“Except what has gone before”<\/strong> during the days of idle distraction. Similarly, a seeker (<em>murīd<\/em>) who has become detached (<em>mutajarrid<\/em>, متجرد) and is looking toward the inward meanings must not busy himself with the exoteric sciences which the people of the outward have entered and whose virginity they have taken—except what he acquired before detachment: such knowledge does not harm him if he becomes absent from it in the secrets of divine unity (<em>asrār al-tawḥīd<\/em>, أسرار التوحيد). God knows best the secrets of His Unseen.<\/p>","authorial":true,"text":"Know that a human being does not become a perfected knower ( ʿārif kāmil , عارف كامل) until he is born three times after his birth from his bodily mother. The first is his emergence from the womb of love for this lowly world; then [he emerges] from heedlessness and bodily appetites; then from the narrowness of the darkened created orders ( al-akwān al-ẓulmāniyya , الأكوان الظلمانية), into the open expanse of witnessing and direct vision ( al-mushāhada wa-l-muʿāyana , المشاهدة والمعاينة). One of the saints said: “One who has not been born twice is not of us.” Here he counted the first and the third [of the births just described]. When a person has emerged from these wombs, God has made it forbidden for him to “marry” them or return to them. [N128] In the same way, returning to the lapses and dark states born from him is forbidden for him; likewise the vain pursuits and customary attachments with which he had formerly made himself intimate and brotherly, and the partisanships, heated loyalties and claims to eminence in which he found his forebears established. There is no difference here between what confronts him through the fathers and what confronts him through the mothers. So too with what he has suckled from the breast of appetites—the milk of heedlessness—and with the veils ( akinna , أكنة) that have accumulated over him: let him hasten to prohibit such things to himself and wean his soul from them before they gain mastery over it. As al-Būṣīrī, may God be pleased with him, said: The soul is like a child: neglect it, and it will grow up loving to suckle; wean it, and it will be weaned. Likewise forbidden to him is the companionship of the brothers and sisters who suckled alongside him from that breast before weaning. The “mothers of sins,” too, are love of this world, command, and standing among people ( al-jāh , الجاه). Likewise the “stepdaughters” of entanglements and obstacles ( al-ʿalāʾiq wa-l-ʿawāʾiq , العلائق والعوائق) have been forbidden to you, so that you may enter the lands of realities ( al-ḥaqāʾiq , الحقائق). If you are not among the people of realities, however, there is no blame upon you, for you belong to the ordinary company of created beings. Equally forbidden to [the advanced seeker] are occupations with worldly means ( al-asbāb , الأسباب)—though permissible for people of his own kind—when they distract him from serving the Lord of lords, and also the combining of love of the world with love of the Master. Al-Shāfiʿī, may God be pleased with him, is reported to have said: “Whoever claims to have joined love of the world with love of its Creator has lied.” [N129] “Except what has gone before” during the days of idle distraction. Similarly, a seeker ( murīd ) who has become detached ( mutajarrid , متجرد) and is looking toward the inward meanings must not busy himself with the exoteric sciences which the people of the outward have entered and whose virginity they have taken—except what he acquired before detachment: such knowledge does not harm him if he becomes absent from it in the secrets of divine unity ( asrār al-tawḥīd , أسرار التوحيد). God knows best the secrets of His Unseen."},{"type":"transition","title":"","html":"<p class=\"transition-lead\">He then speaks of the women who are lawful to marry<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"He then speaks of the women who are lawful to marry"},{"type":"language","title":"The grammar of “made lawful,” seeking with wealth, and chastity","html":"<p><strong>“And made lawful to you …”<\/strong> I say: Ibn ʿAjība first construes <strong>“and made lawful”<\/strong> in an active reading, <em>wa-aḥalla<\/em> (وأَحَلَّ), as coordinated (<em>ʿaṭf<\/em>, عطف) with the unexpressed verb governing <strong>“God’s ordinance upon you”<\/strong>: that is, God prescribed the prohibition of those mentioned and made lawful what lies beyond them. In the passive reading <em>wa-uḥilla<\/em> (وأُحِلَّ), the verb is instead coordinated with <strong>“prohibited [to you]”<\/strong> (<em>ḥurrimat<\/em>, حرمت). <a class=\"noteref\" href=\"#/note/N142\">[N142]<\/a> <strong>“That you should seek”<\/strong> (<em>an tabtaghū<\/em>, أن تبتغوا) is an object of purpose (<em>mafʿūl li-ajlih<\/em>, مفعول لأجله)—“with the intention that you seek”—or a substitute (<em>badal<\/em>, بدل) for <strong>“beyond these”<\/strong>. <strong>“In chastity”<\/strong> (<em>muḥṣinīn<\/em>, محصنين) is a circumstantial accusative (<em>ḥāl<\/em>, حال) relating to the plural pronoun. <em>Sifāḥ<\/em> (السفاح) is fornication, derived from <em>safḥ<\/em> (السفح), “pouring out,” because semen is poured where it does not belong.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"“And made lawful to you …” I say: Ibn ʿAjība first construes “and made lawful” in an active reading, wa-aḥalla (وأَحَلَّ), as coordinated ( ʿaṭf , عطف) with the unexpressed verb governing “God’s ordinance upon you” : that is, God prescribed the prohibition of those mentioned and made lawful what lies beyond them. In the passive reading wa-uḥilla (وأُحِلَّ), the verb is instead coordinated with “prohibited [to you]” ( ḥurrimat , حرمت). [N142] “That you should seek” ( an tabtaghū , أن تبتغوا) is an object of purpose ( mafʿūl li-ajlih , مفعول لأجله)—“with the intention that you seek”—or a substitute ( badal , بدل) for “beyond these” . “In chastity” ( muḥṣinīn , محصنين) is a circumstantial accusative ( ḥāl , حال) relating to the plural pronoun. Sifāḥ (السفاح) is fornication, derived from safḥ (السفح), “pouring out,” because semen is poured where it does not belong."},{"type":"exposition","title":"Lawful marriage, dowers, and the question of temporary marriage","html":"<p>The Real—majestic is His majesty—says: <strong>“And made lawful to you what lies beyond these”<\/strong>: marrying women other than those listed as prohibited, apart also from the prohibitions established by the Sunna through suckling, as mentioned earlier, and the prohibition of combining marriage to a woman and her paternal aunt, or to a woman and her maternal aunt. These the Sunna has prohibited. <a class=\"noteref\" href=\"#/hadith/H031\">[H031]<\/a> God permitted the marriage of women in order that you might seek what is lawful through your wealth, devoting it to women’s bridal gifts (<em>muhūr<\/em>, مهور), while being <strong>“chaste”<\/strong> (<em>muḥṣinīn<\/em>): preserving your modesty and securing yourselves by marriage against what is forbidden, <strong>“not fornicators”<\/strong> (<em>musāfiḥīn<\/em>, مسافحين), who cast their water where it does not belong.<\/p><p><strong>“So for those of them with whom you have enjoyed [marital intimacy], give them their wages as an obligation”<\/strong>: that is, give the women you have married their bridal gifts, for the bridal gift corresponds to marital enjoyment; it is an obligatory, stipulated amount, neither unknown nor ambiguous. <strong>“There is no blame upon you in what you mutually agree upon after the obligation”<\/strong>: in an increase above the contractually stipulated bridal gift or a reduction of it, after the amount upon which the marriage contract was made. <strong>“God has ever been Knowing”<\/strong> of His creatures’ welfare and <strong>“Wise”<\/strong> in the rulings He has prescribed.<\/p><p>Another view says that <strong>“So for those of them with whom you have enjoyed …”<\/strong> to the end was revealed concerning <em>nikāḥ al-mutʿa<\/em> (نكاح المتعة), the fixed-term marriage said to have been permitted for three days at the conquest of Mecca and then abrogated (<em>nusikha<\/em>, نُسخ). This is supported by what is transmitted of the Prophet, blessings and peace upon him: having permitted it, he then said the following morning, “People! I had allowed you to seek temporary enjoyment with these women, but God has forbidden this until the Day of Resurrection.” <a class=\"noteref\" href=\"#/hadith/H022\">[H022]<\/a> It is a marriage stipulated for a known time, called <em>mutʿa<\/em> because the object is enjoyment alone, together with the woman’s enjoyment of what she is given. Ibn ʿAbbās permitted it and then, according to this report, withdrew his permission. God knows best. <a class=\"noteref\" href=\"#/note/N130\">[N130]<\/a><\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real—majestic is His majesty—says: “And made lawful to you what lies beyond these” : marrying women other than those listed as prohibited, apart also from the prohibitions established by the Sunna through suckling, as mentioned earlier, and the prohibition of combining marriage to a woman and her paternal aunt, or to a woman and her maternal aunt. These the Sunna has prohibited. [H031] God permitted the marriage of women in order that you might seek what is lawful through your wealth, devoting it to women’s bridal gifts ( muhūr , مهور), while being “chaste” ( muḥṣinīn ): preserving your modesty and securing yourselves by marriage against what is forbidden, “not fornicators” ( musāfiḥīn , مسافحين), who cast their water where it does not belong. “So for those of them with whom you have enjoyed [marital intimacy], give them their wages as an obligation” : that is, give the women you have married their bridal gifts, for the bridal gift corresponds to marital enjoyment; it is an obligatory, stipulated amount, neither unknown nor ambiguous. “There is no blame upon you in what you mutually agree upon after the obligation” : in an increase above the contractually stipulated bridal gift or a reduction of it, after the amount upon which the marriage contract was made. “God has ever been Knowing” of His creatures’ welfare and “Wise” in the rulings He has prescribed. Another view says that “So for those of them with whom you have enjoyed …” to the end was revealed concerning nikāḥ al-mutʿa (نكاح المتعة), the fixed-term marriage said to have been permitted for three days at the conquest of Mecca and then abrogated ( nusikha , نُسخ). This is supported by what is transmitted of the Prophet, blessings and peace upon him: having permitted it, he then said the following morning, “People! I had allowed you to seek temporary enjoyment with these women, but God has forbidden this until the Day of Resurrection.” [H022] It is a marriage stipulated for a known time, called mutʿa because the object is enjoyment alone, together with the woman’s enjoyment of what she is given. Ibn ʿAbbās permitted it and then, according to this report, withdrew his permission. God knows best. [N130]"},{"type":"spiritual","title":"knowledge beyond discursive transmission","html":"<p>The Real—majestic is His majesty—says by way of allusion: When you have emerged from the womb of appetites, abandoned your previous habits and familiar attachments, and renounced the conventional, formally transmitted sciences (<em>al-ʿulūm al-rasmiyyāt<\/em>, العلوم الرسميات) that preoccupy your thought, then there becomes lawful for you what lies beyond them: the God-given sciences (<em>al-ʿulūm al-ladunniyya<\/em>, العلوم اللدنية) and lordly secrets (<em>al-asrār al-rabbāniyya<\/em>, الأسرار الربانية), which transcend the reach of discursive intellects and cannot be grasped from written pages (<em>ṭurūs<\/em>, طروس) or transmitted reports (<em>nuqūl<\/em>, نقول). Ibn al-Fāriḍ, may God be pleased with him, alluded to these when he said:<\/p><blockquote>\n<p>Be not among those whom their pages have made giddy, so that they have unsettled their intellect and swept it away.<\/p>\n<p>For beyond transmission there is knowledge too subtle for the furthest comprehension of sound intellects.<\/p>\n<p>I received it from myself and took it from myself, while my soul was sustained by a gift.<\/p>\n<\/blockquote><p>We wished you, through the expenditure of your wealth and your very lives (<em>muhaj<\/em>, مهج), to seek those holy sciences and purified secrets, safeguarding yourselves against the taint of sense perception (<em>al-ḥiss<\/em>, الحس) and caprice (<em>al-hawā<\/em>, الهوى), neither touching the impurity of the world nor keeping company with its people, so that you may delight in witnessing Our secrets and the lights of Our holiness. Whatever you have enjoyed of these, guard it from those unworthy of it; yet there is no blame upon you in mutually agreeing to give it to those worthy of it, once you have protected it from those who have no rightful claim. And God knows best.<\/p>","authorial":true,"text":"The Real—majestic is His majesty—says by way of allusion: When you have emerged from the womb of appetites, abandoned your previous habits and familiar attachments, and renounced the conventional, formally transmitted sciences ( al-ʿulūm al-rasmiyyāt , العلوم الرسميات) that preoccupy your thought, then there becomes lawful for you what lies beyond them: the God-given sciences ( al-ʿulūm al-ladunniyya , العلوم اللدنية) and lordly secrets ( al-asrār al-rabbāniyya , الأسرار الربانية), which transcend the reach of discursive intellects and cannot be grasped from written pages ( ṭurūs , طروس) or transmitted reports ( nuqūl , نقول). Ibn al-Fāriḍ, may God be pleased with him, alluded to these when he said: Be not among those whom their pages have made giddy, so that they have unsettled their intellect and swept it away. For beyond transmission there is knowledge too subtle for the furthest comprehension of sound intellects. I received it from myself and took it from myself, while my soul was sustained by a gift. We wished you, through the expenditure of your wealth and your very lives ( muhaj , مهج), to seek those holy sciences and purified secrets, safeguarding yourselves against the taint of sense perception ( al-ḥiss , الحس) and caprice ( al-hawā , الهوى), neither touching the impurity of the world nor keeping company with its people, so that you may delight in witnessing Our secrets and the lights of Our holiness. Whatever you have enjoyed of these, guard it from those unworthy of it; yet there is no blame upon you in mutually agreeing to give it to those worthy of it, once you have protected it from those who have no rightful claim. And God knows best."},{"type":"transition","title":"","html":"<p class=\"transition-lead\">He then gives the ruling concerning someone unable to afford the bridal gift of a free woman<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"He then gives the ruling concerning someone unable to afford the bridal gift of a free woman"}],"cairo_notes":[{"id":"C487.1","page":487,"ar":"الأكنات: الأغطية.","en":"Akinnāt: coverings.","anchor":"وتراكم الأَكِنَّات"},{"id":"C489.1","page":489,"ar":"الطروس: الصحف.","en":"Ṭurūs: written sheets or pages.","anchor":"ولا تدرك بالطروس"}],"english_characters":9304,"arabic_characters":7353},{"id":"q4-25-28","q":"4:25–28","s":4,"surah":"Al-Nisāʾ","title":"4:25–28 — Marriage under constraint, the degrees of knowledge, and divine accommodation","source":"Cairo Arabic edition I:489–493","cairo":"I:489–493","source_page_first":489,"source_page_last":493,"ar":"وَمَنْ لَمْ يَسْتَطِعْ مِنْكُمْ طَوْلاً أَنْ يَنْكِحَ الْمُحْصَناتِ الْمُؤْمِناتِ فَمِنْ مَّا مَلَكَتْ أَيْمانُكُمْ مِنْ فَتَياتِكُمُ الْمُؤْمِناتِ وَاللَّهُ أَعْلَمُ بِإِيمانِكُمْ بَعْضُكُمْ مِنْ بَعْضٍ فَانْكِحُوهُنَّ بِإِذْنِ أَهْلِهِنَّ وَآتُوهُنَّ أُجُورَهُنَّ بِالْمَعْرُوفِ مُحْصَناتٍ غَيْرَ مُسافِحاتٍ وَلا مُتَّخِذاتِ أَخْدانٍ ...\nقلت: الطول: الغنى والسعة، ويطلق على العلو، مصدر طال طَوْلاً، وهو مفعول «يستطع» أو مصدر له- لتقارب معناهما، و (أن ينكح) بدل منه على الأول، أو مفعول به على الثاني، أي: لأن ينكح، و (محصنات غير مسافحات) ، حالان، والعامل فيه: (أنكحوهن) ، والخدن: الخليل.\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله: وَمَنْ لَمْ يَسْتَطِعْ مِنْكُمْ طَوْلًا أي: لم يجد غنى يقدر به على نكاح الْمُحْصَناتُ، أي: الحرائر الْمُؤْمِناتِ، فليتزوج مِّن مَّا مَلَكَتْ أَيْمَانُكُمْ، من الإماء المؤمنات دون الكافرات، فإن أظهرت الإيمان فاكتفوا بذلك، وعلم الباطن لا يعلمه إلا الله، وَاللَّهُ أَعْلَمُ بِإِيمانِكُمْ فلا يمنعكم من نكاحهن خوف المعرة، فإنما أنتم جنس واحد، ودينكم واحد، بَعْضُكُمْ مِنْ بَعْضٍ فلا تستنكفوا من نكاحهن،\nفَانْكِحُوهُنَّ بِإِذْنِ أَهْلِهِنَّ، أي أربابهن، حتى يعقدوا لكم نكاحهن، وَآتُوهُنَّ أُجُورَهُنَّ: أي: مهورهن، وهن أحق به دون ساداتهن، على مذهب مالك، بِالْمَعْرُوفِ من غير مطل، ولا نقص، على ما تقتضيه السنة. حال كونهن مُحْصَناتٍ أي: عفيفات غَيْرَ مُسافِحاتٍ أي: غير زانيات وَلا مُتَّخِذاتِ أَخْدانٍ. أي: أصحاب يزنون بهن. وكان في الجاهلية مِن النساء مَنْ تتخذ صاحبًا واحدًا تزني معه خاصة، ومنها من لا ترد يد لامس.\nقال ابنُ جزي: مذهب مالك وأكثر أصحابه أنه لا يجوز للحر نكاح الأمة إلا بشرطين: أحدهما: عدم الطول وهو ألاَّ يجد ما يتزوج به حُرة، والآخر: خوف العنت وهو الزنا. لقوله بعد هذا: ذلِكَ لِمَنْ خَشِيَ الْعَنَتَ مِنْكُمْ، وأجاز ابن القاسم نكاحهن دون الشرطين، على القول بأن دليل الخطاب لا يُعتبر، واتفقوا على اشتراط الإسلام في الأمة، لقوله: مِنْ فَتَياتِكُمُ الْمُؤْمِناتِ إلا أهل العراق فلم يشترطوه. هـ.\nالإشارة: فمن لم يستطع أن ينكح أبكار الحقائق، لكونه لم يقدر أن يدفع عن قلبه الشواغل والعلائق، فليتنزل لنكاح العلوم الرسمية والأعمال الحسية، بأخذها من أربابها، ويحصنها بالإخلاص في أخذها، ويقوم بحقها بقدر الإمكان، وهو بذلها لأهلها، والصبر على نشرها، والله لا يضيع أجرَ من أحسن عملاً، فإن صح قصده، وخلص عمله، قيض الله له وليًا من أوليائه يغنيه بالله، حتى يصير من الأغنياء به، فيتأهل لنكاح الحرائر، ويلتحق بأولياء الله الأكابر، (وَمَا ذلك على الله بعزيز) .\nقال الشيخ أبو الحسن الشاذلى رضي الله عنه لما تكلم على ثمرات المحبة- قال: فترى النفس مائلة لطاعته، والعقل متحصنا بمعرفته (١) ، والروح مأخوذة في حضرته، والسر مغمورا في مشاهدته، والعبد يستزيد [من حبه ] (٢) فيُزاد ويفاتح بما هو أعذب من لذيذ مناجاته، فيكسي حلل التقريب على بساط القربة، ويمس أبكار الحقائق وثيبات العلوم. هـ. فعلم الحقائق أبكار، وما يوصل إليه من علوم الطريقة ثيبات حرائر، وما سواها من علوم الرسوم إماء بالنسبة إلى غيرها، والله تعالى أعلم.\nثم ذكر حدَّ الأمَةِ إذا زنت، فقال:\n\n... فَإِذا أُحْصِنَّ فَإِنْ أَتَيْنَ بِفاحِشَةٍ فَعَلَيْهِنَّ نِصْفُ مَا عَلَى الْمُحْصَناتِ مِنَ الْعَذابِ ...\nقلت: أحصن الرجل- بفتح الهمزة وضمها-: صار محصنًا بالفتح والكسر، وهذا مما اتحد فيه البناء للفاعل والمفعول. وقيل بالفتح، معناه: أسلم، وبالضم: تزوج.\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله: إن الإماء إذا تزوجن فَإِنْ أَتَيْنَ بِفاحِشَةٍ، وهو الزنا، فعليهن نصف ما على الحرة من الحد، وهو خمسون، لأن حد البكر مائة. ويفهم منه أنها لا ترجم لأن الرجم لا يتبعض. وكذلك الذكور من العبيد عليهم نصف الحدود كلها، ولا رجم عليهم، وسمَى الحد عذابًا، كقوله: وَلْيَشْهَدْ عَذابَهُما طائِفَةٌ مِنَ الْمُؤْمِنِينَ.\nالإشارة: بقدر ما يعلو المقام يُشدد العقاب، وبقدر ما يحصل من القرب يُطلَب الآداب، فليست المعصية في البعد كالمعصية في القرب، وليس يُطلب من البعيد ما يُطلب من القريب، وانظر إلى أزواج النبي صلى الله عليه وسلم حيث قال تعالى لهن: يَا نِساءَ النَّبِيِّ مَنْ يَأْتِ مِنْكُنَّ بِفاحِشَةٍ مُبَيِّنَةٍ يُضاعَفْ لَهَا الْعَذابُ ضِعْفَيْنِ. وما ذلك إلا لحظوتهن وشدة قربهن من الله. ولذلك كان لا يدخل الحضرة إلا أهل الآداب والتهذيب، بعد التدريج والتدريب، وتأمل قضيةَ الجنيد، حيث قيل له في المنام: مثلُك لا يُرضى منه هذا، حيث خطر على قلبه الاعتراض على السائل، غير أن المقربين يعاتبون، ويردون إلى الحضرة، وأهلُ البُعد يزيدون بُعداً، ولكن لا يشعرون، والله تعالى أعلم.\nثم ذكر شرط تزوج الأمة لعادم الطَوْل، فقال:\n... ذلِكَ لِمَنْ خَشِيَ الْعَنَتَ مِنْكُمْ وَأَنْ تَصْبِرُوا خَيْرٌ لَكُمْ وَاللَّهُ غَفُورٌ رَحِيمٌ (25) يُرِيدُ اللَّهُ لِيُبَيِّنَ لَكُمْ وَيَهْدِيَكُمْ سُنَنَ الَّذِينَ مِنْ قَبْلِكُمْ وَيَتُوبَ عَلَيْكُمْ وَاللَّهُ عَلِيمٌ حَكِيمٌ (26) وَاللَّهُ يُرِيدُ أَنْ يَتُوبَ عَلَيْكُمْ وَيُرِيدُ الَّذِينَ يَتَّبِعُونَ الشَّهَواتِ أَنْ تَمِيلُوا مَيْلاً عَظِيماً (27) يُرِيدُ اللَّهُ أَنْ يُخَفِّفَ عَنْكُمْ وَخُلِقَ الْإِنْسانُ ضَعِيفاً (28)\nقلت: العنت: المشقة والضرر، ولا ضرر أعظم من مواقعة الإثم، ولا سيما بأفحش الفواحش وهو الزنا، (يريد الله ليبين لكم) ، أي: لأن يُبين، واللام زائدة في المفعول، لتأكيد معنى الاستقبال اللازم للإرادة.\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله: ذلِكَ أي: نكاح الإماء إنما أبَحْتُهُ لمن خشي الوقوع في الزنا، الذي هو أقبح الفواحش، فنكاح الأمةُ، وإرقاق الولد يباع في الأسواق أخف من الزنا. وَأَنْ تَصْبِرُوا عن نكاحهن، مع التعفف عن الزنا، خَيْرٌ لَكُمْ لئلا يرق أولادكم. وعن أنس قال: سمعت النبي صلى الله عليه وسلم يقول: «من أراد أن يَلْقَى اللهَ طَاهِرًا مُطَهَّرًا فَلْيَتَزَوَّج الْحَرائِر» وقال أبو هريرة: سمعته صلّى الله عليه وسلم يقول: «الحَرَائِر صَلاحُ البَيْتَ، والإماء هلاك البيت (١) » .\nوَاللَّهُ غَفُورٌ لكم فيما سلف من المخالفة، رَحِيمٌ بكم، حيث رخَّص لكم عند خوف الإثم نكاحَ الأمَة، يُرِيدُ اللَّهُ لِيُبَيِّنَ لَكُمْ شرائع دينكم، ومصالح أموركم، وَيَهْدِيَكُمْ سُنَنَ الَّذِينَ مِنْ قَبْلِكُمْ أي: مناهج مَنْ تقدمكم من أهل الرشد، كالأنبياء والصالحين، لتسلكوا مناهجهم، كحفظ الأموال والأنساب، وتحريم الأمهات والبنات والأخوات، فإنهن محرمات على من قبلكم، وَيَتُوبَ عَلَيْكُمْ أي: يغفر ذنوبكم الماضية، أو يرشدكم إلى التوبة، أو يمنعكم من المعاصي بالعصمة. وَاللَّهُ عَلِيمٌ بما أسلفتم وما تستقبلونه من أفعالكم، حَكِيمٌ بما دبر وأبرم.\nوَاللَّهُ يُرِيدُ أَنْ يَتُوبَ عَلَيْكُمْ كرره توطئة لقوله: وَيُرِيدُ الَّذِينَ يَتَّبِعُونَ الشَّهَواتِ أَنْ تَمِيلُوا عن الحق مَيْلًا عَظِيماً بموافقتهم على اتباع الشهوات واستحلال المحرمات، وكأنه تعالى يقول: إنا نريد توبتكم ورشدكم، والذين يتبعون الشهوات يريدون ميلكم وإضلالكم، والمراد بهم الزناة لأنهم يودون أن يكون الناس كلهم زناة، وأمَّا من تعاطى شهوة النكاح في الحلال، فإنه متبع للحق لا لهم، وقد قال- عليه الصلاة والسلام-:\n«تَنَاكَحُوا تَنَاسَلُوا، فَإنِّي مُبَاهِ بِكُمُ الأممَ يَوم القِيَامة» . وقد كان سيدنا علي- كرّم الله وجهه- أزهدَ الصحابة، وكان له أربع حرائر وسبعُ جواري سُرِّيَّاتٍ، وقيل: سبع عشرة، وقيل: المراد بهم اليهود والنصارى، لأن اليهود يُحلون الأقارب من الأب وبنات الأخ وبنات الأخت. وقيل: المجوس.\nيُرِيدُ اللَّهُ أَنْ يُخَفِّفَ عَنْكُمْ فلذلك شرع لكم الشريعة الحنيفية السمحة السهلة، ورخص لكم عند المضايق في نكاح الأمة. وَخُلِقَ الْإِنْسانُ ضَعِيفاً في كل شيء، لأنه خُلق من ضعف، ويؤول إلى ضعف، أسير جَوعة، صريع شبعة، وخصوصًا عن شهوة النساء، فإنه لا يصبر عن الجماع، ولا يكون في شيء أضعفَ منه في أمر النساء، وعن عبادة ابن الصامت رضي الله عنه أنه قال: (ألا تروني أني لا أقوم إلا رفدًا (١) ، ولا آكل إلا ما لُيِّن لي، وقد مات صاحبي- يعني ذكره- منذ زمان، وما يسرني أني خلوت بامرأة لا تحل لي، وأن لي ما تطلع عليه الشمس، مخافة أن يأتيني الشيطان فيحركه، على أنه لا سمع له ولا بصر.) .\nقال ابن عباس: ثماني آيات في سورة النساء، هي خير لهذه الأمة مما طلعت عليه الشمس وغربت، يُرِيدُ اللَّهُ لِيُبَيِّنَ لَكُمْ، وَاللَّهُ يُرِيدُ أَنْ يَتُوبَ عَلَيْكُمْ، يُرِيدُ اللَّهُ أَنْ يُخَفِّفَ عَنْكُمْ، إِنْ تَجْتَنِبُوا كَبائِرَ مَا تُنْهَوْنَ عَنْهُ ...\nالآية، إِنَّ اللَّهَ لاَ يَغْفِرُ أَنْ يُشْرَكَ بِهِ.... الآية، إِنَّ اللَّهَ لا يَظْلِمُ مِثْقالَ ذَرَّةٍ ... ، وَمَنْ يَعْمَلْ سُوءاً أَوْ يَظْلِمْ نَفْسَهُ ...\nالآية، مَّا يَفْعَلُ اللَّهُ بِعَذابِكُمْ ... الآية. هـ.\nالإشارة: إنما ينزل المريد إلى العلوم الرسمية، أو الأعمال الحسية، إذا خشي الانمحاق أو الاصطلام في بحر الحقائق، وإن صبر وتماسك، حتى يتقوى على حمل أعبائها، فهو خير له، لأن الرجوع إلى الحس، لا يؤمن من\nالحبس، والله غفور لمن تنزل لعلة ما تقدم، رحيم حين جعل له الرخصة، يُرِيدُ اللَّهُ لِيُبَيِّنَ لَكُمْ سلوك الطريق إلى عين التحقيق، ويهديكم طرق الوصول، كما هدى مَنْ قبلكم، ويتوب فيما خطر ببالكم، من الفترة أو الوقفة، والله يريد أن يتعطف عليكم، لترجعوا إليه بكليتكم. وأهل الغفلة المنهمكون في الشهوات، يريدون ميلكم عن طريق الوصول إلى حضرة ربكم، يريد الله أن يخفف عنكم، فلا يُحملكم من الواردات إلا ما تطيقه طاقتكم، لأنكم ضعفاء إلا إن قوَّاكُم. اللهم قونا على ما نريد، وأيدنا فيما تريد، إِنك على كل شيءٍ قدير.\nولمّا ذكر ما يتعلق بحفظ أموال اليتامى وأموال النساء، وانجر الكلام إلى ما يتعلق بهن من حدودهن، وما يحل وما يحرم منهن، ذكر ما بقي من حفظ أموال الرجال، فقال:","quran_ar":"وَمَنْ لَمْ يَسْتَطِعْ مِنْكُمْ طَوْلاً أَنْ يَنْكِحَ الْمُحْصَناتِ الْمُؤْمِناتِ فَمِنْ مَّا مَلَكَتْ أَيْمانُكُمْ مِنْ فَتَياتِكُمُ الْمُؤْمِناتِ وَاللَّهُ أَعْلَمُ بِإِيمانِكُمْ بَعْضُكُمْ مِنْ بَعْضٍ فَانْكِحُوهُنَّ بِإِذْنِ أَهْلِهِنَّ وَآتُوهُنَّ أُجُورَهُنَّ بِالْمَعْرُوفِ مُحْصَناتٍ غَيْرَ مُسافِحاتٍ وَلا مُتَّخِذاتِ أَخْدانٍ فَإِذا أُحْصِنَّ فَإِنْ أَتَيْنَ بِفاحِشَةٍ فَعَلَيْهِنَّ نِصْفُ مَا عَلَى الْمُحْصَناتِ مِنَ الْعَذابِ ذلِكَ لِمَنْ خَشِيَ الْعَنَتَ مِنْكُمْ وَأَنْ تَصْبِرُوا خَيْرٌ لَكُمْ وَاللَّهُ غَفُورٌ رَحِيمٌ (25) يُرِيدُ اللَّهُ لِيُبَيِّنَ لَكُمْ وَيَهْدِيَكُمْ سُنَنَ الَّذِينَ مِنْ قَبْلِكُمْ وَيَتُوبَ عَلَيْكُمْ وَاللَّهُ عَلِيمٌ حَكِيمٌ (26) وَاللَّهُ يُرِيدُ أَنْ يَتُوبَ عَلَيْكُمْ وَيُرِيدُ الَّذِينَ يَتَّبِعُونَ الشَّهَواتِ أَنْ تَمِيلُوا مَيْلاً عَظِيماً (27) يُرِيدُ اللَّهُ أَنْ يُخَفِّفَ عَنْكُمْ وَخُلِقَ الْإِنْسانُ ضَعِيفاً (28)","quran_en":"25. Whoever among you lacks the means to marry free believing women may marry believing young women from among those whom your right hands possess. God knows best your faith; you are one of another. Marry them with their people’s permission and give them their bridal gifts in accordance with what is right, as women living chastely in marriage, not committing fornication or taking secret lovers. Once they are married, if they commit an indecency, they incur half the punishment prescribed for free women. This permission is for whoever among you fears falling into sin; but that you remain patient is better for you. God is Forgiving, Merciful.\n26. God wishes to make things clear to you, guide you in the ways of those before you, and turn toward you in mercy. God is Knowing, Wise.\n27. God wishes to turn toward you in mercy, while those who follow their desires wish you to turn far aside.\n28. God wishes to lighten your burden; the human being was created weak.","segments":[{"type":"language","title":"Means, syntactic objects, circumstance, and companionship","html":"<p>I say: <em>Ṭawl<\/em> (الطول) means wealth and ample means; it can also mean exaltedness. It is a verbal noun (<em>maṣdar<\/em>, مصدر) of <em>ṭāla ṭawlan<\/em>. It functions either as the direct object (<em>mafʿūl<\/em>, مفعول) of <strong>“is able”<\/strong> or as a cognate verbal noun (<em>maṣdar<\/em>), since the meanings are closely related. <strong>“To marry”<\/strong> (<em>an yankiḥa<\/em>, أن ينكح) is, on the first analysis, a substitute (<em>badal<\/em>, بدل) for that object, or, on the second, the direct object—“[has the means] to marry.” <strong>“Chaste, not fornicators”<\/strong> are two circumstantial accusatives (<em>ḥālān<\/em>, حالان), governed by <strong>“marry them”<\/strong> (<em>ankiḥūhunna<\/em>, أنكحوهن). And <em>khidn<\/em> (الخدن) means a lover or intimate companion.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"I say: Ṭawl (الطول) means wealth and ample means; it can also mean exaltedness. It is a verbal noun ( maṣdar , مصدر) of ṭāla ṭawlan . It functions either as the direct object ( mafʿūl , مفعول) of “is able” or as a cognate verbal noun ( maṣdar ), since the meanings are closely related. “To marry” ( an yankiḥa , أن ينكح) is, on the first analysis, a substitute ( badal , بدل) for that object, or, on the second, the direct object—“[has the means] to marry.” “Chaste, not fornicators” are two circumstantial accusatives ( ḥālān , حالان), governed by “marry them” ( ankiḥūhunna , أنكحوهن). And khidn (الخدن) means a lover or intimate companion."},{"type":"exposition","title":"Marriage to an enslaved woman and the diversity of legal conditions","html":"<p>The Real—majestic is His majesty—says: <strong>“Whoever among you lacks the means”<\/strong>, namely, does not possess the wealth that would enable him to marry <strong>“chaste believing women”<\/strong>, that is, free believing women, may marry from among <strong>“those whom your right hands possess, of your believing young women”<\/strong>: enslaved believing women, but not unbelieving ones. If one of these women makes an outward profession of faith, that is sufficient: knowledge of the inward state belongs to God alone, for <strong>“God knows best your faith.”<\/strong> Nor should fear of social disgrace deter you from marrying them, for you are of one kind and of one religion: <strong>“You are one of another.”<\/strong> Do not be too proud to marry them.<\/p><p><strong>“Marry them with the permission of their people”<\/strong> means with the consent of their owners, who conclude the marriage contract for you. <strong>“Give them their bridal gifts”<\/strong> (<em>ujūrahunna<\/em>, أجورهن), which, in Mālik’s school as presented here, belong more properly to the women themselves rather than to their masters, <strong>“in the recognized manner”<\/strong> (<em>bi-l-maʿrūf<\/em>, بالمعروف): without delaying payment or diminishing it, as required by the Sunna. They are to be <strong>“chaste”<\/strong> (<em>muḥṣanāt<\/em>, محصنات), that is, modest, <strong>“not fornicators”<\/strong>, and <strong>“not taking secret lovers”<\/strong> (<em>akhdān<\/em>, أخدان), namely, companions with whom they commit fornication. In pre-Islamic times, some women took one exclusive partner for illicit intercourse, while others rejected no man who approached them.<\/p><p>Ibn Juzayy says: “According to Mālik and most of his companions, a free man may marry an enslaved woman only on two conditions. One is the absence of means (<em>ʿadam al-ṭawl<\/em>, عدم الطول)—that he lack enough to marry a free woman. The other is fear of hardship (<em>khawf al-ʿanat<\/em>, خوف العنت), namely fornication, because of what follows: <strong>‘That is for one among you who fears hardship.’<\/strong> Ibn al-Qāsim allowed their marriage even in the absence of both conditions, on the view that the <em>dalīl al-khiṭāb<\/em> (دليل الخطاب)—inference from what a statement leaves unsaid—is not legally probative. They agreed upon requiring that the enslaved woman be Muslim, on the strength of <strong>‘of your believing young women’<\/strong>, except the jurists of Iraq, who did not impose that condition.” End of quotation. <a class=\"noteref\" href=\"#/note/N131\">[N131]<\/a><\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real—majestic is His majesty—says: “Whoever among you lacks the means” , namely, does not possess the wealth that would enable him to marry “chaste believing women” , that is, free believing women, may marry from among “those whom your right hands possess, of your believing young women” : enslaved believing women, but not unbelieving ones. If one of these women makes an outward profession of faith, that is sufficient: knowledge of the inward state belongs to God alone, for “God knows best your faith.” Nor should fear of social disgrace deter you from marrying them, for you are of one kind and of one religion: “You are one of another.” Do not be too proud to marry them. “Marry them with the permission of their people” means with the consent of their owners, who conclude the marriage contract for you. “Give them their bridal gifts” ( ujūrahunna , أجورهن), which, in Mālik’s school as presented here, belong more properly to the women themselves rather than to their masters, “in the recognized manner” ( bi-l-maʿrūf , بالمعروف): without delaying payment or diminishing it, as required by the Sunna. They are to be “chaste” ( muḥṣanāt , محصنات), that is, modest, “not fornicators” , and “not taking secret lovers” ( akhdān , أخدان), namely, companions with whom they commit fornication. In pre-Islamic times, some women took one exclusive partner for illicit intercourse, while others rejected no man who approached them. Ibn Juzayy says: “According to Mālik and most of his companions, a free man may marry an enslaved woman only on two conditions. One is the absence of means ( ʿadam al-ṭawl , عدم الطول)—that he lack enough to marry a free woman. The other is fear of hardship ( khawf al-ʿanat , خوف العنت), namely fornication, because of what follows: ‘That is for one among you who fears hardship.’ Ibn al-Qāsim allowed their marriage even in the absence of both conditions, on the view that the dalīl al-khiṭāb (دليل الخطاب)—inference from what a statement leaves unsaid—is not legally probative. They agreed upon requiring that the enslaved woman be Muslim, on the strength of ‘of your believing young women’ , except the jurists of Iraq, who did not impose that condition.” End of quotation. [N131]"},{"type":"spiritual","title":"the “virgins” of reality and formal learning","html":"<p>Whoever is unable to marry the virgins of spiritual realities (<em>abkār al-ḥaqāʾiq<\/em>, أبكار الحقائق), because he cannot drive distractions and attachments (<em>shawāghil wa-ʿalāʾiq<\/em>, شواغل وعلائق) from his heart, should descend to the marriage of formal sciences (<em>al-ʿulūm al-rasmiyya<\/em>, العلوم الرسمية) and sense-based acts (<em>al-aʿmāl al-ḥissiyya<\/em>, الأعمال الحسية). He should receive these from their masters, protect his acquisition of them with sincerity (<em>ikhlāṣ<\/em>, إخلاص), fulfil their rights to the extent he can by sharing them with those qualified for them, and patiently disseminate them. God does not waste the reward of anyone who acts well. If his intention is sound and his action sincere, God will appoint for him one of His friends (<em>walī<\/em>, ولي) who enriches him through God, until he becomes among those rich through Him. Then he will become qualified to marry free women and will join the great friends of God. “And that is not difficult for God.”<\/p><p>When Shaykh Abū al-Ḥasan al-Shādhilī, may God be pleased with him, spoke about the fruits of love, he said: “You will see the soul inclined toward His obedience, the intellect fortified by knowledge of Him, the spirit taken up into His Presence, and the secret immersed in witnessing Him. The servant seeks an increase [in love] and receives an increase; he is opened to what is sweeter than the delight of intimate conversation with Him; he is clothed in the robes of nearness upon the carpet of nearness; and he touches the virgins of realities and the previously married women of the sciences.” End of quotation. Thus the knowledge of realities is “virgins”; the sciences of the Path that lead to them are “free, previously married women”; and the remaining formal disciplines are, by comparison, “enslaved women.” God knows best. <a class=\"noteref\" href=\"#/note/N132\">[N132]<\/a><\/p>","authorial":true,"text":"Whoever is unable to marry the virgins of spiritual realities ( abkār al-ḥaqāʾiq , أبكار الحقائق), because he cannot drive distractions and attachments ( shawāghil wa-ʿalāʾiq , شواغل وعلائق) from his heart, should descend to the marriage of formal sciences ( al-ʿulūm al-rasmiyya , العلوم الرسمية) and sense-based acts ( al-aʿmāl al-ḥissiyya , الأعمال الحسية). He should receive these from their masters, protect his acquisition of them with sincerity ( ikhlāṣ , إخلاص), fulfil their rights to the extent he can by sharing them with those qualified for them, and patiently disseminate them. God does not waste the reward of anyone who acts well. If his intention is sound and his action sincere, God will appoint for him one of His friends ( walī , ولي) who enriches him through God, until he becomes among those rich through Him. Then he will become qualified to marry free women and will join the great friends of God. “And that is not difficult for God.” When Shaykh Abū al-Ḥasan al-Shādhilī, may God be pleased with him, spoke about the fruits of love, he said: “You will see the soul inclined toward His obedience, the intellect fortified by knowledge of Him, the spirit taken up into His Presence, and the secret immersed in witnessing Him. The servant seeks an increase [in love] and receives an increase; he is opened to what is sweeter than the delight of intimate conversation with Him; he is clothed in the robes of nearness upon the carpet of nearness; and he touches the virgins of realities and the previously married women of the sciences.” End of quotation. Thus the knowledge of realities is “virgins”; the sciences of the Path that lead to them are “free, previously married women”; and the remaining formal disciplines are, by comparison, “enslaved women.” God knows best. [N132]"},{"type":"transition","title":"","html":"<p class=\"transition-lead\">He then describes the penalty concerning the enslaved woman who commits fornication<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"He then describes the penalty concerning the enslaved woman who commits fornication"},{"type":"language","title":"Different readings of the verb “protected by marriage”","html":"<p>I say: <em>Aḥṣana al-rajul<\/em> (أحصن الرجل), with either fatḥa or ḍamma on the initial hamza, means that the man became <em>muḥṣan<\/em> (محصن), with either fatḥa or kasra [in that derived form]; in this instance the active and passive constructions (<em>al-bināʾ li-l-fāʿil wa-l-mafʿūl<\/em>, البناء للفاعل والمفعول) coincide. According to another account, the form with fatḥa signifies embracing Islam, while that with ḍamma signifies marrying.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"I say: Aḥṣana al-rajul (أحصن الرجل), with either fatḥa or ḍamma on the initial hamza, means that the man became muḥṣan (محصن), with either fatḥa or kasra [in that derived form]; in this instance the active and passive constructions ( al-bināʾ li-l-fāʿil wa-l-mafʿūl , البناء للفاعل والمفعول) coincide. According to another account, the form with fatḥa signifies embracing Islam, while that with ḍamma signifies marrying."},{"type":"exposition","title":"The juristic rule on the reduced penalty","html":"<p>The Real—majestic is His majesty—says: If enslaved women are married, then <strong>“if they commit an indecency”<\/strong> (<em>fāḥisha<\/em>, فاحشة)—fornication—their prescribed punishment is half of that imposed upon a free woman: fifty [lashes], because the punishment of an unmarried [free] person is one hundred. One may also infer that they do not undergo stoning, since stoning admits of no division into halves. Likewise enslaved males receive half of the prescribed penalties, and there is no stoning for them. God calls the prescribed penalty “chastisement” (<em>ʿadhāb<\/em>, عذاب), as in <strong>“Let a group of believers witness their chastisement”<\/strong> [Q 24:2]. <a class=\"noteref\" href=\"#/note/N133\">[N133]<\/a><\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real—majestic is His majesty—says: If enslaved women are married, then “if they commit an indecency” ( fāḥisha , فاحشة)—fornication—their prescribed punishment is half of that imposed upon a free woman: fifty [lashes], because the punishment of an unmarried [free] person is one hundred. One may also infer that they do not undergo stoning, since stoning admits of no division into halves. Likewise enslaved males receive half of the prescribed penalties, and there is no stoning for them. God calls the prescribed penalty “chastisement” ( ʿadhāb , عذاب), as in “Let a group of believers witness their chastisement” [Q 24:2]. [N133]"},{"type":"spiritual","title":"the closer the station, the finer the discipline","html":"<p>As rank (<em>maqām<\/em>, مقام) rises, punishment becomes more severe; as nearness increases, good comportment (<em>ādāb<\/em>, آداب) is required more strictly. A lapse far from God is not like a lapse in nearness, and what is expected of someone far away is not what is expected of someone close. Consider the wives of the Prophet, blessings and peace upon him, to whom God said: <strong>“O wives of the Prophet, whoever among you commits a manifest indecency shall have her chastisement doubled”<\/strong> [Q 33:30]. This is only because of their favor and extraordinary nearness to God. Therefore none enters the Presence (<em>al-ḥaḍra<\/em>, الحضرة) except those of comportment and refinement, after gradual education and training. Reflect on the case of al-Junayd: he was told in a dream, “Such a thing cannot be accepted from someone like you,” after the thought of objecting to a petitioner had crossed his heart. Yet those drawn near are reproved and brought back into the Presence, while those remote become still more remote without perceiving it. God knows best. <a class=\"noteref\" href=\"#/note/N134\">[N134]<\/a><\/p>","authorial":true,"text":"As rank ( maqām , مقام) rises, punishment becomes more severe; as nearness increases, good comportment ( ādāb , آداب) is required more strictly. A lapse far from God is not like a lapse in nearness, and what is expected of someone far away is not what is expected of someone close. Consider the wives of the Prophet, blessings and peace upon him, to whom God said: “O wives of the Prophet, whoever among you commits a manifest indecency shall have her chastisement doubled” [Q 33:30]. This is only because of their favor and extraordinary nearness to God. Therefore none enters the Presence ( al-ḥaḍra , الحضرة) except those of comportment and refinement, after gradual education and training. Reflect on the case of al-Junayd: he was told in a dream, “Such a thing cannot be accepted from someone like you,” after the thought of objecting to a petitioner had crossed his heart. Yet those drawn near are reproved and brought back into the Presence, while those remote become still more remote without perceiving it. God knows best. [N134]"},{"type":"transition","title":"","html":"<p class=\"transition-lead\">He next returns to the condition for marrying an enslaved woman when means are lacking<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"He next returns to the condition for marrying an enslaved woman when means are lacking"},{"type":"language","title":"The term ʿanat and the purpose-lām in “God wishes to clarify”","html":"<p>I say: <em>ʿAnat<\/em> (العنت) means hardship and harm; no harm is greater than falling into sin, especially the gravest sexual indecency, fornication. <strong>“God desires to explain to you”<\/strong> (<em>yurīdu Allāhu li-yubayyina lakum<\/em>, يريد الله ليبين لكم) means “so that He may explain”; the <em>lām<\/em> is an additional particle (<em>lām zāʾida<\/em>, لام زائدة) in an object construction, reinforcing the prospective sense that necessarily belongs to intending.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"I say: ʿAnat (العنت) means hardship and harm; no harm is greater than falling into sin, especially the gravest sexual indecency, fornication. “God desires to explain to you” ( yurīdu Allāhu li-yubayyina lakum , يريد الله ليبين لكم) means “so that He may explain”; the lām is an additional particle ( lām zāʾida , لام زائدة) in an object construction, reinforcing the prospective sense that necessarily belongs to intending."},{"type":"exposition","title":"Fear of fornication, patience, and reports about marriage","html":"<p>The Real—majestic is His majesty—says: <strong>“That”<\/strong>—the marriage of enslaved women—“is for whoever among you fears hardship”: I have only permitted it for one who fears falling into fornication, the vilest of indecencies. Under the historical law being described, the marriage of an enslaved woman, with the prospect that her child would also enter slavery and be sold in the markets, is treated as less harmful than fornication. <strong>“But that you should remain patient is better for you”<\/strong>: refrain from such a marriage while abstaining from fornication, so that your children do not become enslaved. <a class=\"noteref\" href=\"#/note/N135\">[N135]<\/a><\/p><p>Anas said: “I heard the Prophet, blessings and peace upon him, say, ‘Whoever wishes to meet God pure and purified, let him marry free women.’” Abū Hurayra said: “I heard him say, ‘Free women are the well-being of the household, and enslaved women the destruction of the household.’” [Translator’s note: Both reports are transmitted in the tradition literature, but neither may safely be treated as an authenticated Prophetic saying.] <a class=\"noteref\" href=\"#/hadith/H023\">[H023]<\/a> <a class=\"noteref\" href=\"#/hadith/H024\">[H024]<\/a><\/p><p><strong>“And God is Forgiving”<\/strong> toward you for whatever contravention has passed; <strong>“Merciful”<\/strong> to you because He granted a dispensation (<em>rukhṣa<\/em>, رخصة) to marry an enslaved woman when you fear sin. <strong>“God desires to make clear to you”<\/strong> the ordinances of your religion and the welfare of your affairs, <strong>“and to guide you to the ways of those who came before you”<\/strong>, the paths of the rightly guided—prophets and righteous people—so that you follow their ways: protecting property and descent, and prohibiting marriage to mothers, daughters and sisters, just as these were prohibited to those before you. <strong>“And to turn to you”<\/strong> means to forgive your past sins, or guide you to repentance, or preserve you from disobedience through protection (<em>ʿiṣma<\/em>, عصمة). <strong>“God is Knowing”<\/strong> of all your past and future acts and <strong>“Wise”<\/strong> in what He disposes and determines.<\/p><p><strong>“God desires to turn toward you”<\/strong> is repeated to prepare for the contrast <strong>“while those who follow desires wish you to incline tremendously”<\/strong>, away from truth, so that you conform to them in pursuit of appetites and treating forbidden acts as lawful. It is as though God says: We wish your repentance and right guidance; those who follow appetites desire your deviation and ruin. He means fornicators, who would like everyone to be like themselves. As for someone who satisfies sexual desire in a lawful marriage, he follows the truth and not them. The Prophet, blessings and peace upon him, is reported to have said: “Marry and beget children; I will boast of your numbers before the nations on the Day of Resurrection.” <a class=\"noteref\" href=\"#/hadith/H025\">[H025]<\/a> Our master ʿAlī—may God ennoble his face—was among the most ascetic of the Companions, yet he had four free wives and seven women kept as concubines; another report gives seventeen. Another interpretation takes <strong>“those who follow appetites”<\/strong> as Jews and Christians, on the ground that Jews permit marriage within certain close relations, such as paternal relatives and brothers’ and sisters’ daughters; still another says that it means the Magians. [Translator’s note: These are Ibn ʿAjība’s transmitted identifications, not a definition established by the verse itself.] <a class=\"noteref\" href=\"#/note/N136\">[N136]<\/a><\/p><p><strong>“God desires to lighten things for you”<\/strong>: He accordingly ordained the easy, broad and upright religious law (<em>al-sharīʿa al-ḥanīfiyya al-samḥa al-sahla<\/em>, الشريعة الحنيفية السمحة السهلة), and in circumstances of pressure He allowed the dispensation of marrying an enslaved woman. <strong>“The human being was created weak”<\/strong> in every respect. He was created in weakness and will return to weakness; a single bout of hunger enslaves him, and a full meal can strike him down. Particularly in matters of sexual desire, he is unable to endure indefinitely without intercourse; in no matter, says the author, is a man weaker than with respect to women.<\/p><p>ʿUbāda b. al-Ṣāmit, may God be pleased with him, reportedly said: “Do you not see that I can stand only with support, and eat only what has been softened for me? My companion”—meaning his male organ—“has been dead for some time. Yet it would not please me to be alone with a woman not lawful to me, even if I possessed everything upon which the sun rises, for fear that Satan should come and stir it, though it has neither hearing nor sight.” <a class=\"noteref\" href=\"#/note/N137\">[N137]<\/a><\/p><p>Ibn ʿAbbās said: “There are eight verses in Sūrat al-Nisāʾ better for this community than everything upon which the sun rises and sets”: <strong>“God desires to explain to you”<\/strong> [4:26], <strong>“God desires to turn toward you”<\/strong> [4:27], <strong>“God desires to lighten things for you”<\/strong> [4:28], <strong>“If you avoid the grave sins you are forbidden”<\/strong> [4:31], <strong>“God does not forgive the association of partners with Him”<\/strong> [4:48], <strong>“God does not wrong [anyone] by so much as an atom’s weight”<\/strong> [4:40], <strong>“Whoever does evil or wrongs his own soul”<\/strong> [4:110], and <strong>“What would God gain from punishing you?”<\/strong> [4:147]. End of report. <a class=\"noteref\" href=\"#/note/N138\">[N138]<\/a><\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real—majestic is His majesty—says: “That” —the marriage of enslaved women—“is for whoever among you fears hardship”: I have only permitted it for one who fears falling into fornication, the vilest of indecencies. Under the historical law being described, the marriage of an enslaved woman, with the prospect that her child would also enter slavery and be sold in the markets, is treated as less harmful than fornication. “But that you should remain patient is better for you” : refrain from such a marriage while abstaining from fornication, so that your children do not become enslaved. [N135] Anas said: “I heard the Prophet, blessings and peace upon him, say, ‘Whoever wishes to meet God pure and purified, let him marry free women.’” Abū Hurayra said: “I heard him say, ‘Free women are the well-being of the household, and enslaved women the destruction of the household.’” [Translator’s note: Both reports are transmitted in the tradition literature, but neither may safely be treated as an authenticated Prophetic saying.] [H023] [H024] “And God is Forgiving” toward you for whatever contravention has passed; “Merciful” to you because He granted a dispensation ( rukhṣa , رخصة) to marry an enslaved woman when you fear sin. “God desires to make clear to you” the ordinances of your religion and the welfare of your affairs, “and to guide you to the ways of those who came before you” , the paths of the rightly guided—prophets and righteous people—so that you follow their ways: protecting property and descent, and prohibiting marriage to mothers, daughters and sisters, just as these were prohibited to those before you. “And to turn to you” means to forgive your past sins, or guide you to repentance, or preserve you from disobedience through protection ( ʿiṣma , عصمة). “God is Knowing” of all your past and future acts and “Wise” in what He disposes and determines. “God desires to turn toward you” is repeated to prepare for the contrast “while those who follow desires wish you to incline tremendously” , away from truth, so that you conform to them in pursuit of appetites and treating forbidden acts as lawful. It is as though God says: We wish your repentance and right guidance; those who follow appetites desire your deviation and ruin. He means fornicators, who would like everyone to be like themselves. As for someone who satisfies sexual desire in a lawful marriage, he follows the truth and not them. The Prophet, blessings and peace upon him, is reported to have said: “Marry and beget children; I will boast of your numbers before the nations on the Day of Resurrection.” [H025] Our master ʿAlī—may God ennoble his face—was among the most ascetic of the Companions, yet he had four free wives and seven women kept as concubines; another report gives seventeen. Another interpretation takes “those who follow appetites” as Jews and Christians, on the ground that Jews permit marriage within certain close relations, such as paternal relatives and brothers’ and sisters’ daughters; still another says that it means the Magians. [Translator’s note: These are Ibn ʿAjība’s transmitted identifications, not a definition established by the verse itself.] [N136] “God desires to lighten things for you” : He accordingly ordained the easy, broad and upright religious law ( al-sharīʿa al-ḥanīfiyya al-samḥa al-sahla , الشريعة الحنيفية السمحة السهلة), and in circumstances of pressure He allowed the dispensation of marrying an enslaved woman. “The human being was created weak” in every respect. He was created in weakness and will return to weakness; a single bout of hunger enslaves him, and a full meal can strike him down. Particularly in matters of sexual desire, he is unable to endure indefinitely without intercourse; in no matter, says the author, is a man weaker than with respect to women. ʿUbāda b. al-Ṣāmit, may God be pleased with him, reportedly said: “Do you not see that I can stand only with support, and eat only what has been softened for me? My companion”—meaning his male organ—“has been dead for some time. Yet it would not please me to be alone with a woman not lawful to me, even if I possessed everything upon which the sun rises, for fear that Satan should come and stir it, though it has neither hearing nor sight.” [N137] Ibn ʿAbbās said: “There are eight verses in Sūrat al-Nisāʾ better for this community than everything upon which the sun rises and sets”: “God desires to explain to you” [4:26], “God desires to turn toward you” [4:27], “God desires to lighten things for you” [4:28], “If you avoid the grave sins you are forbidden” [4:31], “God does not forgive the association of partners with Him” [4:48], “God does not wrong [anyone] by so much as an atom’s weight” [4:40], “Whoever does evil or wrongs his own soul” [4:110], and “What would God gain from punishing you?” [4:147]. End of report. [N138]"},{"type":"spiritual","title":"overload, concession, and return to the path","html":"<p>The disciple (<em>murīd<\/em>, مريد) descends to the formal sciences or sensible activities only if he fears being blotted out (<em>inmiḥāq<\/em>, انمحاق) or overwhelmed (<em>iṣṭilām<\/em>, اصطلام) in the sea of realities. If he can remain patient and hold firm until strengthened to carry its burdens, that is better for him, because after returning to sensible experience (<em>al-ḥiss<\/em>, الحس) there is no certainty that he will not be detained there (<em>al-ḥabs<\/em>, الحبس). Yet God is Forgiving to one who descends for the reason just given, Merciful in having established the dispensation for him.<\/p><p><strong>“God desires to explain to you”<\/strong> the journey of the Path (<em>sulūk al-ṭarīq<\/em>, سلوك الطريق) toward the very reality of realization (<em>ʿayn al-taḥqīq<\/em>, عين التحقيق), and guide you along the roads of arrival (<em>ṭuruq al-wuṣūl<\/em>, طرق الوصول), as He guided those before you; He forgives the pause or stagnation (<em>fatra aw waqfa<\/em>, فترة أو وقفة) that might cross your mind, and He seeks to turn tenderly toward you so that you return to Him with your entire being. But the heedless who are submerged in appetites wish you to deviate from the way of arrival into your Lord’s Presence. God wishes to lighten your burden: He will not lay upon you spiritual visitations (<em>wāridāt<\/em>, واردات) beyond what your strength can bear, for you are weak unless He strengthens you. O God, strengthen us for what we desire and support us in what You desire; You have power over all things.<\/p>","authorial":true,"text":"The disciple ( murīd , مريد) descends to the formal sciences or sensible activities only if he fears being blotted out ( inmiḥāq , انمحاق) or overwhelmed ( iṣṭilām , اصطلام) in the sea of realities. If he can remain patient and hold firm until strengthened to carry its burdens, that is better for him, because after returning to sensible experience ( al-ḥiss , الحس) there is no certainty that he will not be detained there ( al-ḥabs , الحبس). Yet God is Forgiving to one who descends for the reason just given, Merciful in having established the dispensation for him. “God desires to explain to you” the journey of the Path ( sulūk al-ṭarīq , سلوك الطريق) toward the very reality of realization ( ʿayn al-taḥqīq , عين التحقيق), and guide you along the roads of arrival ( ṭuruq al-wuṣūl , طرق الوصول), as He guided those before you; He forgives the pause or stagnation ( fatra aw waqfa , فترة أو وقفة) that might cross your mind, and He seeks to turn tenderly toward you so that you return to Him with your entire being. But the heedless who are submerged in appetites wish you to deviate from the way of arrival into your Lord’s Presence. God wishes to lighten your burden: He will not lay upon you spiritual visitations ( wāridāt , واردات) beyond what your strength can bear, for you are weak unless He strengthens you. O God, strengthen us for what we desire and support us in what You desire; You have power over all things."},{"type":"transition","title":"","html":"<p class=\"transition-lead\">After speaking of protecting the estates of orphans and women, and being led into the legal boundaries concerning women, he now resumes the protection of people’s property<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"After speaking of protecting the estates of orphans and women, and being led into the legal boundaries concerning women, he now resumes the protection of people’s property"}],"cairo_notes":[{"id":"C490.1","page":490,"ar":"في الأصول: بمعونته، والمثبت هو ما في لطائف المنن للسكندري.","en":"The source copies read “by His assistance” (bi-maʿūnatihi). The reading printed here is that found in al-Iskandarī’s Laṭāʾif al-minan.","anchor":"والعقل متحصنا بمعرفته"},{"id":"C490.2","page":490,"ar":"ما بين المعكوفتين من تدخل الشيخ المفسر في النقل.","en":"The words within square brackets were inserted by the shaykh commentator in transmitting the quotation.","anchor":"[من حبه ]"},{"id":"C491.1","page":491,"ar":"الحديث ضعفه السيوطي في الجامع الصغير.","en":"Al-Suyūṭī classified this hadith as weak in al-Jāmiʿ al-ṣaghīr.","anchor":"الحَرَائِر صَلاحُ البَيْتَ، والإماء هلاك البيت"},{"id":"C492.1","page":492,"ar":"أي: إلا بمعاونة غيري.","en":"That is, only with someone else’s assistance.","anchor":"لا أقوم إلا رفدًا"}],"english_characters":13806,"arabic_characters":8301},{"id":"q4-29-30","q":"4:29–30","s":4,"surah":"Al-Nisāʾ","title":"4:29–30 — Trading by consent, preservation of life, and the spiritual meaning of self-destruction","source":"Cairo Arabic edition I:493–495","cairo":"I:493–495","source_page_first":493,"source_page_last":495,"ar":"يا أَيُّهَا الَّذِينَ آمَنُوا لا تَأْكُلُوا أَمْوالَكُمْ بَيْنَكُمْ بِالْباطِلِ إِلاَّ أَنْ تَكُونَ تِجارَةٌ عَنْ تَراضٍ مِنْكُمْ ...\nقلت: الاستثناء منقطع، وكان تامة لمن رفَع، وناقصة لمن نصب، واسمها: ضمير الأموال، على حذف مضاف، إلاَّ أن تكون الأموال أموالَ تجارة.\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله: يا أَيُّهَا الَّذِينَ آمَنُوا لا تَأْكُلُوا أَمْوالَكُمْ بَيْنَكُمْ بِالْباطِلِ الذي لا تُجوزه الشريعة، كالربا والقمار، والغصب والسرقة، والخيانة والكهانة والسحر وغير ذلك. إِلَّا أَنْ تَكُونَ، أي: لكن إن وجدت تِجارَةً صحيحة عَنْ تَراضٍ مِنْكُمْ أي: اتفاق منكم على البيع، وبه استدلت المالكية على انعقاد البيع بالعقد ولو لم يحصل تفرق بالأبدان.\nوقال الشافعي: إنما يتم بالتفرق بالأبدان، لقوله- عليه الصلاة والسلام: «البَيِّعَان بالخِيَار مَا لَمْ يتَفَرَّقَا» . وحمله مالك على التفرق بالكلام، وقال أكثر المفسرين: التخيير، هو أن يُخير كلُ واحد منهما صاحبه بعد عقد البيع. وقد ابتاع عمرو ابن جرير فرسًا، ثم خَيَّر صاحبه بعد البيع، ثم قال: سمعت أبا هريرة يقول: البيع عن تراض. قال البيضاوي:\nوتخصيص التجارة من الوجوه التي يحل بها انتقال مال الغير، لأنها أغلب وأوفق لذوي المروءات، ويجوز أن يُراد بها الانتقال مطلقًا. وقيل: المقصود بالنهي: صرف المال فيما لا يرضاه الله تعالى، وبالتجارة: صرفه فيما يرضى. هـ.\nالإشارة: لا تصرفوا أموالكم ولا أحوالكم في غير ما يُقربكم إلى الحق فإن ما سوى الحق كله باطل، كما قال الشاعر:\nألا كل شيء ما خلا الله باطل ... وكل نعيم لا محالة زائل (١)\nإلا أن يكون صرفه في تجارة رابحة، تقربكم من الحبيب، وتجلبُكم إلى حضرة القريب، فتلك تجارة رابحة وصفقة نافعة. والله تعالى أعلم.\nثم تكلم على بعض ما يتعلق بحفظ الأبدان، وسيأتي تمامه في قوله: (وَمَا كَانَ لمؤمن ... ) إلى آخر الآيات، فقال:\n... وَلا تَقْتُلُوا أَنْفُسَكُمْ إِنَّ اللَّهَ كانَ بِكُمْ رَحِيماً (29) وَمَنْ يَفْعَلْ ذلِكَ عُدْواناً وَظُلْماً فَسَوْفَ نُصْلِيهِ نَاراً وَكانَ ذلِكَ عَلَى اللَّهِ يَسِيراً (30)\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله: وَلا تَقْتُلُوا أَنْفُسَكُمْ، بالخنق أو بالنخع (١) أو بالجرح، الذي يؤدي إلى الموت، أو بالإلقاء إلى التهلكة. وعن عمرو بن العاص رضي الله عنه قال: (بعثني رسول الله صلى الله عليه وسلم في غَزوة ذَات السَّلاَسِلِ، فأجنَبتُ في لَيْلَة بَارِدَة، فَأشْفَقْتُ على نفسي وصَليْتُ بأصحَابِي صَلاة الصُبح بالتيمم. فلَمّا قَدِمتُ ذَكَرتُ ذلك لرسول الله صلى الله عليه وسلم، فقال: «يا عَمرُو صلَّيتَ بأصحَابِكَ وأنت جُنُب؟» قلت: نعم يا رسول الله، أشفقت إن اغتسلتُ أن أهلك، فذكرت قوله تعالى: لا تَقْتُلُوا أَنْفُسَكُمْ إِنَّ اللَّهَ كانَ بِكُمْ رَحِيماً، فضحك النبي صلى الله عليه وسلم، ولم يقل شيئاً) .\nأو: ولا تقتلوا إخوانكم في الإسلام، فإن المؤمنين كنفس واحدة. قال البيضاوي: جمع في التوصية بين حفظ النفس والمال- الذي هو شقيقها من حيث إنه سبب قوامها- استبقاء لهم. هـ.\nوإنما نهاكم عن قتل أنفسكم رأفة، ورحمة بكم، إِنَّ اللَّهَ كانَ بِكُمْ رَحِيماً، فقد أمر بني إسرائيل بقتل أنفسهم، وأنتم نهاكم عنه. وَمَنْ يَفْعَلْ ذلِكَ القتل. أو جميع ما سبق من المحرمات عُدْواناً وَظُلْماً، أي:\nإفراطًا في التجاوز عن الحد، وإتيانًا بما لا يستحق، أو تعديًا على الغير وظلمًا على النفس، بتعريضها للعقاب، فَسَوْفَ نُصْلِيهِ نارًا أي: نحرقه ونشويه فيها. وَكانَ ذلِكَ عَلَى اللَّهِ يَسِيراً.\nوفي الحديث عنه صلّى الله عليه وسلم: «مَن قَتَل نفسَهُ بِشَيءٍ عذب به في نار جهنم خَالِدًا مُخَلَّدًا فِيهَا» وهو تغليظ، أو لمن استحل ذلك. وهذا الوعيد الذي ذكره الحق هنا في قتل الإنسان بيده، أهون مما ذكره في قتل الغير، الذي يأتي، لأنه زاد هناك الغضب واللعنة والعذاب العظيم، أما قول ابن عطية: إنه أجمع المفسرون أن هذه الآية في قتل بعضهم بعضًا، فليس بصحيح، والله تعالى أعلم.\nالإشارة: ولا تقتلوا أنفسكم باتباع الشهوات وتراكم الغفلات، فإنه يفوتها الحياة الحقيقية. وقال الفضيل بن عياض رضي الله عنه: (لا تغفلوا عن حظ أنفسكم، فمن غفل عن حظ نفسه، فكأنما قتلها) . وحظ النفس هو تزكيتها وتحليتها بالكمالات، أو قُوتها من العلم اليقين، والمعرفة وصحة التمكين، والمراد بالنفس هنا الروح، وأما ما اصطلحت عليه الصوفية من أن النفس يجب قتلها، فإن مرادهم بذلك النفس الأمارة، فإن الروح ما دامت مُظلمة بالمعاصي والهوى سُميت نفساً، فإذا تطهرت وتزكت سميت روحًا. وهو المراد هنا. سماها نفسًا باعتبار ما كانت عليه. والله تعالى أعلم.\nثم إن قتل النفس من الكبائر، فمن اجتنبه مع غيره من الكبائر غفرت له الصغائر، كما أشار إلى ذلك ترغيبا فى اجتناب ما ذكر، فقال:","quran_ar":"يا أَيُّهَا الَّذِينَ آمَنُوا لا تَأْكُلُوا أَمْوالَكُمْ بَيْنَكُمْ بِالْباطِلِ إِلاَّ أَنْ تَكُونَ تِجارَةٌ عَنْ تَراضٍ مِنْكُمْ وَلا تَقْتُلُوا أَنْفُسَكُمْ إِنَّ اللَّهَ كانَ بِكُمْ رَحِيماً (29) وَمَنْ يَفْعَلْ ذلِكَ عُدْواناً وَظُلْماً فَسَوْفَ نُصْلِيهِ نَاراً وَكانَ ذلِكَ عَلَى اللَّهِ يَسِيراً (30)","quran_en":"29. O you who believe, do not consume one another’s wealth unjustly, except through commerce conducted by mutual consent among you; and do not kill yourselves. God is indeed Merciful to you.\n30. Whoever does that in aggression and wrongdoing, We shall make him enter a Fire; and that is easy for God.","segments":[{"type":"language","title":"The disconnected exception and the two analyses of kāna","html":"<p>I say: The exception (<em>al-istithnāʾ<\/em>, الاستثناء) is disconnected (<em>munqaṭiʿ<\/em>, منقطع), meaning “but [let there be] trade.” In the reading with the nominative (<em>rafʿ<\/em>, رفع), <strong>“be”<\/strong> (<em>kāna<\/em>, كان) is complete (<em>tāmma<\/em>, تامة), meaning “occur”; in the accusative reading (<em>naṣb<\/em>, نصب), it is incomplete (<em>nāqiṣa<\/em>, ناقصة), with a pronoun referring to property as its subject after supplying an omitted construct: “except that the property be property of trade.”<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"I say: The exception ( al-istithnāʾ , الاستثناء) is disconnected ( munqaṭiʿ , منقطع), meaning “but [let there be] trade.” In the reading with the nominative ( rafʿ , رفع), “be” ( kāna , كان) is complete ( tāmma , تامة), meaning “occur”; in the accusative reading ( naṣb , نصب), it is incomplete ( nāqiṣa , ناقصة), with a pronoun referring to property as its subject after supplying an omitted construct: “except that the property be property of trade.”"},{"type":"exposition","title":"Unlawful enrichment, lawful trading, and disagreement over the option to rescind","html":"<p>The Real—majestic is His majesty—says: <strong>“O you who believe, do not consume one another’s property unlawfully”<\/strong> (<em>bi-l-bāṭil<\/em>, بالباطل): by any means the religious law does not permit, such as usury, gambling, violent appropriation, theft, betrayal of trust, divination, sorcery, and the like. <strong>“Except that there be trade by mutual consent among you”<\/strong>: that is, rather, when a valid commercial transaction occurs through mutual agreement on the sale. The Mālikīs draw from this the conclusion that a sale is constituted by the contract even before the two parties physically separate.<\/p><p>Al-Shāfiʿī says that the sale is completed only once the parties separate physically, relying on the Prophet’s saying, blessings and peace upon him: “The seller and buyer retain their option [to annul the contract] so long as they have not separated.” <a class=\"noteref\" href=\"#/hadith/H026\">[H026]<\/a> Mālik interprets “separation” as separation of speech [the ending of negotiation]. According to most exegetes, the “option” (<em>takhyīr<\/em>, تخيير) means each party formally offering the other a choice once the contract is concluded. ʿAmr b. Jarīr reportedly bought a horse and then gave his counterpart the choice after the sale, saying: “I heard Abū Hurayra say that trade must be by mutual consent.” Al-Bayḍāwī says: “Trade is singled out among the means by which another’s property may lawfully pass [to one] because it is the most common, and most fitting for people of dignity. It is also possible that ‘trade’ is intended to mean transfer [of property] in general.” Another interpretation takes the prohibition as forbidding spending wealth on what God does not approve, and “trade” as spending it on what He does approve. End of quotation. <a class=\"noteref\" href=\"#/note/N139\">[N139]<\/a><\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real—majestic is His majesty—says: “O you who believe, do not consume one another’s property unlawfully” ( bi-l-bāṭil , بالباطل): by any means the religious law does not permit, such as usury, gambling, violent appropriation, theft, betrayal of trust, divination, sorcery, and the like. “Except that there be trade by mutual consent among you” : that is, rather, when a valid commercial transaction occurs through mutual agreement on the sale. The Mālikīs draw from this the conclusion that a sale is constituted by the contract even before the two parties physically separate. Al-Shāfiʿī says that the sale is completed only once the parties separate physically, relying on the Prophet’s saying, blessings and peace upon him: “The seller and buyer retain their option [to annul the contract] so long as they have not separated.” [H026] Mālik interprets “separation” as separation of speech [the ending of negotiation]. According to most exegetes, the “option” ( takhyīr , تخيير) means each party formally offering the other a choice once the contract is concluded. ʿAmr b. Jarīr reportedly bought a horse and then gave his counterpart the choice after the sale, saying: “I heard Abū Hurayra say that trade must be by mutual consent.” Al-Bayḍāwī says: “Trade is singled out among the means by which another’s property may lawfully pass [to one] because it is the most common, and most fitting for people of dignity. It is also possible that ‘trade’ is intended to mean transfer [of property] in general.” Another interpretation takes the prohibition as forbidding spending wealth on what God does not approve, and “trade” as spending it on what He does approve. End of quotation. [N139]"},{"type":"spiritual","title":"the profitable exchange","html":"<p>Do not expend your wealth—or your spiritual states (<em>aḥwāl<\/em>, أحوال)—on anything that does not draw you nearer to the Real (<em>al-Ḥaqq<\/em>, الحق), for everything besides the Real is vain. As the poet said:<\/p><blockquote>\n<p>Truly everything apart from God is vain; and every delight will surely vanish.<\/p>\n<\/blockquote><p>There is an exception when the expenditure becomes profitable trade, bringing you near to the Beloved and drawing you into the Presence of the Near One: that is a profitable trade and a beneficial bargain. God knows best.<\/p>","authorial":true,"text":"Do not expend your wealth—or your spiritual states ( aḥwāl , أحوال)—on anything that does not draw you nearer to the Real ( al-Ḥaqq , الحق), for everything besides the Real is vain. As the poet said: Truly everything apart from God is vain; and every delight will surely vanish. There is an exception when the expenditure becomes profitable trade, bringing you near to the Beloved and drawing you into the Presence of the Near One: that is a profitable trade and a beneficial bargain. God knows best."},{"type":"transition","title":"","html":"<p class=\"transition-lead\">He then turns to one aspect of preserving bodily life, a topic completed later in the discussion of “It is not for a believer …”<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"He then turns to one aspect of preserving bodily life, a topic completed later in the discussion of “It is not for a believer …”"},{"type":"exposition","title":"The prohibition on killing oneself and the precedent of ʿAmr b. al-ʿĀṣ","html":"<p><strong>“Do not kill yourselves”<\/strong> by strangling, severing the spinal cord, or inflicting a wound that brings death; nor by throwing yourselves into destruction. ʿAmr b. al-ʿĀṣ, may God be pleased with him, reported: “The Messenger of God sent me on the expedition to Dhāt al-Salāsil. I became ritually impure on a cold night; fearing for my life [if I washed], I performed <em>tayammum<\/em> (التيمم) and led my companions in the dawn prayer. When I returned I mentioned this to the Messenger of God. He said, ‘ʿAmr, did you lead them in prayer while you were in a state of major ritual impurity?’ I said, ‘Yes, Messenger of God. I feared that washing would destroy me, and I remembered the saying of God: <strong>Do not kill yourselves. God has indeed been merciful to you.<\/strong>’ The Prophet laughed and said nothing.” <a class=\"noteref\" href=\"#/hadith/H027\">[H027]<\/a><\/p><p>Alternatively, <strong>“Do not kill yourselves”<\/strong> can mean: do not kill your brothers in Islam, for the believers are like one soul. Al-Bayḍāwī says that by joining the preservation of life and wealth, which is its companion because life depends on it, God enjoins the continued safety of both. End of quotation. It is only out of kindness and mercy that God has forbidden you to kill yourselves: <strong>“God has ever been Merciful to you.”<\/strong> He commanded the Children of Israel to kill themselves [in a particular account], whereas He forbade it to you.<\/p><p><strong>“Whoever does that”<\/strong>—meaning the killing, or all the prohibited acts just mentioned—<strong>“in aggression and wrongdoing”<\/strong>: that is, excessive transgression of a limit and doing what one has no right to do, or trespassing against another while wronging oneself by exposing oneself to punishment, <strong>“We shall soon expose him to a Fire”<\/strong>, that is, burn and roast him in it. <strong>“And that is easy for God.”<\/strong> The Prophet, blessings and peace upon him, is reported to have said: “Whoever kills himself by something will be punished by it in the Fire of Hell, remaining there forever.” <a class=\"noteref\" href=\"#/hadith/H028\">[H028]<\/a> This is a threat expressed with extraordinary severity, or, Ibn ʿAjība says, it applies to someone who declares the act lawful. The warning given here for killing oneself, he observes, is lighter than the warning later given for killing another human being, where wrath, the curse, and a tremendous punishment are added. As for Ibn ʿAṭiyya’s assertion that the exegetes unanimously agreed that this verse refers to killing one another, Ibn ʿAjība says that assertion is not correct. God knows best. <a class=\"noteref\" href=\"#/note/N140\">[N140]<\/a><\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"“Do not kill yourselves” by strangling, severing the spinal cord, or inflicting a wound that brings death; nor by throwing yourselves into destruction. ʿAmr b. al-ʿĀṣ, may God be pleased with him, reported: “The Messenger of God sent me on the expedition to Dhāt al-Salāsil. I became ritually impure on a cold night; fearing for my life [if I washed], I performed tayammum (التيمم) and led my companions in the dawn prayer. When I returned I mentioned this to the Messenger of God. He said, ‘ʿAmr, did you lead them in prayer while you were in a state of major ritual impurity?’ I said, ‘Yes, Messenger of God. I feared that washing would destroy me, and I remembered the saying of God: Do not kill yourselves. God has indeed been merciful to you. ’ The Prophet laughed and said nothing.” [H027] Alternatively, “Do not kill yourselves” can mean: do not kill your brothers in Islam, for the believers are like one soul. Al-Bayḍāwī says that by joining the preservation of life and wealth, which is its companion because life depends on it, God enjoins the continued safety of both. End of quotation. It is only out of kindness and mercy that God has forbidden you to kill yourselves: “God has ever been Merciful to you.” He commanded the Children of Israel to kill themselves [in a particular account], whereas He forbade it to you. “Whoever does that” —meaning the killing, or all the prohibited acts just mentioned— “in aggression and wrongdoing” : that is, excessive transgression of a limit and doing what one has no right to do, or trespassing against another while wronging oneself by exposing oneself to punishment, “We shall soon expose him to a Fire” , that is, burn and roast him in it. “And that is easy for God.” The Prophet, blessings and peace upon him, is reported to have said: “Whoever kills himself by something will be punished by it in the Fire of Hell, remaining there forever.” [H028] This is a threat expressed with extraordinary severity, or, Ibn ʿAjība says, it applies to someone who declares the act lawful. The warning given here for killing oneself, he observes, is lighter than the warning later given for killing another human being, where wrath, the curse, and a tremendous punishment are added. As for Ibn ʿAṭiyya’s assertion that the exegetes unanimously agreed that this verse refers to killing one another, Ibn ʿAjība says that assertion is not correct. God knows best. [N140]"},{"type":"spiritual","title":"preserving the soul’s real life","html":"<p>Do not kill your souls by following appetites and allowing heedlessness to accumulate over you, for these deprive the soul of true life. Al-Fuḍayl b. ʿIyāḍ, may God be pleased with him, said: “Do not be heedless of your soul’s portion; one who neglects his soul’s portion is as if he had killed it.” The soul’s portion is its purification (<em>tazkiya<\/em>, تزكية) and adornment with perfections, or its nourishment with certain knowledge (<em>ʿilm al-yaqīn<\/em>, علم اليقين), gnosis (<em>maʿrifa<\/em>, معرفة), and sound establishment (<em>tamkīn<\/em>, تمكين).<\/p><p>By “soul” here [the author] means spirit (<em>rūḥ<\/em>, روح). When Sufis say that the soul must be “killed,” they intend the commanding soul (<em>al-nafs al-ammāra<\/em>, النفس الأمارة). So long as the spirit remains darkened by sins and caprice it is called <em>nafs<\/em> (نفس); when purified and refined it is called <em>rūḥ<\/em> (روح). This is the sense intended here: the verse calls it <em>nafs<\/em> in view of what it had previously been. God knows best.<\/p>","authorial":true,"text":"Do not kill your souls by following appetites and allowing heedlessness to accumulate over you, for these deprive the soul of true life. Al-Fuḍayl b. ʿIyāḍ, may God be pleased with him, said: “Do not be heedless of your soul’s portion; one who neglects his soul’s portion is as if he had killed it.” The soul’s portion is its purification ( tazkiya , تزكية) and adornment with perfections, or its nourishment with certain knowledge ( ʿilm al-yaqīn , علم اليقين), gnosis ( maʿrifa , معرفة), and sound establishment ( tamkīn , تمكين). By “soul” here [the author] means spirit ( rūḥ , روح). When Sufis say that the soul must be “killed,” they intend the commanding soul ( al-nafs al-ammāra , النفس الأمارة). So long as the spirit remains darkened by sins and caprice it is called nafs (نفس); when purified and refined it is called rūḥ (روح). This is the sense intended here: the verse calls it nafs in view of what it had previously been. God knows best."},{"type":"transition","title":"","html":"<p class=\"transition-lead\">Killing oneself is one of the grave sins; avoidance of the grave sins brings forgiveness of lesser ones<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"Killing oneself is one of the grave sins; avoidance of the grave sins brings forgiveness of lesser ones"}],"cairo_notes":[{"id":"C493.1","page":493,"ar":"راجع التعليق على هذا البيت عند إشارة الآية [١٥٠] من سورة البقرة.","en":"See the note on this verse at the allusion on verse [150] of Sūrat al-Baqara.","anchor":"وكل نعيم لا محالة زائل"},{"id":"C494.1","page":494,"ar":"النخع: هو القتل الشديد، مشتق من قطع النخاع.","en":"Nakhʿ is a violent killing; the word derives from severing the spinal cord.","anchor":"بالنخع"}],"english_characters":6235,"arabic_characters":3960},{"id":"q4-31","q":"4:31","s":4,"surah":"Al-Nisāʾ","title":"4:31 — Major sins, lesser sins, and nearness to the Divine Presence","source":"Cairo Arabic edition I:495–496","cairo":"I:495–496","source_page_first":495,"source_page_last":496,"ar":"إِنْ تَجْتَنِبُوا كَبائِرَ مَا تُنْهَوْنَ عَنْهُ نُكَفِّرْ عَنْكُمْ سَيِّئاتِكُمْ وَنُدْخِلْكُمْ مُدْخَلاً كَرِيماً (31)\nقلت: المدخل- بالضم: مصدر، بمعنى الإدخال، وبالفتح: المكان، ويحتمل المصدر.\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله: إن تجتنبوا كبائر الذنوب التي تُنهون عنها نُكَفِّرْ عَنْكُمْ سَيِّئاتِكُمْ الصغائر وَنُدْخِلْكُمْ مُدْخَلًا كَرِيماً وهو الجنة، أو إدخالاً مصحوبًا بالكرامة والتعظيم، واختلف في الكبائر، هل تعرف بالعد أو بالحد؟ فقيل: سبع، وقيل: سبعون، وقيل: سبعمائة، وقيل: كل معصية فهى كبيرة. وعنه صلّى الله عليه وسلم أنه قال: «اجتَنِبُوا السَّبعَ المُوبِقَاتِ: الإشراك باللهِ، والسَّحرَ، وقَتلَ النَّفس بغير حقها، وأكل الرّبا، وأكلَ مَالِ اليَتِيمِ، والفرار مِن الزَّحفِ، ورَمي المُحصنَاتٍ الغَافِلاتِ المُؤمِنَاتِ» .\nقال ابن جزي: لا شك أن هذه مِنَ الكبائر لنص الشارع عليها، وزاد بعضهم عليها أشياء ورد النص عليها في الحديث أنها من الكبائر، منها عقوق الوالدين، وشهادة الزور، واليمين الغموس، والزنا، والسرقة، وشرب الخمر، والنهبة، والقنوط من رحمة الله، والأمن من مكر الله، ومنع ابن السبيل الماء، والإلحاد في البيت الحرام، والنميمة، وترك التحرّز من البول، والغلول، واستطالة الرجل في عرض أخيه، والجور في الحكم.\nوقيل في حدّها: كل جريمة تؤذن بقلة الدين ورقة الديانة، وقيل: ذنوب الظاهر صغائر، وذنوب الباطن كبائر.\nوقيل: كل ما فيه حق الغير فهو كبائر، وما كان بينك وبين الله تعالى صغائر، واحتج هذا بقوله- عليه الصلاة والسلام-: «يُنَادِي يومَ القيامة مناد من بُطنان العرش (١) : يا أمّة أحمد، أن الله تعالى يقول: أمَّا ما كان لي قِبلَكُمُ فقد وهبتهُ لكم، وبقيت التباعات، فتواهبوها، وادخلوا الجنة» .\nالإشارة: كل ما يُبعد العبد عن حضرة ربه فهو من أكبر الكبائر، فمن اجتنب ذلك واتقى كلَّ ما يشغله عن الله أدخله الله مدخلاً كريمًا، وهو حضرة الشهود والتلذذ برؤية المعبود، والترقي في أسرار الحبيب الودود. قال الورتجبي: قال أبو تراب: أمر الله باجتناب الكبائر، وهي الدعاوى الفاسدة، والإشارات الباطلة، وإطلاق اللفظ بغير الحقيقة. هـ.\nولما قسّم الله المواريث على ما تقدم، قال بعض النساء: ليتنا استوينا مع الرجال، أو يكون لنا سهمان لأننا أحوج منهم، فأنزل الله:","quran_ar":"إِنْ تَجْتَنِبُوا كَبائِرَ مَا تُنْهَوْنَ عَنْهُ نُكَفِّرْ عَنْكُمْ سَيِّئاتِكُمْ وَنُدْخِلْكُمْ مُدْخَلاً كَرِيماً\n﴿31﴾","quran_en":"If you avoid the major sins you are forbidden, We shall efface your lesser misdeeds and admit you through a noble entrance.","segments":[{"type":"language","title":"The two forms and senses of mudkhal","html":"<p>I say: <em>Mudkhal<\/em> (مدخل), with ḍamma on the mīm, is a verbal noun (<em>maṣdar<\/em>, مصدر) meaning “causing to enter” (<em>idkhāl<\/em>, إدخال); with fatḥa it denotes the place of entry, although it can also be a verbal noun.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"I say: Mudkhal (مدخل), with ḍamma on the mīm, is a verbal noun ( maṣdar , مصدر) meaning “causing to enter” ( idkhāl , إدخال); with fatḥa it denotes the place of entry, although it can also be a verbal noun."},{"type":"theology","title":"How the major sins are enumerated—or defined","html":"<p>The Real—majestic is His majesty—says: <strong>“If you avoid the grave sins of what you are forbidden”<\/strong>, the major sins (<em>kabāʾir al-dhunūb<\/em>, كبائر الذنوب), <strong>“We shall efface your evil deeds”<\/strong>, that is, your minor sins, <strong>“and admit you through a noble entrance”<\/strong>: into the Garden, or through an act of admission attended by honor and magnification. There is disagreement about grave sins: are they known by enumerating (<em>ʿadd<\/em>, عد) them or by defining their limit (<em>ḥadd<\/em>, حد)? Some say seven, others seventy, others seven hundred; others again say that every act of disobedience is grave.<\/p><p>The Prophet, blessings and peace upon him, said: <strong>“Avoid the seven destructive sins”<\/strong> (<em>al-sabʿ al-mūbiqāt<\/em>, السبع الموبقات): associating others with God, sorcery, killing a life without right, taking usury, consuming an orphan’s wealth, fleeing the advance of battle, and slandering chaste, unsuspecting believing women. <a class=\"noteref\" href=\"#/hadith/H029\">[H029]<\/a><\/p><p>Ibn Juzayy says: “These are undoubtedly among the grave sins, for the Lawgiver expressly named them. Others add matters explicitly called grave sins in hadith: disobedience to parents, false witness, the oath that plunges one into wrongdoing (<em>al-yamīn al-ghamūs<\/em>, اليمين الغموس), fornication, theft, drinking wine, plunder, despair of God’s mercy, feeling secure from God’s plan, refusing a traveler access to water, profaning the Sacred House, tale-bearing, failing to safeguard oneself from urine, embezzlement of communal spoils (<em>ghulūl<\/em>, غلول), violating one’s brother’s reputation, and ruling unjustly.”<\/p><p>Another definition is any offence indicating diminished religious seriousness and weakened religious commitment. Another says outward sins are minor and inward sins are grave. Another says every offence involving another person’s right is grave, while what remains between a servant and God is minor. Its proponents cite a report attributed to the Prophet: “On the Day of Resurrection a caller from within the Throne will proclaim: ‘Community of Aḥmad! God says: As for the claims I had against you, I have granted them to you; claims belonging to others remain, so grant them freely to one another, and enter the Garden.’” [Translator’s note: This report is weak and is presented as an argument cited by a particular group, not an authenticated foundation for cancelling the rights of injured persons.] <a class=\"noteref\" href=\"#/hadith/H030\">[H030]<\/a> <a class=\"noteref\" href=\"#/note/N141\">[N141]<\/a><\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real—majestic is His majesty—says: “If you avoid the grave sins of what you are forbidden” , the major sins ( kabāʾir al-dhunūb , كبائر الذنوب), “We shall efface your evil deeds” , that is, your minor sins, “and admit you through a noble entrance” : into the Garden, or through an act of admission attended by honor and magnification. There is disagreement about grave sins: are they known by enumerating ( ʿadd , عد) them or by defining their limit ( ḥadd , حد)? Some say seven, others seventy, others seven hundred; others again say that every act of disobedience is grave. The Prophet, blessings and peace upon him, said: “Avoid the seven destructive sins” ( al-sabʿ al-mūbiqāt , السبع الموبقات): associating others with God, sorcery, killing a life without right, taking usury, consuming an orphan’s wealth, fleeing the advance of battle, and slandering chaste, unsuspecting believing women. [H029] Ibn Juzayy says: “These are undoubtedly among the grave sins, for the Lawgiver expressly named them. Others add matters explicitly called grave sins in hadith: disobedience to parents, false witness, the oath that plunges one into wrongdoing ( al-yamīn al-ghamūs , اليمين الغموس), fornication, theft, drinking wine, plunder, despair of God’s mercy, feeling secure from God’s plan, refusing a traveler access to water, profaning the Sacred House, tale-bearing, failing to safeguard oneself from urine, embezzlement of communal spoils ( ghulūl , غلول), violating one’s brother’s reputation, and ruling unjustly.” Another definition is any offence indicating diminished religious seriousness and weakened religious commitment. Another says outward sins are minor and inward sins are grave. Another says every offence involving another person’s right is grave, while what remains between a servant and God is minor. Its proponents cite a report attributed to the Prophet: “On the Day of Resurrection a caller from within the Throne will proclaim: ‘Community of Aḥmad! God says: As for the claims I had against you, I have granted them to you; claims belonging to others remain, so grant them freely to one another, and enter the Garden.’” [Translator’s note: This report is weak and is presented as an argument cited by a particular group, not an authenticated foundation for cancelling the rights of injured persons.] [H030] [N141]"},{"type":"spiritual","title":"the greatest sin is whatever bars the Presence","html":"<p>Everything that drives the servant away from his Lord’s Presence is among the greatest grave sins. Whoever avoids it and guards against everything that distracts him from God is admitted by God through a noble entrance: the Presence of witnessing (<em>ḥaḍrat al-shuhūd<\/em>, حضرة الشهود), delight in beholding the Worshipped One, and advancing in the secrets of the Beloved, the All-Loving. Al-Wartajbī says: “Abū Turāb said: God commanded the avoidance of grave sins, and these are corrupt pretensions, false allusions, and the utterance of words without their reality.” End of quotation.<\/p>","authorial":true,"text":"Everything that drives the servant away from his Lord’s Presence is among the greatest grave sins. Whoever avoids it and guards against everything that distracts him from God is admitted by God through a noble entrance: the Presence of witnessing ( ḥaḍrat al-shuhūd , حضرة الشهود), delight in beholding the Worshipped One, and advancing in the secrets of the Beloved, the All-Loving. Al-Wartajbī says: “Abū Turāb said: God commanded the avoidance of grave sins, and these are corrupt pretensions, false allusions, and the utterance of words without their reality.” End of quotation."},{"type":"transition","title":"","html":"<p class=\"transition-lead\">After God divided inheritances, some women reportedly wished to have the same portions as men, or two portions because of their greater need. At this point Ibn ʿAjība introduces the following verse, Q 4:32. <\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"After God divided inheritances, some women reportedly wished to have the same portions as men, or two portions because of their greater need. At this point Ibn ʿAjība introduces the following verse, Q 4:32."}],"cairo_notes":[{"id":"C495.1","page":495,"ar":"بطنان العرش: أي من وسطه، وقيل: البطنان جمع بطن. يريد من دواخل العرش. انظر النهاية.","en":"Buṭnān al-ʿarsh means from its midst. Alternatively, buṭnān is the plural of baṭn: it means from the interior parts of the Throne. See al-Nihāya.","anchor":"من بُطنان العرش"}],"english_characters":3329,"arabic_characters":1930},{"id":"q4_32","q":"4:32","s":4,"surah":"Al-Nisāʾ","title":"4:32 · Allotted gifts, work, and the stations of the friends of God","source":"Cairo I:496–497","cairo":"I:496–497","source_page_first":496,"source_page_last":497,"ar":"وَلا تَتَمَنَّوْا مَا فَضَّلَ اللَّهُ بِهِ بَعْضَكُمْ عَلى بَعْضٍ لِلرِّجالِ نَصِيبٌ مِمَّا اكْتَسَبُوا وَلِلنِّساءِ نَصِيبٌ مِمَّا اكْتَسَبْنَ وَسْئَلُوا اللَّهَ مِنْ فَضْلِهِ إِنَّ اللَّهَ كانَ بِكُلِّ شَيْءٍ عَلِيماً (32)\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله: وَلا تَتَمَنَّوْا مَا فَضَّلَ اللَّهُ بِهِ من الميراث (١) بَعْضَكُمْ عَلى بَعْضٍ، كتضعيف الذكر على الأنثى، فللرجال نَصِيبٌ مِمَّا اكْتَسَبُوا أي: مما أصابوا وأحرزوا في القسمة، وَلِلنِّساءِ نَصِيبٌ مِمَّا اكْتَسَبْنَ منه، قلَّ أو كثر، فلتقنع بما قسم الله لها، ولا تعترض على أحكام الشريعة، ولكن سْئَلُوا اللَّهَ مِنْ فَضْلِهِ يُعطكم من غير الميراث، هكذا فسرها ابن عباس.\nوقال مجاهد: قالت أُم سلمة: يغزو الرجال ولا نغزو، فليتنا رجال نغزو، ونبلغ ما يبلغ الرجال. فنزلت. فيكون المعنى: وَلاَ تَتَمَنَّوْاْ مَا فَضَّلَ الله به الرجال على النساء كالغزو وغيره، فللرجال نصيب مما اكتسبوا من ثواب الجهاد وسائر أعمالهم، (وللنساء نصيب مما اكتسبن) من طاعة أزواجهن وحفظ فروجهن وسائر بقية أعمالهن.\nوالتحقيق أنها عامة في جميع المراتب الدينية والدنيوية لأن ذلك ذريعة إلى التحاسد والتعادي، ومعربة عن عدم الرضا بما قسم الله له، وإلى التشهي لحصول الشيء له من غير طلب، وهو مذموم لأن تمنّي ما لم يقدر له، معارضة لحِكمة القدر، وتمنّي ما قدر له بكسب، بطالة وتضييع حظ، وتمنّي ما قدر له بغير كسب، ضياع ومحال، قاله البيضاوي. فللرجال نصيب من أجل ما اكتسبوا من الأعمال، وتحملوا من المشاق، فيعطيهم الله على قدر ما اكتسبوا وَلِلنِّساءِ نَصِيبٌ مِمَّا اكْتَسَبْنَ كذلك، فلا فائدة في تمني ما للناس، ولكن (اسألوا الله من فضله) يُعطكم مثله، أو أكثر من خزائنه التي لا تنفد. إِنَّ اللَّهَ كانَ بِكُلِّ شَيْءٍ عَلِيماً وهو يعلم ما يستحقه\nكل إنسان، فيُفَضِّل من شاء بما شاء عن علم وبيان، ومناسبة الآية حينئِذ لما قبلها: أن تجنب الكبائر فضل من الله ونعمة، وهو أفضل ممن يقع فيها، لكن لا ينبغي تمني ذلك من غير عمل، ولكن يسأل الله من فضلِهِ حتى يُلحقه بأهل العصمة. وبالله التوفيق.\nالإشارة: قد وقع التفضيل في مقامات الأولياء كالأنبياء، لكن لا ينبغي تعيين الفاضل من المفضول، لِما يؤدي إليه من التنقيص فيؤدي إلى الغيبة، والتفضيل يقع بزيادة اليقين وصحة التمكين، والترقي في أنوار التوحيد وأسرار التفريد. ويكون أيضًا بهداية الخلق على يده، وظهور إحسانه ورفده، فإذا رأى العبدُ أنه لم يبلغ إلى مقام غيره فلا يتمنى ذلك المقام بعينه، فقد يكون مقامه عند الله في علمه أعظم، وقد يكون أدون، فيُسيء الأدب، فالخير كله في العبودية والرضى بأحكام الربوبية، فللأقوياء نصيب مما اكتسبوا بالقوة والمجاهدة التي خلق الله فيهم، حكمةً وفضلاً، وللضعفاء نصيب مما اكتسبوا قسمة وعدلاً، ولكن يسأل الله من فضله العظيم، فإن الله بكل شيء عليم، فقد يُعطى بلا سبب ويُبَلِّغ بلا تعب.\nوفي الحديث عنه صلّى الله عليه وسلم أنه قال: «سَلُوا الله مِن فَضلِهِ فَإنَّ اللهَ يُحِبُ أن يُسألَ» . وفي حديث آخر: «مَن لَم يَسألِ اللهَ يَغضَب عَلَيهِ» . وقال الورتجبي: أمر بالسؤال ونهى عن التمني لأن السؤال افتقار، والتمني، اختيار. هـ. والله تعالى أعلم.\nثم تكلم على ميراث الحليف على ما كان في أول الإسلام، فقال:","quran_ar":"وَلا تَتَمَنَّوْا مَا فَضَّلَ اللَّهُ بِهِ بَعْضَكُمْ عَلى بَعْضٍ لِلرِّجالِ نَصِيبٌ مِمَّا اكْتَسَبُوا وَلِلنِّساءِ نَصِيبٌ مِمَّا اكْتَسَبْنَ وَسْئَلُوا اللَّهَ مِنْ فَضْلِهِ إِنَّ اللَّهَ كانَ بِكُلِّ شَيْءٍ عَلِيماً (32)","quran_en":"Do not long for what God has given some of you in preference to others. Men have a share of what they have earned, and women have a share of what they have earned. Ask God of His bounty: God has knowledge of everything.","segments":[{"type":"exposition","title":"Inheritance and the limits of comparison","html":"<p>The Real, majestic is His glory, says: Do not wish for what God has bestowed upon some of you beyond others in inheritance, such as the male receiving twice the female’s share. “Men have a share of what they have earned” means what they have received and taken possession of in the division; women too have a share of what they receive from it, whether small or great. Let each be content with what God has allotted to her rather than objecting to the rulings of the Sacred Law. Instead, “ask God of His bounty”: He may grant you something other than inheritance. Ibn ʿAbbās explained the verse in this way.<\/p>\n","authorial":false,"text":"The Real, majestic is His glory, says: Do not wish for what God has bestowed upon some of you beyond others in inheritance, such as the male receiving twice the female’s share. “Men have a share of what they have earned” means what they have received and taken possession of in the division; women too have a share of what they receive from it, whether small or great. Let each be content with what God has allotted to her rather than objecting to the rulings of the Sacred Law. Instead, “ask God of His bounty”: He may grant you something other than inheritance. Ibn ʿAbbās explained the verse in this way."},{"type":"exposition","title":"The reported circumstances of revelation","html":"<p>Mujāhid, however, related that Umm Salama said: “Men go out on military expeditions while we do not. If only we were men, so that we could fight and attain what the men attain.” Then the verse was sent down. On this reading, its meaning is: Do not desire the advantages God has assigned to men over women, such as participation in battle and similar matters. Men have the reward of their military striving and other works, and women have the reward of obedience to their husbands, guarding their chastity, and the rest of their works.<\/p>\n","authorial":false,"text":"Mujāhid, however, related that Umm Salama said: “Men go out on military expeditions while we do not. If only we were men, so that we could fight and attain what the men attain.” Then the verse was sent down. On this reading, its meaning is: Do not desire the advantages God has assigned to men over women, such as participation in battle and similar matters. Men have the reward of their military striving and other works, and women have the reward of obedience to their husbands, guarding their chastity, and the rest of their works."},{"type":"exposition","title":"A general teaching about all worldly and religious ranks","html":"<p>The more exact determination (التحقيق) is that the verse is general, embracing every rank in religion and worldly affairs. Wishing for another’s allotted rank can lead to envy and hostility; it betrays dissatisfaction with what God has apportioned and a desire to obtain something without seeking its proper means. That is blameworthy. To wish for what has not been decreed for oneself is to challenge the wisdom of the decree (حكمة القدر). To wish for what has been decreed through work while neglecting the work is idleness and the squandering of one’s opportunity. To wish for what has been decreed independently of work is a futile loss of effort. Al-Bayḍāwī makes this observation.<\/p>\n<p>Thus men obtain a portion on account of the deeds they perform and the hardships they undergo, and God grants them according to what they earn; women likewise have a portion according to their deeds. There is no benefit in coveting what belongs to others. Ask God of His inexhaustible bounty, and He can give you the like of it or more. “God has knowledge of everything”: He knows what each person merits and favours whom He wills with what He wills, in knowledge and discernment.<\/p>\n<p>The verse is connected to the preceding discussion as follows: abstaining from grave sins is itself God’s grace and favour, and those who avoid them are better off than those who fall into them. One should not merely wish for such a condition without acting; one should ask God of His bounty that He may join one to those He preserves. And through God comes success.<\/p>\n","authorial":false,"text":"The more exact determination (التحقيق) is that the verse is general, embracing every rank in religion and worldly affairs. Wishing for another’s allotted rank can lead to envy and hostility; it betrays dissatisfaction with what God has apportioned and a desire to obtain something without seeking its proper means. That is blameworthy. To wish for what has not been decreed for oneself is to challenge the wisdom of the decree (حكمة القدر). To wish for what has been decreed through work while neglecting the work is idleness and the squandering of one’s opportunity. To wish for what has been decreed independently of work is a futile loss of effort. Al-Bayḍāwī makes this observation. Thus men obtain a portion on account of the deeds they perform and the hardships they undergo, and God grants them according to what they earn; women likewise have a portion according to their deeds. There is no benefit in coveting what belongs to others. Ask God of His inexhaustible bounty, and He can give you the like of it or more. “God has knowledge of everything”: He knows what each person merits and favours whom He wills with what He wills, in knowledge and discernment. The verse is connected to the preceding discussion as follows: abstaining from grave sins is itself God’s grace and favour, and those who avoid them are better off than those who fall into them. One should not merely wish for such a condition without acting; one should ask God of His bounty that He may join one to those He preserves. And through God comes success."},{"type":"spiritual","title":"Different degrees of sainthood without disparagement","html":"<p>Degrees of excellence exist among the stations of God’s friends, as they do among the prophets. Yet one should not insist on ranking this saint above that one: the resulting disparagement can lead to backbiting (الغيبة). Distinction may arise through an increase in certainty (اليقين), the soundness of established realization (التمكين), progression through the lights of divine oneness (أنوار التوحيد) and the mysteries of singularization (أسرار التفريد), through guiding others by one’s hand, or through manifest generosity and benefit.<\/p>\n<p>When someone observes that he has not attained the station of another, he should not wish for that precise station. His own degree may be greater in God’s knowledge, or less; such wishing may therefore violate proper comportment (الأدب). The entire good lies in servanthood (العبودية) and contentment with the ordinances of Lordship (الربوبية). The strong have a share of what they have gained through the strength and spiritual struggle (المجاهدة) God created in them as wisdom and grace; the weak have a share of what they have gained by divine allotment and justice. Let all ask of God’s vast bounty, for He knows every thing. He may bestow without apparent means and bring a person to arrival without toil.<\/p>\n","authorial":true,"text":"Degrees of excellence exist among the stations of God’s friends, as they do among the prophets. Yet one should not insist on ranking this saint above that one: the resulting disparagement can lead to backbiting (الغيبة). Distinction may arise through an increase in certainty (اليقين), the soundness of established realization (التمكين), progression through the lights of divine oneness (أنوار التوحيد) and the mysteries of singularization (أسرار التفريد), through guiding others by one’s hand, or through manifest generosity and benefit. When someone observes that he has not attained the station of another, he should not wish for that precise station. His own degree may be greater in God’s knowledge, or less; such wishing may therefore violate proper comportment (الأدب). The entire good lies in servanthood (العبودية) and contentment with the ordinances of Lordship (الربوبية). The strong have a share of what they have gained through the strength and spiritual struggle (المجاهدة) God created in them as wisdom and grace; the weak have a share of what they have gained by divine allotment and justice. Let all ask of God’s vast bounty, for He knows every thing. He may bestow without apparent means and bring a person to arrival without toil."},{"type":"spiritual","title":"Asking, rather than choosing one’s portion","html":"<p>A report attributed to the Prophet, blessings and peace upon him, says: “Ask God of His bounty, for God loves to be asked.” <a class=\"noteref\" href=\"#/hadith/H032\">[H032]<\/a> Another states: “Whoever does not ask God, He becomes angry with him.” <a class=\"noteref\" href=\"#/hadith/H033\">[H033]<\/a> Al-Wartajbī says: “He commands asking and forbids wishing, because asking is confession of poverty (افتقار), while wishing is an act of choosing (اختيار).” End of quotation. God knows best.<\/p>\n","authorial":true,"text":"A report attributed to the Prophet, blessings and peace upon him, says: “Ask God of His bounty, for God loves to be asked.” [H032] Another states: “Whoever does not ask God, He becomes angry with him.” [H033] Al-Wartajbī says: “He commands asking and forbids wishing, because asking is confession of poverty (افتقار), while wishing is an act of choosing (اختيار).” End of quotation. God knows best."},{"type":"transition","title":"","html":"<p>He then spoke of the inheritance of an ally, as this had existed at the beginning of Islam.<\/p>\n","authorial":false,"text":"He then spoke of the inheritance of an ally, as this had existed at the beginning of Islam."}],"cairo_notes":[{"id":"C496.1","page":496,"ar":"سيذكر الشيخ بعد أن الآية عامة.","en":"The shaykh will state below that the verse is general.","anchor":"من الميراث"}],"english_characters":4413,"arabic_characters":2757},{"id":"q4_33","q":"4:33","s":4,"surah":"Al-Nisāʾ","title":"4:33 · Heirs, contractual alliances, and the inheritance of spiritual guidance","source":"Cairo I:497–498","cairo":"I:497–498","source_page_first":497,"source_page_last":498,"ar":"وَلِكُلٍّ جَعَلْنا مَوالِيَ مِمَّا تَرَكَ الْوالِدانِ وَالْأَقْرَبُونَ وَالَّذِينَ عَقَدَتْ أَيْمانُكُمْ فَآتُوهُمْ نَصِيبَهُمْ إِنَّ اللَّهَ كانَ عَلى كُلِّ شَيْءٍ شَهِيداً (33)\nقلت: التنوين في «كل» : للعوض، و «مما ترك» بيان للمعوض منه، أي: ولكل مال مما ترك الوالدان والأقربون جعلنا موالي، أي: ورثة، وهم الذرية والعصبة يرثون من ذلك المال، والوالدان على هذا فاعل، ويحتمل أن يكون مبتدأ والتنوين عوض عن الميت الموروث، أي: ولكل ميت جعلنا ورثة يرثون مما ترك ذلك الميت، وهم الوالدان والأقربون فيوقف على (ترك) ، و (مما) يتعلق بمحذوف، و (الذين) مبتدأ، و (فآتوهم) خبر، دخلت الفاء لما في المبتدأ من العموم.\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله: ولكل ميت جعلنا ورثة يرثون مِمَّا تَرَكَ ذلك الميت، وهم الْوالِدانِ وَالْأَقْرَبُونَ، أو لكل تركة جعلنا لها مَوالِيَ أي: ورثة يرثون مما ترك الوالدان والأقربون، وَالَّذِينَ عَقَدَتْ أَيْمانُكُمْ وهم موالي الحِلف، كانوا يتحالفون في الجاهلية على النصرة والمؤازرة، يقول الرجل لآخر: دمي دمك،\nوهدمي هدمك، وثأري ثأرك. فيضرب بعضهم على يد الآخر في عقد ذلك الحلف. فلذلك قال: عَقَدَتْ أَيْمانُكُمْ فكان في أول الإسلام يرث من حليفه السدس، وإليه أشار بقوله: فَآتُوهُمْ نَصِيبَهُمْ، ثم نسخ.\nوقيل: نزلت في المؤاخاة التي آخى رسول الله صلى الله عليه وسلم بين المهاجرين والأنصار، فكان يرث السدس، ثم نُسخ بقوله: وَأُولُوا الْأَرْحامِ بَعْضُهُمْ أَوْلى بِبَعْضٍ فِي كِتابِ اللَّهِ. وعن أبي حنيفة: لو أسلم رجل على يد رجل وتعاقدا على أن يتعاقلا ويتوارثا صح وورث. وقال ابن عباس: آتوهم نصيبهم من النصرة التي تعاقدوا عليها، فيُوفي لهم بها، فلا نسخ.\nإِنَّ اللَّهَ كانَ عَلى كُلِّ شَيْءٍ شَهِيداً، هو تهديد لمن تعدى الحدود، ونقض العهود. والله تعالى أعلم.\nالإشارة: ولكل زمان جعلنا أولياء كبراء، يرثون مما ترك أشياخهم من خصوصية الولاية وسر العناية، إلى يوم القيامة فالأرض لا تخلو ممن يقوم بالحجة ويظهر المحجة، فيقال لهم: والذين عقدت أيمانكم في الصحبة معكم، فظهر صدقهم، وبانت خدمتهم، فآتوهم نصيبهم مما خصكم الله به من سر الولاية ولطف العناية، (إِن الله كان على كل شيء شهيدًا) ، لا يخفى عليه مَن يستحق الخلافة ويرث سرَ الولاية. والله تعالى أعلم.\nثم بيّن حكمة تفضيل الرجال على النساء فى المواريث وغيرها، فقال:","quran_ar":"وَلِكُلٍّ جَعَلْنا مَوالِيَ مِمَّا تَرَكَ الْوالِدانِ وَالْأَقْرَبُونَ وَالَّذِينَ عَقَدَتْ أَيْمانُكُمْ فَآتُوهُمْ نَصِيبَهُمْ إِنَّ اللَّهَ كانَ عَلى كُلِّ شَيْءٍ شَهِيداً (33)","quran_en":"For each [estate] We have appointed heirs from what parents and near relatives leave. And those with whom you have bound your right hands by pledge: give them their due portion. God is witness over everything.","segments":[{"type":"language","title":"The substitution in kull, and two readings of the syntax","html":"<p>I say: The tanwīn (التنوين) of “each” (كُلٍّ) stands in place of an omitted term (للعوض); “of what was left” (مِمَّا تَرَكَ) explains what that omitted term represents. Thus: “For every property left by parents and relatives, We appointed heirs (مَوَالِي),” namely children and agnatic relatives (العصبة), who inherit from that property. On this analysis “the parents” (الوالدان) is the subject (فاعل) of “left.”<\/p>\n<p>Alternatively, “the parents” (الوالدان) may be construed as the subject of a nominal sentence (مبتدأ), while the tanwīn stands in for “the deceased person”: “For every deceased person We appointed heirs who inherit what that deceased person leaves”; those heirs are the parents and near relatives. In that case one pauses after “left” (ترك), and “from what” (مما) depends upon an omitted expression (محذوف). “Those with whom your right hands have contracted” (الذين عقدت أيمانكم) is a subject (مبتدأ), and “give them their share” (فآتوهم نصيبهم) its predicate (خبر). The fāʾ (الفاء) enters the predicate because of the generality expressed by the subject.<\/p>\n","authorial":false,"text":"I say: The tanwīn (التنوين) of “each” (كُلٍّ) stands in place of an omitted term (للعوض); “of what was left” (مِمَّا تَرَكَ) explains what that omitted term represents. Thus: “For every property left by parents and relatives, We appointed heirs (مَوَالِي),” namely children and agnatic relatives (العصبة), who inherit from that property. On this analysis “the parents” (الوالدان) is the subject (فاعل) of “left.” Alternatively, “the parents” (الوالدان) may be construed as the subject of a nominal sentence (مبتدأ), while the tanwīn stands in for “the deceased person”: “For every deceased person We appointed heirs who inherit what that deceased person leaves”; those heirs are the parents and near relatives. In that case one pauses after “left” (ترك), and “from what” (مما) depends upon an omitted expression (محذوف). “Those with whom your right hands have contracted” (الذين عقدت أيمانكم) is a subject (مبتدأ), and “give them their share” (فآتوهم نصيبهم) its predicate (خبر). The fāʾ (الفاء) enters the predicate because of the generality expressed by the subject."},{"type":"legal","title":"Allies, the first inheritance rule, and its reported replacement","html":"<p>The Real, majestic is His glory, says: For every deceased person We have appointed inheritors from what that deceased person leaves, namely parents and near relatives. Or: every estate has its own heirs, who inherit what parents and kin leave.<\/p>\n<p>“As for those with whom your right hands have contracted” (الذين عقدت أيمانكم), these are the allies joined by a pact (موالي الحلف). In the Age of Ignorance they formed compacts of mutual protection and assistance. A man might say to another, “My blood is your blood, my injury your injury, and my vengeance your vengeance.” They would then clasp or strike one another’s hands in concluding the pact; hence the reference to what their “right hands have contracted.” At the beginning of Islam an ally inherited a sixth from his fellow ally; “give them their share” referred to that provision, which was later abrogated (نُسخ).<\/p>\n","authorial":false,"text":"The Real, majestic is His glory, says: For every deceased person We have appointed inheritors from what that deceased person leaves, namely parents and near relatives. Or: every estate has its own heirs, who inherit what parents and kin leave. “As for those with whom your right hands have contracted” (الذين عقدت أيمانكم), these are the allies joined by a pact (موالي الحلف). In the Age of Ignorance they formed compacts of mutual protection and assistance. A man might say to another, “My blood is your blood, my injury your injury, and my vengeance your vengeance.” They would then clasp or strike one another’s hands in concluding the pact; hence the reference to what their “right hands have contracted.” At the beginning of Islam an ally inherited a sixth from his fellow ally; “give them their share” referred to that provision, which was later abrogated (نُسخ)."},{"type":"legal","title":"Brotherhood between emigrants and helpers; a contrary account","html":"<p>It is also said that the verse came concerning the brotherhood (المؤاخاة) established by the Messenger of God between the Emigrants (المهاجرين) and Helpers (الأنصار): one would inherit a sixth through that bond, until this was abrogated by “Relatives are nearer to one another in God’s Book” [Q 8:75]. According to a report from Abū Ḥanīfa, if a man became Muslim at another man’s hand and the two contracted to bear indemnity and inherit from one another, this was valid and they inherited. Ibn ʿAbbās, however, explained “give them their share” as referring to the support due under their mutual undertaking, which must still be fulfilled; on this interpretation there is no abrogation. The exegetical report of Ibn ʿAbbās has a close transmitted parallel in al-Bukhārī <a class=\"noteref\" href=\"#/hadith/H034\">[H034]<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p>“God is witness over everything” warns against transgressing prescribed bounds or breaking undertakings. God knows best.<\/p>\n","authorial":false,"text":"It is also said that the verse came concerning the brotherhood (المؤاخاة) established by the Messenger of God between the Emigrants (المهاجرين) and Helpers (الأنصار): one would inherit a sixth through that bond, until this was abrogated by “Relatives are nearer to one another in God’s Book” [Q 8:75]. According to a report from Abū Ḥanīfa, if a man became Muslim at another man’s hand and the two contracted to bear indemnity and inherit from one another, this was valid and they inherited. Ibn ʿAbbās, however, explained “give them their share” as referring to the support due under their mutual undertaking, which must still be fulfilled; on this interpretation there is no abrogation. The exegetical report of Ibn ʿAbbās has a close transmitted parallel in al-Bukhārī [H034] . “God is witness over everything” warns against transgressing prescribed bounds or breaking undertakings. God knows best."},{"type":"spiritual","title":"The inheritance of walāya and the trust placed in disciples","html":"<p>In every age We have appointed great friends of God who inherit from their teachers the special grace of sainthood (خصوصية الولاية) and the mystery of divine solicitude (سر العناية) until the Resurrection. The earth is never without someone who establishes the proof and discloses the clear way. To those who have bound themselves to you through companionship (الصحبة), whose sincerity and service have become manifest, give a share of the mystery of sainthood and the kindness of divine solicitude that God has specially granted you. “God is witness over everything”: He knows who merits succession (الخلافة) and inherits the mystery of sainthood. God knows best.<\/p>\n","authorial":true,"text":"In every age We have appointed great friends of God who inherit from their teachers the special grace of sainthood (خصوصية الولاية) and the mystery of divine solicitude (سر العناية) until the Resurrection. The earth is never without someone who establishes the proof and discloses the clear way. To those who have bound themselves to you through companionship (الصحبة), whose sincerity and service have become manifest, give a share of the mystery of sainthood and the kindness of divine solicitude that God has specially granted you. “God is witness over everything”: He knows who merits succession (الخلافة) and inherits the mystery of sainthood. God knows best."},{"type":"transition","title":"","html":"<p>He then explains the rationale given for the preference of men over women in inheritance and other matters.<\/p>\n","authorial":false,"text":"He then explains the rationale given for the preference of men over women in inheritance and other matters."}],"cairo_notes":[],"english_characters":3609,"arabic_characters":1995},{"id":"q4_34","q":"4:34","s":4,"surah":"Al-Nisāʾ","title":"4:34 · Marital responsibility, contested disciplinary reports, and spiritual mastery of the self","source":"Cairo I:498–500","cairo":"I:498–500","source_page_first":498,"source_page_last":500,"ar":"الرِّجالُ قَوَّامُونَ عَلَى النِّساءِ بِما فَضَّلَ اللَّهُ بَعْضَهُمْ عَلى بَعْضٍ وَبِما أَنْفَقُوا مِنْ أَمْوالِهِمْ فَالصَّالِحاتُ قانِتاتٌ حافِظاتٌ لِلْغَيْبِ بِما حَفِظَ اللَّهُ وَاللاَّتِي تَخافُونَ نُشُوزَهُنَّ فَعِظُوهُنَّ وَاهْجُرُوهُنَّ فِي الْمَضاجِعِ وَاضْرِبُوهُنَّ فَإِنْ أَطَعْنَكُمْ فَلا تَبْغُوا عَلَيْهِنَّ سَبِيلاً إِنَّ اللَّهَ كانَ عَلِيًّا كَبِيراً (34)\nقلت: (فالصالحات) مبتدأ، وما بعده إخبار عنه، وأتى بالفاء المؤذنة بالسببية والتفريع، وكأنه تعالى يقول:\nالرجال قَوَّامُونَ عَلَى النسآء، فمن كانت صالحة قام عليها بما تستحقه من حسن المعاشرة، ومن كانت ناشزة عاملها بما تستحقه من الوعظ وغيره. وكل ما هنا من لفظ (ما) فهي مصدرية. إلا ما قرأ به أبو جعفر: [بما حفظ الله] ، بالنصب، فهي عنده موصولة اسمية، أي: بالأمر الذي حفظ الله وهو طاعتها لله فحفظها بذلك، وقيل إنها مصدرية. انظر الثعلبي.\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله: الرِّجالُ قَوَّامُونَ عَلَى النِّساءِ أي: قائمون عليهن قيام الولاة على الرعية، في التأديب والإنفاق والتعليم، ذلك لأمرين: أحدهما وهبي، والآخر كسبي فالوهبي: هو تفضيل الله لهم على\nالنساء بكمال العقل وحسن التدبير ومزيد القوة في الأعمال والطاعات، ولذلك خُصوا بالنبوة، والإمامة، والولاية، وإقامة الشعائر، والشهادة، في مجامع القضايا، ووجوب الجهاد والجمعة ونحوهما، والتعصيب، وزيادة السهم في الميراث، والاستبداد بالطلاق. والكسبي هو: (بما أنفقوا من أموالهم) في مهورهن، ونفقتهن، وكسوتهن.\nفيجب على الزوج أن يقوم بالعدل في أمر نسائه، فالمرأة الصالحة القانتة، أي: المطيعة لزوجها ولله تعالى، الحافظة للغيب، أي: لما غاب عن زوجها من مال بيته وفرجها وسر زوجها، حفظت ذلك بحفظ الله، أي: بما جعل الله فيها من الأمانة والحفظ، وبما ربط على قلبها من الديانة، أو بحفظها حق الله، فلما حفظت حقوق الله حفظها الله بعصمته، لقوله- عليه الصلاة والسلام-: «احفَظَ اللهَ يَحفَظكَ» . فمن كانت على هذا الوصف من النساء فيجب على الزوج حُسن القيام بها، ومقابلتها في القيام بما قابلته من الإحسان، وعنه صلّى الله عليه وسلم أنه قال: «خير النساء امرأةٌ إن نَظَرتَ إلَيهَا سَرَّتكَ، وإن أمرتها أطَاعَتكَ، وإن غبت عنها حفظتك في مَالِها ونَفسِهَا» . وتلا هذه الآية.\nوأما النساء التي تَخافُونَ أي: تتيقنون نُشُوزَهُنَّ أي: ترفعهن عن طاعة أزواجهن وعصيانهن، فَعِظُوهُنَّ بالقول، فإن لم ينفع فاهجروهن في المضاجع، أي: لا تدخلوا معهن في لحاف، أو لا تجامعوهن، فإن لم ينفع فاضربوهن ضربًا غير مؤلم ولا شائن. قال صلّى الله عليه وسلم: «عَلِّق السَّوطَ حَيثُ يَرَاهُ أهلُ البيت» . وعن أسماء بنت أبي بكر- رضى الله عنهما- قالت: (كنتُ رابعَ نسوة عند الزبير بن العوام، فإذا غضب على إحدانا، ضربها بعود المِشجب، حتى ينكسر) . والمشجب: أعواد مركبة يجعل عليها الثياب.\nفَإِنْ أَطَعْنَكُمْ يا معشر الأزواج، أو عقدن التوبة مما مضى، فَلا تَبْغُوا عَلَيْهِنَّ سَبِيلًا أي: لا تطلبوا عليهن طريقًا تجعلونه سبيلاً لإيذائهن، بل اجعلوا ما كان منها من النشوز كأن لم يكن، (فإنَّ التَّائِبَ مِن الذَّنبِ كَمَن لا ذَنَب لَهُ) . وقال ابن عُيَيْنَة: أي لا تكلفوهن بحبكم. هـ. وقال الورتجبي: إذا حصل منهن صورة طاعة الرجال فلا يطلب منهن موافقة الطباع، فإن ذلك منازعة للقدر. قال تعالى: لاَ تَبْدِيلَ لِخَلْقِ اللَّهِ، وذكر حديث: «الأروَاح جُنودٌ مُجنَّدةٌ» .\nثم هدد الأزواج فقال: إِنَّ اللَّهَ كانَ عَلِيًّا كَبِيراً فاحذروه، فإنه أقدر عليكم منكم على من تحت ولايتكم، أو: إنه على علو شأنه، يتجاوز عن سيئاتكم، فأنتم أولى بالعفو عن نسائكم، أو: أنه يتعالى ويَكبر أن يظلم أحدًا أو يُنقص حقه.\nوسبب نزول الآية: أن سَعدَ بنَ الرَّبِيع، وَكَانَ مِن النُقَبَاءِ، لَطَمَ امرأته حَبيبَةَ بِنتَ زَيدِ بن أبي زُهَيرِ، وكانت نَشَزَتَ عَليهِ، فَانطَلَقَ أبُوُها معَهَا إلى رسول الله صلى الله عليه وسلم فقال: أفرَشتُهُ كَرِيمَتِي فَلَطَمَها، فقال- عليه الصلاة والسلام-:\nلِتقتَصَّ مِنهُ، فَانصرَفَت لتقتصّ منه، فقال صلّى الله عليه وسلم: ارجعوا، هذا جِبرِيلُ أتَاني وأنزَلَ الله هَذِهِ الآية: الرِّجالُ قَوَّامُونَ عَلَى النِّساءِ إلى آخرها، فقال عليه الصلاة والسلام: «أَرَدنَا أمرًا، وأرَادَ اللهُ أمرًا، والَّذِي أرَاد اللهُ خَير» فرفع القِصَاص. وقيل: نزلت في غيره ممن وقع له مثل هذا من النشوز. والله تعالى أعلم.\nالإشارة: الرجال الأقوياء قوامون على نفوسهم قهارون لها، بفضل القوة التي مكنهم الله منها، وبما أنفقوا عليها من المجاهدات والرياضات، فهم ينظرون إليها ويتهمونها في كل حين، فإن صلحت وأطاعت وانقادت لما يراد منها من أحكام العبودية، والقيام بوظائف الربوبية، عاملوها بالإكرام والإجمال، ورفعوا عنها الآداب والنكال، وإن نشزت وترفعت أدبوها وهجروها عن مواطن شهواتها ومضاجع نومها، وضربوها على قدر لجاجها وغفلتها.\nوكان الشيخ أبو يزيد يأخذ قبضة من القضبان ويذهب إلى خلوته، فكلما غفلت ضربها، حتى يكسرها كلها، وكان بعض أصحابنا يأخذ خشبة ويذهب إلى خلوته، فكلما غفل ضرب رأسه بها، حتى يأتي رأسُه كلَّه مفلول.\nوبلغني أن بعض أصحابنا كان يُدخل في لحمة رجله سكيناً كلما غفل قلبه، وهذا إغراق، وخير الأمور أوسطها.\nوبالله التوفيق.\nولمّا تكلم على حكم المرأة الطائعة والناشزة، تكلم على ما إذا أشكل الأمر، فقال:","quran_ar":"الرِّجالُ قَوَّامُونَ عَلَى النِّساءِ بِما فَضَّلَ اللَّهُ بَعْضَهُمْ عَلى بَعْضٍ وَبِما أَنْفَقُوا مِنْ أَمْوالِهِمْ فَالصَّالِحاتُ قانِتاتٌ حافِظاتٌ لِلْغَيْبِ بِما حَفِظَ اللَّهُ وَاللاَّتِي تَخافُونَ نُشُوزَهُنَّ فَعِظُوهُنَّ وَاهْجُرُوهُنَّ فِي الْمَضاجِعِ وَاضْرِبُوهُنَّ فَإِنْ أَطَعْنَكُمْ فَلا تَبْغُوا عَلَيْهِنَّ سَبِيلاً إِنَّ اللَّهَ كانَ عَلِيًّا كَبِيراً (34)","quran_en":"Men are charged with responsibility for women by what God has favoured some of them with over others and by what they spend from their wealth. Upright women are devout, guarding what is unseen as God guards. As for those whose defiance you fear, admonish them, leave them alone in their beds, and strike them. If they obey you, seek no means against them. God is exalted, great.","segments":[{"type":"language","title":"The subject al-ṣāliḥāt; causation; a variant reading","html":"<p>I say: “The upright women” (الصالحات) is a subject (مبتدأ), and what follows gives information about it (خبر). The prefixed fāʾ (الفاء) indicates consequence and derivation (السببية والتفريع), as though He were saying: Men are charged with responsibility for women; whoever is upright is to receive the good treatment she deserves, and whoever is recalcitrant is dealt with through admonition and the other measures indicated. Every mā (ما) in the verse is verbal-noun forming (مصدرية), except on Abū Jaʿfar’s reading “by what has safeguarded God [His rights]” (بما حفظ الله), where “God” is in the accusative; here mā is understood as a nominal relative (موصولة اسمية): through that which safeguards God [His rights], namely her obedience to God, by which He in turn protects her. It has also been analysed as a maṣdariyya. Consult al-Thaʿlabī.<\/p>\n","authorial":false,"text":"I say: “The upright women” (الصالحات) is a subject (مبتدأ), and what follows gives information about it (خبر). The prefixed fāʾ (الفاء) indicates consequence and derivation (السببية والتفريع), as though He were saying: Men are charged with responsibility for women; whoever is upright is to receive the good treatment she deserves, and whoever is recalcitrant is dealt with through admonition and the other measures indicated. Every mā (ما) in the verse is verbal-noun forming (مصدرية), except on Abū Jaʿfar’s reading “by what has safeguarded God [His rights]” (بما حفظ الله), where “God” is in the accusative; here mā is understood as a nominal relative (موصولة اسمية): through that which safeguards God [His rights], namely her obedience to God, by which He in turn protects her. It has also been analysed as a maṣdariyya. Consult al-Thaʿlabī."},{"type":"exposition","title":"Two grounds of household responsibility in Ibn ʿAjība’s account","html":"<p>The Real, majestic is His glory, says: “Men are charged with responsibility for women”—that is, they stand in relation to them as governors to those in their care with respect to discipline, expenditure and teaching. Ibn ʿAjība describes two grounds for this in the social and legal order he expounds: one bestowed (وهبي) and the other acquired (كسبي). Under the former he places what he considers the greater strength, planning and discernment of men in works and acts of worship, and the historically male offices of prophecy, leadership, public guardianship, certain acts of worship, testimony in judicial proceedings, military obligation, Friday prayer, agnatic inheritance, larger inheritance shares and unilateral pronouncement of divorce. The acquired ground is “what they spend of their wealth” in dowries (المهور), maintenance (النفقة), and clothing.<\/p>\n<p>A husband is therefore obliged to discharge his dealings with his wives justly (بالعدل). An upright woman (صالحة) devoted in obedience (قانتة)—to God and, within the account being given, to her husband—guards in his absence his household property, her chastity and his confidence. God has preserved her by giving her trustworthiness and constancy and by fixing religion firmly in her heart. Or, because she preserves God’s right, God protects her: as the Prophet is quoted as saying, “Be mindful of God and He will guard you.” <a class=\"noteref\" href=\"#/hadith/H035\">[H035]<\/a><\/p>\n","authorial":false,"text":"The Real, majestic is His glory, says: “Men are charged with responsibility for women”—that is, they stand in relation to them as governors to those in their care with respect to discipline, expenditure and teaching. Ibn ʿAjība describes two grounds for this in the social and legal order he expounds: one bestowed (وهبي) and the other acquired (كسبي). Under the former he places what he considers the greater strength, planning and discernment of men in works and acts of worship, and the historically male offices of prophecy, leadership, public guardianship, certain acts of worship, testimony in judicial proceedings, military obligation, Friday prayer, agnatic inheritance, larger inheritance shares and unilateral pronouncement of divorce. The acquired ground is “what they spend of their wealth” in dowries (المهور), maintenance (النفقة), and clothing. A husband is therefore obliged to discharge his dealings with his wives justly (بالعدل). An upright woman (صالحة) devoted in obedience (قانتة)—to God and, within the account being given, to her husband—guards in his absence his household property, her chastity and his confidence. God has preserved her by giving her trustworthiness and constancy and by fixing religion firmly in her heart. Or, because she preserves God’s right, God protects her: as the Prophet is quoted as saying, “Be mindful of God and He will guard you.” [H035]"},{"type":"legal","title":"Reciprocal good treatment, nushūz and its limits","html":"<p>Such a wife, Ibn ʿAjība says, is owed good conduct by her husband: he must respond to her beneficence with equally good care. He cites the report, “The best woman is she who pleases you when you look at her, obeys when you instruct her, and in your absence safeguards you in respect of her property and herself.” <a class=\"noteref\" href=\"#/hadith/H036\">[H036]<\/a> He says that the Prophet then recited this verse.<\/p>\n<p>Regarding women whose nushūz (نشوز)—rising up in disobedience against the husband—is feared or, on one interpretation, known, Ibn ʿAjība glosses the successive expressions as verbal admonition (وعظ), if that proves ineffective, withdrawal in the sleeping place (هجر في المضاجع), that is not sharing the cover or refraining from intercourse, and, if that too proves ineffective, finally <strong>ḍarb<\/strong> (ضرب), which he constrains as a blow that is neither painful nor disfiguring (غير مؤلم ولا شائن). He quotes, “Hang the whip where the household can see it” <a class=\"noteref\" href=\"#/hadith/H037\">[H037]<\/a>, and relates from Asmāʾ daughter of Abū Bakr an account of al-Zubayr striking one of his four wives with a wooden clothes-rack rod until it broke <a class=\"noteref\" href=\"#/hadith/R002\">[R002]<\/a>. [Translator’s note: the latter is a report about al-Zubayr, not a saying attributed here to the Prophet.]<\/p>\n<p>“If they obey you”—or resolve to repent of what has passed—“do not seek a way against them”: do not look for a pretext to injure them. Treat any preceding nushūz as though it had not occurred; “one who repents of sin is like one without sin” <a class=\"noteref\" href=\"#/hadith/H038\">[H038]<\/a>. Ibn ʿUyayna explained that husbands are not to demand that their wives love them. Al-Wartajbī likewise says that when the outward form of compliance occurs, requiring their inward natural dispositions to match one’s own would be a quarrel with providence. He cites “There is no changing God’s creation” [Q 30:30] and the report “Souls are like enlisted companies” <a class=\"noteref\" href=\"#/hadith/H039\">[H039]<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p>Finally, “God is exalted, great” is a warning to husbands: He has greater power over you than you possess over those in your care; despite His greatness He overlooks your faults, and you have still stronger reason to pardon theirs. Or the verse teaches that God is too great and exalted to wrong anyone or withhold anyone’s due.<\/p>\n","authorial":false,"text":"Such a wife, Ibn ʿAjība says, is owed good conduct by her husband: he must respond to her beneficence with equally good care. He cites the report, “The best woman is she who pleases you when you look at her, obeys when you instruct her, and in your absence safeguards you in respect of her property and herself.” [H036] He says that the Prophet then recited this verse. Regarding women whose nushūz (نشوز)—rising up in disobedience against the husband—is feared or, on one interpretation, known, Ibn ʿAjība glosses the successive expressions as verbal admonition (وعظ), if that proves ineffective, withdrawal in the sleeping place (هجر في المضاجع), that is not sharing the cover or refraining from intercourse, and, if that too proves ineffective, finally ḍarb (ضرب), which he constrains as a blow that is neither painful nor disfiguring (غير مؤلم ولا شائن). He quotes, “Hang the whip where the household can see it” [H037] , and relates from Asmāʾ daughter of Abū Bakr an account of al-Zubayr striking one of his four wives with a wooden clothes-rack rod until it broke [R002] . [Translator’s note: the latter is a report about al-Zubayr, not a saying attributed here to the Prophet.] “If they obey you”—or resolve to repent of what has passed—“do not seek a way against them”: do not look for a pretext to injure them. Treat any preceding nushūz as though it had not occurred; “one who repents of sin is like one without sin” [H038] . Ibn ʿUyayna explained that husbands are not to demand that their wives love them. Al-Wartajbī likewise says that when the outward form of compliance occurs, requiring their inward natural dispositions to match one’s own would be a quarrel with providence. He cites “There is no changing God’s creation” [Q 30:30] and the report “Souls are like enlisted companies” [H039] . Finally, “God is exalted, great” is a warning to husbands: He has greater power over you than you possess over those in your care; despite His greatness He overlooks your faults, and you have still stronger reason to pardon theirs. Or the verse teaches that God is too great and exalted to wrong anyone or withhold anyone’s due."},{"type":"reports","title":"A reported occasion of revelation—its evidential limits","html":"<p>As the occasion of revelation Ibn ʿAjība relates a story that Saʿd ibn al-Rabīʿ, one of the Medinan representatives, slapped his wife Ḥabība bint Zayd ibn Abī Zuhayr after a dispute. Her father accompanied her to the Messenger of God and said, “I gave him my precious daughter as wife, and he has slapped her!” The Prophet is reported first to have said that she should take equivalent retaliation; they departed, but he called them back and stated that Gabriel had brought the verse “Men are charged with responsibility for women.” He is then reported to have said, “We intended one thing and God intended another; what God intended is better,” whereupon the retaliation was abandoned. A second account attributes the occasion to another similar marital dispute. [Translator’s note: These transmitted narratives are <strong>not verified Prophetic hadith with secure isnāds<\/strong>; the core story is found in early tafsīr reports, several of which are disconnected.] <a class=\"noteref\" href=\"#/hadith/R003\">[R003]<\/a> God knows best.<\/p>\n","authorial":false,"text":"As the occasion of revelation Ibn ʿAjība relates a story that Saʿd ibn al-Rabīʿ, one of the Medinan representatives, slapped his wife Ḥabība bint Zayd ibn Abī Zuhayr after a dispute. Her father accompanied her to the Messenger of God and said, “I gave him my precious daughter as wife, and he has slapped her!” The Prophet is reported first to have said that she should take equivalent retaliation; they departed, but he called them back and stated that Gabriel had brought the verse “Men are charged with responsibility for women.” He is then reported to have said, “We intended one thing and God intended another; what God intended is better,” whereupon the retaliation was abandoned. A second account attributes the occasion to another similar marital dispute. [Translator’s note: These transmitted narratives are not verified Prophetic hadith with secure isnāds ; the core story is found in early tafsīr reports, several of which are disconnected.] [R003] God knows best."},{"type":"spiritual","title":"The strong wayfarer governs the lower self","html":"<p>Strong men, in the inward meaning, are those who stand firmly over their lower selves (نفوس), mastering them through the strength God enables and through the spiritual struggles (المجاهدات) and disciplines (الرياضات) they expend. They watch and question the self at every moment. If it becomes upright, obeying and yielding to the demands of servanthood (أحكام العبودية) and carrying out the obligations that proceed from Lordship (وظائف الربوبية), they treat it honourably and graciously and release it from severity and chastisement. If it rebels and exalts itself, they train it, remove it from the places of its desires and repose, and impose correction proportionate to its obstinacy and heedlessness.<\/p>\n<p>Ibn ʿAjība then transmits accounts of exceptional ascetic practices: Abū Yazīd, he says, would enter seclusion with a handful of twigs and strike himself when inattentive until he had broken them all; one of the author’s companions took a wooden implement into seclusion and struck his own head whenever his attention lapsed, until his whole head was battered. He also relates having been informed of a person who injured his own leg with a knife during distraction. <strong>Ibn ʿAjība himself rejects the excess<\/strong>, stating: “This is going to extremes (إغراق); the best of matters is their middle course.” [Translator’s note: these anecdotes are historical claims in the source, not practical instructions to the reader.] Through God comes success.<\/p>\n","authorial":true,"text":"Strong men, in the inward meaning, are those who stand firmly over their lower selves (نفوس), mastering them through the strength God enables and through the spiritual struggles (المجاهدات) and disciplines (الرياضات) they expend. They watch and question the self at every moment. If it becomes upright, obeying and yielding to the demands of servanthood (أحكام العبودية) and carrying out the obligations that proceed from Lordship (وظائف الربوبية), they treat it honourably and graciously and release it from severity and chastisement. If it rebels and exalts itself, they train it, remove it from the places of its desires and repose, and impose correction proportionate to its obstinacy and heedlessness. Ibn ʿAjība then transmits accounts of exceptional ascetic practices: Abū Yazīd, he says, would enter seclusion with a handful of twigs and strike himself when inattentive until he had broken them all; one of the author’s companions took a wooden implement into seclusion and struck his own head whenever his attention lapsed, until his whole head was battered. He also relates having been informed of a person who injured his own leg with a knife during distraction. Ibn ʿAjība himself rejects the excess , stating: “This is going to extremes (إغراق); the best of matters is their middle course.” [Translator’s note: these anecdotes are historical claims in the source, not practical instructions to the reader.] Through God comes success."},{"type":"transition","title":"","html":"<p>After addressing the compliant wife and one described as rebellious, he turns to cases in which the matter is disputed or unclear.<\/p>\n","authorial":false,"text":"After addressing the compliant wife and one described as rebellious, he turns to cases in which the matter is disputed or unclear."}],"cairo_notes":[],"english_characters":6927,"arabic_characters":4512},{"id":"q4_35","q":"4:35","s":4,"surah":"Al-Nisāʾ","title":"4:35 · Two arbiters, reconciliation and the dialogue of soul and spirit","source":"Cairo I:500–501","cairo":"I:500–501","source_page_first":500,"source_page_last":501,"ar":"وَإِنْ خِفْتُمْ شِقاقَ بَيْنِهِما فَابْعَثُوا حَكَماً مِنْ أَهْلِهِ وَحَكَماً مِنْ أَهْلِها إِنْ يُرِيدا إِصْلاحاً يُوَفِّقِ اللَّهُ بَيْنَهُما إِنَّ اللَّهَ كانَ عَلِيماً خَبِيراً (35)\nقلت: الشقاق: المخالفة والمساورة، وأضيف إلى الظرف توسعًا كقوله: بَلْ مَكْرُ اللَّيْلِ، والأصل: شقاقًا بينهما، والضمير في (يُريدا) للحكمين، وفي (بينهما) للزوجين، وقيل: للحكمين معًا، وقيل: للزوجين معًا.\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله: إِنْ خِفْتُمْ يا معشر الحكام، أي علمتم خلافَا بين الزوجين ومشارة، ولم تدروا الظالم من المظلوم، فَابْعَثُوا رجلين أمينين يحكمان بينهما، يكون أحدهما من أهله والآخر من أهلها، لأن الأقارب أعرف ببواطن الأحوال، وأطلب للإصلاح، فإن بعثهما الحاكمُ أجنبيين صح، وكذا إن أقامهما الزوجان.\nوما اتفق عليه الحكمان لزم الزوجين من خُلع أو طلاق أو وفاق. وقال أبو حنيفة: ليس لهما التطليق إلاَّ أن يجعل لهما، وإذا اختلفا لم يلزم شيء، ويستأنفان الحكم، قال ابن جُزَي: ومشهور مذهب مالك: أن الحاكم هو الذي يبعث\nالحكمين، وقيل: الزوجان، وجرت عادة القضاء أن يبعثوا امرأة أمينة ولا يبعثوا الحكمين، قال بعض العلماء: هو تغيير للقرآن والسنة الجارية. هـ.\nفإن بُعِث الحكمان، فإن أرادا إصلاحًا بين الزوجين، واتفقا عليه، وفق الله بينهما ببركة قصدهما، وفيه تنبيه على أن من أصلح نيته فيما يتحراه أصلح الله مبتغاه. إِنَّ اللَّهَ كانَ عَلِيماً خَبِيراً بما في الظواهر والبواطن، فيعلم كيف يرفع الشقاق ويوقع الوفاق.\nالإشارة: وإن خفتم، أيها الشيوخ، على صاحبكم منازعة النفس والروح فكانت النفس تجمح به إلى أسفل سافلين، بمتابعة هواها وعصيان مولاها، والروح تجنح به إلى أعلى عليين، بجهاد هواها ومشاهدة مولاها، فابعثوا له واردين قويين، إما شوق مقلق يرحل الروح إلى مولاها، أو خوف مزعج يزجر النفس عن هواها. فإن أراد الله بذلك العبد إصلاحًا لحاله أرسلهما معًا متفقين على تخليصه وارتفاعه، فيتقدم الخوف المزعج ويستدركه الشوق المقلق، فيلتحق بأهل التحقيق من أهل التوفيق، وما ذلك على الله بعزيز، وفي الحكم: «لا يُخرِج الشهوةَ من القلب إلا خوف مُزعج، أو شوقٍ مقلق» . والله تعالى أعلم.\nولما فرغ الحق جل جلاله من الكلام على حفظ الأموال، وحفظ الأنساب، وبعض حفظ الأبدان، شرع يتكلم على حفظ الأديان، وما يتعلق بذلك، فقال:","quran_ar":"وَإِنْ خِفْتُمْ شِقاقَ بَيْنِهِما فَابْعَثُوا حَكَماً مِنْ أَهْلِهِ وَحَكَماً مِنْ أَهْلِها إِنْ يُرِيدا إِصْلاحاً يُوَفِّقِ اللَّهُ بَيْنَهُما إِنَّ اللَّهَ كانَ عَلِيماً خَبِيراً (35)","quran_en":"If you fear a breach between the two of them, appoint an arbiter from his family and an arbiter from hers. If the two desire reconciliation, God will bring accord between them. God is knowing, fully aware.","segments":[{"type":"language","title":"Shiqāq, ellipsis and the referents of the pronouns","html":"<p>I say: <em>Shiqāq<\/em> (الشقاق) signifies opposition and conflict. Its annexation to an adverbial expression (إضافة إلى الظرف) is an extension of usage, like “the plotting of the night” (مكر الليل); the underlying wording would be “a rupture between them.” The dual pronoun in “if they both desire” (إن يريدا) refers to the two arbiters, whereas that in “He will reconcile between them” (بينهما) refers to husband and wife. Some apply both pronouns to the arbiters; others apply both to the spouses.<\/p>\n","authorial":false,"text":"I say: Shiqāq (الشقاق) signifies opposition and conflict. Its annexation to an adverbial expression (إضافة إلى الظرف) is an extension of usage, like “the plotting of the night” (مكر الليل); the underlying wording would be “a rupture between them.” The dual pronoun in “if they both desire” (إن يريدا) refers to the two arbiters, whereas that in “He will reconcile between them” (بينهما) refers to husband and wife. Some apply both pronouns to the arbiters; others apply both to the spouses."},{"type":"legal","title":"Appointment and powers of the two arbitrators","html":"<p>The Real, majestic is His glory, says: If you, the authorities (الحكام), perceive a dispute between the spouses and cannot tell oppressor from wronged party, appoint two trustworthy men to adjudicate between them, one from his family and one from hers. Relatives generally know their inner circumstances better and are more eager to effect reconciliation. It remains valid if a magistrate appoints two outsiders, and likewise if the spouses themselves appoint the pair.<\/p>\n<p>What the two arbiters agree upon, whether khulʿ (خلع), divorce (طلاق), or accommodation, binds the couple according to the view presented here. Abū Ḥanīfa, by contrast, holds that they cannot effect divorce unless authorized to do so. If the arbiters disagree, nothing binds the spouses and the adjudication must be undertaken afresh. Ibn Juzayy says: “The well-known position in Mālik’s school is that the magistrate appoints the two arbiters, though another opinion assigns that appointment to the spouses. In judicial practice a trustworthy woman is sometimes sent instead of the two arbiters; some scholars called this a change to the Qurʾān and the established sunna.” End of quotation.<\/p>\n","authorial":false,"text":"The Real, majestic is His glory, says: If you, the authorities (الحكام), perceive a dispute between the spouses and cannot tell oppressor from wronged party, appoint two trustworthy men to adjudicate between them, one from his family and one from hers. Relatives generally know their inner circumstances better and are more eager to effect reconciliation. It remains valid if a magistrate appoints two outsiders, and likewise if the spouses themselves appoint the pair. What the two arbiters agree upon, whether khulʿ (خلع), divorce (طلاق), or accommodation, binds the couple according to the view presented here. Abū Ḥanīfa, by contrast, holds that they cannot effect divorce unless authorized to do so. If the arbiters disagree, nothing binds the spouses and the adjudication must be undertaken afresh. Ibn Juzayy says: “The well-known position in Mālik’s school is that the magistrate appoints the two arbiters, though another opinion assigns that appointment to the spouses. In judicial practice a trustworthy woman is sometimes sent instead of the two arbiters; some scholars called this a change to the Qurʾān and the established sunna.” End of quotation."},{"type":"exposition","title":"Right intention and divine help in reconciliation","html":"<p>When the two arbiters are appointed and sincerely seek reconciliation, God brings harmony between the spouses through the blessing of their intention. This points to a general principle: whoever makes his intention sound in what he pursues, God makes the goal of his pursuit sound. “God is knowing, fully aware”: He knows outward states and inward thoughts and knows how to lift the estrangement and establish concord.<\/p>\n","authorial":false,"text":"When the two arbiters are appointed and sincerely seek reconciliation, God brings harmony between the spouses through the blessing of their intention. This points to a general principle: whoever makes his intention sound in what he pursues, God makes the goal of his pursuit sound. “God is knowing, fully aware”: He knows outward states and inward thoughts and knows how to lift the estrangement and establish concord."},{"type":"spiritual","title":"Fear and longing as arbiters between the lower soul and the spirit","html":"<p>If, spiritual teachers, you fear conflict between the lower soul (النفس) and the spirit (الروح) in a companion—when the soul races towards the lowest depths by following its passion and disobeying its Lord, while the spirit rises towards the highest heights by striving against passion and witnessing its Master—send powerful inward arrivals of two kinds (واردان): either a disquieting yearning (شوق مقلق) that draws the spirit toward its Master, or an unsettling fear (خوف مزعج) that restrains the lower soul from its passion.<\/p>\n<p>If God intends reform for the servant, He sends both in harmony for the servant’s liberation and ascent. Fear comes first, disturbing the soul and curbing it; longing then catches up, so that the servant joins the people of realization (أهل التحقيق) and divine enabling (أهل التوفيق). This is no great matter for God. As a saying in the <em>Ḥikam<\/em> has it: “Nothing drives desire from the heart except a fear that unsettles or a longing that disquiets.” <a class=\"noteref\" href=\"#/hadith/A035\">[A035]<\/a> God knows best.<\/p>\n","authorial":true,"text":"If, spiritual teachers, you fear conflict between the lower soul (النفس) and the spirit (الروح) in a companion—when the soul races towards the lowest depths by following its passion and disobeying its Lord, while the spirit rises towards the highest heights by striving against passion and witnessing its Master—send powerful inward arrivals of two kinds (واردان): either a disquieting yearning (شوق مقلق) that draws the spirit toward its Master, or an unsettling fear (خوف مزعج) that restrains the lower soul from its passion. If God intends reform for the servant, He sends both in harmony for the servant’s liberation and ascent. Fear comes first, disturbing the soul and curbing it; longing then catches up, so that the servant joins the people of realization (أهل التحقيق) and divine enabling (أهل التوفيق). This is no great matter for God. As a saying in the Ḥikam has it: “Nothing drives desire from the heart except a fear that unsettles or a longing that disquiets.” [A035] God knows best."},{"type":"transition","title":"","html":"<p>After discussing the preservation of wealth, lineage, and some matters of bodily security, the Real begins to discuss the preservation of religion and what pertains to it.<\/p>\n","authorial":false,"text":"After discussing the preservation of wealth, lineage, and some matters of bodily security, the Real begins to discuss the preservation of religion and what pertains to it."}],"cairo_notes":[],"english_characters":3238,"arabic_characters":1964},{"id":"q4_36","q":"4:36","s":4,"surah":"Al-Nisāʾ","title":"4:36 · The rights of parents, neighbours and dependants; service as an expression of tawḥīd","source":"Cairo I:501–503","cairo":"I:501–503","source_page_first":501,"source_page_last":503,"ar":"وَاعْبُدُوا اللَّهَ وَلا تُشْرِكُوا بِهِ شَيْئاً وَبِالْوالِدَيْنِ إِحْساناً وَبِذِي الْقُرْبى وَالْيَتامى وَالْمَساكِينِ وَالْجارِ ذِي الْقُرْبى وَالْجارِ الْجُنُبِ وَالصَّاحِبِ بِالْجَنْبِ وَابْنِ السَّبِيلِ وَما مَلَكَتْ أَيْمانُكُمْ إِنَّ اللَّهَ لا يُحِبُّ مَنْ كانَ مُخْتالاً فَخُوراً (36)\nقلت: الجُنب- بالضم-: البعيد، يقال فيه: جُنُب وأجنب وأجنبي، وسمي الجُنبُ جُنُبًا لأنه يبعد من المسجد وعن الصلاة وعن التلاوة، و (مختال) اسم فاعل، وأصله: مختيل، بالكسر، من الخُيَلاَء وهو التكبر.\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله: وَاعْبُدُوا اللَّهَ أي: وَحِّدُوه وأطيعوه وَلا تُشْرِكُوا بِهِ شَيْئاً جليًا أو خفيًا في اعتقادكم أو في عبادتكم، فمن قصد الحج والتجارة، فقد أشرك مع الله في عبادته، وأحسنوا بالوالدين إحسانًا حسنًا، وهو برهما والقيام بحقهما، وَبِذِي الْقُرْبى، أي: القرابة في النسب، أو الدين وَالْيَتامى لضعف حالهم، وَالْمَساكِينِ لقلة ما بيدهم، وقد شكى بعض الناس قساوة قلبه، فقال له عليه الصلاة والسلام: «إن أردتَ أن يلين قلبُك، فأطعم المسكين وامسح رأس اليتيم، وأطعمه» .\nوَالْجارِ ذِي الْقُرْبى الذي قّرُب جواره أو نسبه، وَالْجارِ الْجُنُبِ الذي بَعُد مكانه أو نسبه، وحَدَّد بعضُهم الجوار بأربعين دارًا من كل ناحية. وقال ابن عباس: الجار ذي القربى: الجار الذي بينك وبينه قرابة، والجار الجنب: الجار من قوم آخرين. هـ.\nقيل يا رسول الله: ما حق الجار على الجار قال: «إن دعاك أجبتَه، وإن أصابته فاقةٌ عُدتَ عليه، وإن استقرضك أقرضته، وإن أصابه خير هنأته، وإن مرض عدته، وإن أصابته مصيبة عزيته، وإن توفي شهدت جنازته، ولا تستعل عليه بالبنيان لتحجب عنه الريح إلا بإذنه، ولا تؤذه بقُتار قدرك- أي: بخارها- إلا أن تغرف له منها، وإن ابتعت فاكهة فأهد له منها، فإن لم تفعل فأدخلها سرًا، ولا يخرج ولدك منها بشيء فيغيظ ولده» ثم قال: «الجيران ثلاثة: فَجَارٌ له ثلاثة حقوق: حق الجوَارِ، وحق القرابة، وحق الإسلام، وجار له حقان: حق الجِوَار، وحق الإسلام، وجَارٌ له حق واحد: وهو المشرك من أهل الكتاب» .\nوَالصَّاحِبِ بِالْجَنْبِ، وهو الرفيق في أمر حسن، كتعلم وتصرف وصناعة وسفر، فإنه صحبك بجنبك، وعن عليّ- كرّم الله وجهه- (أنها الزّوجة) ، فيتأكد في حقها الإحسان زيادة على المعاشرة بالمعروف، قال بعضهم: أول قدم في الولاية كف الأذى وحمل الجفا، ومعيار ذلك حسن معاشرة الأهل والولد، وقال- عليه الصلاة السلام-: «خَيرُكُمْ خَيْرُكُمْ لِنسَائِه، وأنَا خَيْرُكُمْ لِنسَائِي» . وَابْنِ السَّبِيلِ، وهو الضيف أو المسافر لغرابته، وَما مَلَكَتْ أَيْمانُكُمْ، من الإماء والعبيد، وكَانَ آخر كلام النبي- عليه الصلاة والسلام-: «الصلاةَ الصَلاةَ وَمَا مَلَكتْ أيمانُكُمْ» .\nإِنَّ اللَّهَ لا يُحِبُّ مَنْ كانَ مُخْتالًا أي: متكبراً، يأنف عن أقاربه وجيرانه وأصحابه ولا يلتفت إليهم، فَخُوراً يتفاخر عليهم بماله وجاهه، وما خوله الله من نعمه، فهو جدير أن تسلب منه.\nالإشارة: واعبدوا الله، أي: بالقيام بوظائف العبودية، ومشاهدة عظمة الربوبية، وقال بعض الحكماء:\nالعبودية: ترك الاختيار، وملازمة الذل والافتقار. وقيل: العبودية أربعة أشياء: الوفاء بالعهود، والحفظ للحدود، والرضا بالموجود، والصبر على المفقود، وعنوان ذلك صفاء التوحيد، ولذلك قال: وَلا تُشْرِكُوا بِهِ شَيْئاً أي:\nلا تَرَوْا معه غيره، كما قال القائل:\nمُذْ عَرفْتُ الإِلَهَ لَمْ أَرَ غَيْراً ... وكَذا الْغَيْرُ عندنا ممْنُوعُ\nوقال آخر: (لو كلفت أن أرى غيره، لم أستطع، فإنه لا غير معه حتى أشهده) . فإذا حصلت العبودية في الظاهر، وتحقق التوحيد في الباطن، ظهرت عليه مكارم الأخلاق فيُحسن إلى الأقارب والأجانب، ويجود عليهم\nبالحس والمعنى، لأن الفتوة من شأن أهل التوحيد، ومن شيم أهل التجريد، كما هو معلوم من حالهم، نفعنا الله بذكرهم، وخرطنا في سلكهم. آمين.\nقال الورتجبي: «الوالدين» : مشايخ المعرفة. ثم نقل عن الجنيد، أنه قال: أمرني أبي أمرًا، وأمرني السري أمرًا.\nفقدمت أمر السري على أمر أبي، وكل ما وجدت فهو من بركاته. هـ. وذوو القربى هم الأخوة في الشيخ، واليتامى: من قصدهم من المتفقرة الجاهلة، والمساكين: ضعفاء اليقين من العامة، أمر الله تعالى أهل الخصوصية بالإحسان إليهم والبرور بهم، وهو أن يقرهم في طريقهم، ويحوشهم إلى ربهم.\nوالجار ذي القربى وهو جارك في السكنى وأخوك في النسبة، فيستحق عليك زيادة الإحسان. والجار الجنب: من جاورك من العوام فتنصحه وترشده، والصاحب بالجنب: من رافقك في أمر من العوام، كَسَفَرٍ وغيره، وابن السبيل:\nمن نزل بأهل الخصوصية من الأضياف، فلهم حق الضيافة عليهم حسًّا ومعنًى، وما ملكت أيمانكم: مالكم تصرف عليهم من الأهل والبنين والإماء والعبيد، فتقربونهم إلى حضرة الملك المجيد. ثم أمرهم بالتواضع والإقبال على الخاص والعام. فقال: إِنَّ اللَّهَ لا يُحِبُّ مَنْ كانَ مُخْتالًا فَخُوراً. والله تعالى أعلم.\nثم بيّن حال أضداد هؤلاء، فقال:","quran_ar":"وَاعْبُدُوا اللَّهَ وَلا تُشْرِكُوا بِهِ شَيْئاً وَبِالْوالِدَيْنِ إِحْساناً وَبِذِي الْقُرْبى وَالْيَتامى وَالْمَساكِينِ وَالْجارِ ذِي الْقُرْبى وَالْجارِ الْجُنُبِ وَالصَّاحِبِ بِالْجَنْبِ وَابْنِ السَّبِيلِ وَما مَلَكَتْ أَيْمانُكُمْ إِنَّ اللَّهَ لا يُحِبُّ مَنْ كانَ مُخْتالاً فَخُوراً (36)","quran_en":"Worship God and associate nothing with Him. Show good conduct to parents, relatives, orphans, the poor, the near neighbour, the distant neighbour, the companion at your side, the traveller, and those whom your right hands possess. God does not love anyone who is arrogant and boastful.","segments":[{"type":"language","title":"Junub and mukhtāl: lexical and morphological explanations","html":"<p>I say: <em>Junub<\/em> (الجُنُب), with ḍamma, means “distant.” One may say junub, ajnab, or ajnabī. A person in a state of major ritual impurity is called <em>junub<\/em>, it is said, because he remains away from the mosque, prayer, and Qurʾānic recitation. <em>Mukhtāl<\/em> (مختال, arrogant or vainglorious) is an active participle (اسم فاعل). Its underlying form is <em>mukhtayil<\/em> (مختيل) from <em>khuyalāʾ<\/em> (الخيلاء), pride.<\/p>\n","authorial":false,"text":"I say: Junub (الجُنُب), with ḍamma, means “distant.” One may say junub, ajnab, or ajnabī. A person in a state of major ritual impurity is called junub , it is said, because he remains away from the mosque, prayer, and Qurʾānic recitation. Mukhtāl (مختال, arrogant or vainglorious) is an active participle (اسم فاعل). Its underlying form is mukhtayil (مختيل) from khuyalāʾ (الخيلاء), pride."},{"type":"exposition","title":"The divine rights and kindness to parents and vulnerable people","html":"<p>The Real, majestic is His glory, says: “Worship God”: affirm His oneness (وحّدوه) and obey Him. “Do not associate anything with Him,” openly or covertly, in creed or worship. Ibn ʿAjība gives as an example a person intending pilgrimage and commerce simultaneously: on the rigorous interpretation of purity of intention presented here, such a person mixes another aim into his worship.<\/p>\n<p>“Show kindness to parents” means to honour them and fulfil their claims; to relatives, by blood or in religion; to orphans, on account of the weakness of their state; and to the poor, because of their scarce resources. It is reported that someone complained of a hard heart to the Prophet, who replied, “If you wish your heart to soften, feed the needy, stroke the head of an orphan, and feed him.” <a class=\"noteref\" href=\"#/hadith/H041\">[H041]<\/a> [Source note: The Aḥmad 7576 route located in the source register passes through an unnamed intermediary. This is a limitation of that chain, not a verdict on every parallel. The additional words “and feed him” in Ibn ʿAjība’s quotation are not present in that short Aḥmad wording.]<\/p>\n","authorial":false,"text":"The Real, majestic is His glory, says: “Worship God”: affirm His oneness (وحّدوه) and obey Him. “Do not associate anything with Him,” openly or covertly, in creed or worship. Ibn ʿAjība gives as an example a person intending pilgrimage and commerce simultaneously: on the rigorous interpretation of purity of intention presented here, such a person mixes another aim into his worship. “Show kindness to parents” means to honour them and fulfil their claims; to relatives, by blood or in religion; to orphans, on account of the weakness of their state; and to the poor, because of their scarce resources. It is reported that someone complained of a hard heart to the Prophet, who replied, “If you wish your heart to soften, feed the needy, stroke the head of an orphan, and feed him.” [H041] [Source note: The Aḥmad 7576 route located in the source register passes through an unnamed intermediary. This is a limitation of that chain, not a verdict on every parallel. The additional words “and feed him” in Ibn ʿAjība’s quotation are not present in that short Aḥmad wording.]"},{"type":"exposition","title":"Near and distant neighbours and the long catalogue of obligations","html":"<p>“The near neighbour” is one whose dwelling or kinship is close; “the distant neighbour” is one whose place or ancestry is further away. Some defined neighbourhood as extending forty houses in every direction. Ibn ʿAbbās said that the near neighbour is the one connected to you by kin, while the distant neighbour belongs to another group.<\/p>\n<p>When someone asked the Messenger of God about a neighbour’s rights, Ibn ʿAjība quotes an extended report: “If he invites you, accept his invitation; if he falls into need, provide for him; if he seeks a loan, lend to him; if good comes to him, congratulate him; if he becomes sick, visit him; if misfortune befalls him, console him; if he dies, attend his funeral. Do not raise your building above his so as to deprive him of the breeze without his permission. Do not distress him with the smell of your cooking unless you give him a portion. If you buy fruit, give some to him; otherwise bring it indoors discreetly, and do not let your child carry it out in sight of his child so as to provoke longing.” He then quotes its classification of neighbours according to three, two, or one right: the Muslim neighbour who is also a relative, the Muslim neighbour, and a polytheist neighbour from the People of the Book. <a class=\"noteref\" href=\"#/hadith/H042\">[H042]<\/a><\/p>\n<aside class=\"translator-note\" role=\"note\"><p><b>Translator’s source note.<\/b> The extended version is transmitted through a route whose narrators Suwayd b. ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz, ʿUthmān b. ʿAṭāʾ and his father are criticized by al-Bayhaqī; al-Dhahabī also identifies weaknesses in this route and a disconnection in a separate Muʿādh version. The shorter report in al-Bukhārī 6014 supports the general duty towards neighbours <a class=\"noteref\" href=\"#/hadith/H048\">[H048]<\/a>.<\/p><\/aside>\n","authorial":false,"text":"“The near neighbour” is one whose dwelling or kinship is close; “the distant neighbour” is one whose place or ancestry is further away. Some defined neighbourhood as extending forty houses in every direction. Ibn ʿAbbās said that the near neighbour is the one connected to you by kin, while the distant neighbour belongs to another group. When someone asked the Messenger of God about a neighbour’s rights, Ibn ʿAjība quotes an extended report: “If he invites you, accept his invitation; if he falls into need, provide for him; if he seeks a loan, lend to him; if good comes to him, congratulate him; if he becomes sick, visit him; if misfortune befalls him, console him; if he dies, attend his funeral. Do not raise your building above his so as to deprive him of the breeze without his permission. Do not distress him with the smell of your cooking unless you give him a portion. If you buy fruit, give some to him; otherwise bring it indoors discreetly, and do not let your child carry it out in sight of his child so as to provoke longing.” He then quotes its classification of neighbours according to three, two, or one right: the Muslim neighbour who is also a relative, the Muslim neighbour, and a polytheist neighbour from the People of the Book. [H042] Translator’s source note. The extended version is transmitted through a route whose narrators Suwayd b. ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz, ʿUthmān b. ʿAṭāʾ and his father are criticized by al-Bayhaqī; al-Dhahabī also identifies weaknesses in this route and a disconnection in a separate Muʿādh version. The shorter report in al-Bukhārī 6014 supports the general duty towards neighbours [H048] ."},{"type":"exposition","title":"Companions, spouses, travellers, and historically dependent persons","html":"<p>“The companion at one’s side” (الصاحب بالجنب) is anyone accompanying one in a good undertaking: learning, work, a craft, or a journey. ʿAlī interpreted this expression as “the wife”; then the call to excellence towards her is especially emphatic, in addition to the command to associate with her honourably. Someone said: “The first step upon the path of sainthood is to withhold harm and to bear harshness; the test is one’s good conduct towards spouse and children.” The Prophet is quoted as saying, “The best of you are those best to their wives, and I am the best of you to my wives.” <a class=\"noteref\" href=\"#/hadith/H043\">[H043]<\/a><\/p>\n<p>“The wayfarer” is a traveller or guest who is a stranger in the place. “Those whom your right hands possess” denotes female and male slaves within historical slaveholding institutions. Ibn ʿAjība adds a last injunction attributed to the Prophet: “Prayer, prayer—and those whom your right hands possess!” <a class=\"noteref\" href=\"#/hadith/H044\">[H044]<\/a> [Source note: Ibn Mājah 2698 transmits the last-words frame and the injunction, with “prayer” stated once. Source attribution does not by itself establish the strength of this particular chain or its separate parallels.] “God does not love the arrogant, boastful person”: one who disdains relatives, neighbours and companions and pays no attention to them, and boasts over them about wealth, office, and God’s gifts, exposes himself to the withdrawal of those gifts.<\/p>\n","authorial":false,"text":"“The companion at one’s side” (الصاحب بالجنب) is anyone accompanying one in a good undertaking: learning, work, a craft, or a journey. ʿAlī interpreted this expression as “the wife”; then the call to excellence towards her is especially emphatic, in addition to the command to associate with her honourably. Someone said: “The first step upon the path of sainthood is to withhold harm and to bear harshness; the test is one’s good conduct towards spouse and children.” The Prophet is quoted as saying, “The best of you are those best to their wives, and I am the best of you to my wives.” [H043] “The wayfarer” is a traveller or guest who is a stranger in the place. “Those whom your right hands possess” denotes female and male slaves within historical slaveholding institutions. Ibn ʿAjība adds a last injunction attributed to the Prophet: “Prayer, prayer—and those whom your right hands possess!” [H044] [Source note: Ibn Mājah 2698 transmits the last-words frame and the injunction, with “prayer” stated once. Source attribution does not by itself establish the strength of this particular chain or its separate parallels.] “God does not love the arrogant, boastful person”: one who disdains relatives, neighbours and companions and pays no attention to them, and boasts over them about wealth, office, and God’s gifts, exposes himself to the withdrawal of those gifts."},{"type":"spiritual","title":"Servanthood as poverty, trust and purified intention","html":"<p>“Worship God” means to carry out the functions of servanthood (وظائف العبودية) while witnessing the majesty of Lordship (عظمة الربوبية). Some sages said: “Servanthood is relinquishing personal choice, adhering to humility, and acknowledging need.” Another definition gives four components: fulfilment of covenants, preservation of limits, contentment with what is present, and patience concerning what is absent. The mark that gathers these is the purity of the realization of oneness (صفاء التوحيد). Thus “associate nothing with Him” means: do not see any other alongside Him. As a poet said:<\/p>\n<p>“Since I knew the Godhead, I have seen no other; / Indeed, for us any ‘other’ is ruled out.”<\/p>\n<p>Someone else said: “If I were commanded to behold something other than Him, I could not, for there is no other alongside Him for me to witness.” Once servanthood is established outwardly and oneness inwardly realized, noble character appears in the servant. He does good to relatives and strangers and gives to them both materially and spiritually, because generosity of soul (الفتوة) is a characteristic of the people of oneness and of spiritual detachment (التجريد). May God benefit us through their remembrance and number us among them. Amen.<\/p>\n","authorial":true,"text":"“Worship God” means to carry out the functions of servanthood (وظائف العبودية) while witnessing the majesty of Lordship (عظمة الربوبية). Some sages said: “Servanthood is relinquishing personal choice, adhering to humility, and acknowledging need.” Another definition gives four components: fulfilment of covenants, preservation of limits, contentment with what is present, and patience concerning what is absent. The mark that gathers these is the purity of the realization of oneness (صفاء التوحيد). Thus “associate nothing with Him” means: do not see any other alongside Him. As a poet said: “Since I knew the Godhead, I have seen no other; / Indeed, for us any ‘other’ is ruled out.” Someone else said: “If I were commanded to behold something other than Him, I could not, for there is no other alongside Him for me to witness.” Once servanthood is established outwardly and oneness inwardly realized, noble character appears in the servant. He does good to relatives and strangers and gives to them both materially and spiritually, because generosity of soul (الفتوة) is a characteristic of the people of oneness and of spiritual detachment (التجريد). May God benefit us through their remembrance and number us among them. Amen."},{"type":"spiritual","title":"Parents and neighbours as metaphors for relationships of spiritual training","html":"<p>Al-Wartajbī explains “parents” as the teachers of divine knowledge. He transmits from al-Junayd: “My father gave me one command and al-Sarrī gave me another. I put al-Sarrī’s command before my father’s, and everything I have found has come through its blessing.” Kin are one’s brothers in the spiritual guide. Orphans are those untrained, needy seekers who approach them; the poor are the people of weak certainty among the generality. God has commanded those granted special realization (أهل الخصوصية) to treat them with beneficence: to confirm them in their path and gather them toward their Lord.<\/p>\n<p>The near neighbour is someone who lives next to you and is joined to you in spiritual affiliation; an additional share of good conduct is owed him. The distant neighbour is an ordinary person living nearby: advise and guide him. The companion at one’s side is an ordinary person accompanying you in travel or another affair. The wayfarer is a guest staying with the people of special realization; such a guest has rights of hospitality, material and spiritual. “Those whom your right hands possess” signifies, in the author’s social order, the family members, children, and persons under one’s care whom one can affect; bring them near to the Presence of the glorious King. Finally, humility and openness towards both the special and the general is demanded by “God does not love the arrogant, boastful.” God knows best.<\/p>\n","authorial":true,"text":"Al-Wartajbī explains “parents” as the teachers of divine knowledge. He transmits from al-Junayd: “My father gave me one command and al-Sarrī gave me another. I put al-Sarrī’s command before my father’s, and everything I have found has come through its blessing.” Kin are one’s brothers in the spiritual guide. Orphans are those untrained, needy seekers who approach them; the poor are the people of weak certainty among the generality. God has commanded those granted special realization (أهل الخصوصية) to treat them with beneficence: to confirm them in their path and gather them toward their Lord. The near neighbour is someone who lives next to you and is joined to you in spiritual affiliation; an additional share of good conduct is owed him. The distant neighbour is an ordinary person living nearby: advise and guide him. The companion at one’s side is an ordinary person accompanying you in travel or another affair. The wayfarer is a guest staying with the people of special realization; such a guest has rights of hospitality, material and spiritual. “Those whom your right hands possess” signifies, in the author’s social order, the family members, children, and persons under one’s care whom one can affect; bring them near to the Presence of the glorious King. Finally, humility and openness towards both the special and the general is demanded by “God does not love the arrogant, boastful.” God knows best."},{"type":"transition","title":"","html":"<p>He then describes those who behave in the contrary way.<\/p>\n","authorial":false,"text":"He then describes those who behave in the contrary way."}],"cairo_notes":[],"english_characters":7178,"arabic_characters":4143},{"id":"q4_cont_037","q":"4:37–39","s":4,"surah":"Al-Nisāʾ","title":"4:37–39 · Miserliness, ostentation, and sincerity","source":"Cairo I:503–504","cairo":"I:503–504","source_page_first":503,"source_page_last":504,"ar":"الَّذِينَ يَبْخَلُونَ وَيَأْمُرُونَ النَّاسَ بِالْبُخْلِ وَيَكْتُمُونَ ما آتاهُمُ اللَّهُ مِنْ فَضْلِهِ وَأَعْتَدْنا لِلْكافِرِينَ عَذاباً مُهِيناً (37) وَالَّذِينَ يُنْفِقُونَ أَمْوالَهُمْ رِئاءَ النَّاسِ وَلا يُؤْمِنُونَ بِاللَّهِ وَلا بِالْيَوْمِ الْآخِرِ وَمَنْ يَكُنِ الشَّيْطانُ لَهُ قَرِيناً فَساءَ قَرِيناً (38) وَماذا عَلَيْهِمْ لَوْ آمَنُوا بِاللَّهِ وَالْيَوْمِ الْآخِرِ وَأَنْفَقُوا مِمَّا رَزَقَهُمُ اللَّهُ وَكانَ اللَّهُ بِهِمْ عَلِيماً (39)\nقلت: (الذين) بدل مِن: «مَن كان» ، أو منصوب على الذم، أو مرفوع عليه، أي: هم. أو مبتدأ حُذف خبره، أي: نعذبهم عذابًا مُهينًا، أو أحقاء بكل ملامة، و (الذين ينفقون) : عطف على الأولى، أو مبتدأ حُذف خبره، أي:\nالشيطان قرينهم. والبخل فيه لغتان: البُخل والبَخَل بحركتين.\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله: الَّذِينَ يَبْخَلُونَ بأموالهم على أقاربهم وجيرانهم، وَيَأْمُرُونَ النَّاسَ بِالْبُخْلِ وَيَكْتُمُونَ ما آتاهُمُ اللَّهُ من الغنى، فيظهرون القلة والعيلة، أو يكتمون العلم بصفة النبي صلى الله عليه وسلم، هم أحقاء بكل لوم وعتاب. وَأَعْتَدْنا لِلْكافِرِينَ منهم عَذاباً مُهِيناً يُهينهم ويُخزيهم، نزلت في اليهود، كانوا يقولون\nللأنصار: لا تنفقوا أموالكم، فإنا نخشى عليكم الفقر، وكتموا صفته- عليه الصلاة والسلام-. ووضع الظاهر موضع المضمر وكأنه يقول: وأعتدنا لهم، إشعارًا بأن مَن هذا شأنه فهو كافر بنعمة الله تعالى، ومن كفر بنعمة الله وأهانها استحق عذابًا مهينًا.\nوَالَّذِينَ يُنْفِقُونَ أَمْوالَهُمْ رِئاءَ النَّاسِ طلبا لمدحهم وخوفًا من ذمهم، وَلا يُؤْمِنُونَ بِاللَّهِ وَلا بِالْيَوْمِ الْآخِرِ، يتحرّون بإنفاقهم مراضيه، فالشيطان قرينهم لا يفارقهم، وَمَنْ يَكُنِ الشَّيْطانُ لَهُ قَرِيناً فَساءَ قَرِيناً، فلمّا كان الشيطان قرينهم زين لهم التهالك على الأموال والرياء في الأعمال، وإنما أشرك أهل الرياء مع البخلاء في الوعيد من حيث إنهما طَرَفَا تفريط وإفراط، وهما سواء في القبح واستجلاب الذم.\nوَماذا عَلَيْهِمْ لَوْ آمَنُوا بِاللَّهِ وَالْيَوْمِ الْآخِرِ وَأَنْفَقُوا مِمَّا رَزَقَهُمُ اللَّهُ أي: لا ملامة عليهم ولا تبعة تحيق بهم لو أخلصوا الإيمان وأنفقوا مما رزقهم الكريم المنان. قال البيضاوي: وفيه تنبيه على أن المدعو إلى أمر لا ضرر فيه ينبغي أن يُجيب إليه احتياطاً، فكيف إذا تضمن المنافع. وإنما قدم الإيمان هاهنا وأخره في الآية الأخرى: لأن القصد بذكره هنا التخصيص، وثَم التقليل. هـ. وَكانَ اللَّهُ بِهِمْ عَلِيماً لا يخفى عليه شيء من أمورهم وقصدهم.\nالإشارة: قال بعض الصوفية: (مِن أقبح كل قبيح صوفي شحيح) ، فالصوفية العارفون- رضى الله عنهم- الذين هم صفوة العباد متخلقون بأضداد ما وسم به الحق- تعالى- أهل العناد، فهم يجودون بأنفسهم وما خصهم الله به من العلوم اللدنية والأسرار القدسية، على من يستحقه من أهل التخلية والتحلية، ويأمرون الناس بالسخاء ومكارم الأخلاق، ويتحدثون بما منحهم الملك الخلاق، ويظهرون الغنى بالله والاكتفاء به عن كل ما سواه، وإذا بذلوا أموالهم أعطوها لله وبالله ومن الله وإلى الله وابتغاء مرضاة الله، هجم عليهم اليقين، وتمكنوا من شهود رب العالمين، فلا يقرب ساحتهم الشيطان، ولا يرون في الدارين إلا الملك الديان، تحبهم ملائكة الرحمن، ويحن إليهم الإنس والجان. نفعنا الله بمحبتهم، وخرطنا في مسلكهم، آمين.\nثم رغّب الحق- تعالى- فى الإنفاق مع الإخلاص، الذي هو عنوان الدين الخاص، فقال:","quran_ar":"الَّذِينَ يَبْخَلُونَ وَيَأْمُرُونَ النَّاسَ بِالْبُخْلِ وَيَكْتُمُونَ ما آتاهُمُ اللَّهُ مِنْ فَضْلِهِ وَأَعْتَدْنا لِلْكافِرِينَ عَذاباً مُهِيناً (37) وَالَّذِينَ يُنْفِقُونَ أَمْوالَهُمْ رِئاءَ النَّاسِ وَلا يُؤْمِنُونَ بِاللَّهِ وَلا بِالْيَوْمِ الْآخِرِ وَمَنْ يَكُنِ الشَّيْطانُ لَهُ قَرِيناً فَساءَ قَرِيناً (38) وَماذا عَلَيْهِمْ لَوْ آمَنُوا بِاللَّهِ وَالْيَوْمِ الْآخِرِ وَأَنْفَقُوا مِمَّا رَزَقَهُمُ اللَّهُ وَكانَ اللَّهُ بِهِمْ عَلِيماً (39)","quran_en":"“Those who are miserly, command people to be miserly, and conceal what God has given them of His bounty—and We have prepared for the unbelievers a humiliating punishment. And those who spend their wealth to be seen by people, while believing neither in God nor in the Last Day: whoever has Satan for a companion—what an evil companion! What harm would there have been to them had they believed in God and the Last Day and spent from what God had provided them? God has always known them well.”","segments":[{"type":"language","title":"Language and construction (qultu)","html":"<p>“Those who” (<em>alladhīna<\/em>) may be a substitute (<em>badal<\/em>) for “whoever was” (<em>man kāna<\/em>) in the preceding verse; alternatively it is in the accusative of censure (<em>naṣb ʿalā l-dhamm<\/em>) or nominative of censure, with an understood “they are.” It may also be an initial subject (<em>mubtadaʾ<\/em>) whose predicate has been omitted: “We shall punish them with a humiliating punishment,” or “they deserve every reproach.” “And those who spend” is coordinated with the first relative phrase, or begins a further sentence with an omitted predicate: “Satan is their companion.” The Arabic noun for miserliness is pronounced both <em>bukhl<\/em> and <em>bakhal<\/em> (with both vowels sounded in the latter form).<\/p>\n","authorial":false,"text":"“Those who” ( alladhīna ) may be a substitute ( badal ) for “whoever was” ( man kāna ) in the preceding verse; alternatively it is in the accusative of censure ( naṣb ʿalā l-dhamm ) or nominative of censure, with an understood “they are.” It may also be an initial subject ( mubtadaʾ ) whose predicate has been omitted: “We shall punish them with a humiliating punishment,” or “they deserve every reproach.” “And those who spend” is coordinated with the first relative phrase, or begins a further sentence with an omitted predicate: “Satan is their companion.” The Arabic noun for miserliness is pronounced both bukhl and bakhal (with both vowels sounded in the latter form)."},{"type":"exposition","title":"The outward interpretation (yaqūlu l-Ḥaqq)","html":"<p>God—majestic and exalted is He—speaks of those who withhold their wealth from their relatives and neighbours, tell others to be miserly, and conceal the abundance He has given them, making themselves appear poor and needy. Alternatively, they conceal their knowledge of the Prophet's description, God bless him and grant him peace. Such people deserve reproach on every account. “We have prepared for the unbelievers among them a humiliating punishment”: one that disgraces and abases them. The passage was revealed concerning Jews who, according to the account cited here, advised the Anṣār not to spend because they feared poverty for them, and concealed the Prophet's description. God places the explicit word “unbelievers” where a pronoun, “them,” might have stood; this indicates that one who acts in this way has denied God's blessing, and whoever denies and dishonours His blessing deserves a humiliating punishment.<\/p>\n<p>Those who spend “to be seen by people” seek their praise and fear their blame; they do not believe in God or the Last Day [so as to] seek His good pleasure through their expenditure. [Source and textual note: Al-Bayḍāwī and Abū al-Suʿūd on 4:38 explicitly connect this phrase to belief with <em>li-yataḥarraw<\/em>, “so as to seek”: genuine belief would direct expenditure toward God’s pleasure. Cairo I:504 instead prints <em>yataḥarrawna<\/em> without that connective. The bracketed words recover the relation supported by both earlier texts; “His” remains divine, and no assertion that ostentatious spending pleases God is intended. This is a disclosed explanatory supply, not a claim that Cairo prints the connective.] Satan is the companion who does not leave them. He beautifies for them a ruinous attachment to wealth and ostentation (<em>riyāʾ<\/em>) in their works. Ibn ʿAjība says that the ostentatious are joined to the miserly in the warning because they occupy the two opposite extremes of falling short and going too far (<em>tafrīṭ<\/em> and <em>ifrāṭ<\/em>): alike in their ugliness and in provoking blame.<\/p>\n<p>“What harm would there have been to them had they believed in God and the Last Day and spent from what God had provided them?” Had they made their faith sincere and given of what the Generous Bestower had provided, no blame or harmful consequence would attach to them. Al-Bayḍāwī remarks that a person invited to something entailing no harm ought prudently to accept it—how much more when it entails benefits. He also explains the different ordering of belief in this passage and another: here belief is mentioned first to mark specification, there later to give [the reason]. [Textual note: Cairo I:504 attributes <em>al-taqlīl<\/em>, “diminution,” to al-Bayḍāwī. His commentary on 4:39 instead reads <em>al-taʿlīl<\/em>, “giving the reason”: the preceding verse’s unbelief explains the ostentation. The bracketed rendering follows the checked earlier source; the different Cairo word is expressly recorded.] “God has always known them well”: nothing of their deeds or intentions escapes Him.<\/p>\n","authorial":false,"text":"God—majestic and exalted is He—speaks of those who withhold their wealth from their relatives and neighbours, tell others to be miserly, and conceal the abundance He has given them, making themselves appear poor and needy. Alternatively, they conceal their knowledge of the Prophet's description, God bless him and grant him peace. Such people deserve reproach on every account. “We have prepared for the unbelievers among them a humiliating punishment”: one that disgraces and abases them. The passage was revealed concerning Jews who, according to the account cited here, advised the Anṣār not to spend because they feared poverty for them, and concealed the Prophet's description. God places the explicit word “unbelievers” where a pronoun, “them,” might have stood; this indicates that one who acts in this way has denied God's blessing, and whoever denies and dishonours His blessing deserves a humiliating punishment. Those who spend “to be seen by people” seek their praise and fear their blame; they do not believe in God or the Last Day [so as to] seek His good pleasure through their expenditure. [Source and textual note: Al-Bayḍāwī and Abū al-Suʿūd on 4:38 explicitly connect this phrase to belief with li-yataḥarraw , “so as to seek”: genuine belief would direct expenditure toward God’s pleasure. Cairo I:504 instead prints yataḥarrawna without that connective. The bracketed words recover the relation supported by both earlier texts; “His” remains divine, and no assertion that ostentatious spending pleases God is intended. This is a disclosed explanatory supply, not a claim that Cairo prints the connective.] Satan is the companion who does not leave them. He beautifies for them a ruinous attachment to wealth and ostentation ( riyāʾ ) in their works. Ibn ʿAjība says that the ostentatious are joined to the miserly in the warning because they occupy the two opposite extremes of falling short and going too far ( tafrīṭ and ifrāṭ ): alike in their ugliness and in provoking blame. “What harm would there have been to them had they believed in God and the Last Day and spent from what God had provided them?” Had they made their faith sincere and given of what the Generous Bestower had provided, no blame or harmful consequence would attach to them. Al-Bayḍāwī remarks that a person invited to something entailing no harm ought prudently to accept it—how much more when it entails benefits. He also explains the different ordering of belief in this passage and another: here belief is mentioned first to mark specification, there later to give [the reason]. [Textual note: Cairo I:504 attributes al-taqlīl , “diminution,” to al-Bayḍāwī. His commentary on 4:39 instead reads al-taʿlīl , “giving the reason”: the preceding verse’s unbelief explains the ostentation. The bracketed rendering follows the checked earlier source; the different Cairo word is expressly recorded.] “God has always known them well”: nothing of their deeds or intentions escapes Him."},{"type":"spiritual","title":"(al-ishāra)","html":"<p>A Sufi once said, “Among the ugliest of all ugly things is a miserly Sufi.” The Sufis who know God, the elect of His servants, have acquired the qualities opposite to those with which God describes obstinate people. They give generously of themselves and of the divinely bestowed knowledges (<em>ʿulūm laduniyya<\/em>) and holy mysteries (<em>asrār qudsiyya<\/em>) entrusted to them, to those ready to receive them through emptying themselves of vices (<em>takhliya<\/em>) and adorning themselves with virtues (<em>taḥliya<\/em>). They summon people to liberality and noble character, speak of the gifts granted by the Creator, and show themselves rich through God and sufficient with Him against everything else.<\/p>\n<p>When they give their wealth, they give it for God, by God, from God, toward God, seeking His good pleasure. Certainty (<em>yaqīn<\/em>) has overtaken them, and they are established in witnessing (<em>shuhūd<\/em>) the Lord of the worlds. Thus Satan cannot approach their precinct; in either abode they behold only the Sovereign, the Judge. The angels of the Merciful love them; human beings and jinn long for them. May God profit us through love for them and enroll us in their way. Amen.<\/p>\n","authorial":true,"text":"A Sufi once said, “Among the ugliest of all ugly things is a miserly Sufi.” The Sufis who know God, the elect of His servants, have acquired the qualities opposite to those with which God describes obstinate people. They give generously of themselves and of the divinely bestowed knowledges ( ʿulūm laduniyya ) and holy mysteries ( asrār qudsiyya ) entrusted to them, to those ready to receive them through emptying themselves of vices ( takhliya ) and adorning themselves with virtues ( taḥliya ). They summon people to liberality and noble character, speak of the gifts granted by the Creator, and show themselves rich through God and sufficient with Him against everything else. When they give their wealth, they give it for God, by God, from God, toward God, seeking His good pleasure. Certainty ( yaqīn ) has overtaken them, and they are established in witnessing ( shuhūd ) the Lord of the worlds. Thus Satan cannot approach their precinct; in either abode they behold only the Sovereign, the Judge. The angels of the Merciful love them; human beings and jinn long for them. May God profit us through love for them and enroll us in their way. Amen."},{"type":"transition","title":"","html":"<p>God now encourages expenditure joined to sincerity, the distinguishing mark of the way of the elect.<\/p>\n","authorial":false,"text":"God now encourages expenditure joined to sincerity, the distinguishing mark of the way of the elect."}],"cairo_notes":[],"english_characters":4906,"arabic_characters":2929}];
